<h2 id="p5-2-tell-me-about-your-project-build-this-answer-now">2. "Tell me about your project" — build this answer now<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-2-tell-me-about-your-project-build-this-answer-now">#</a></h2>
<p>This is asked in <strong>every</strong> round-2 and it sets the tone. Prepare a 2-minute version:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>"I work on <code>[system]</code>, which does <code>[business purpose]</code>. It's a Spring Boot microservice estate — about <code>[N]</code> services — with Postgres per service, Kafka for async events, Redis for caching, deployed on Kubernetes via a Jenkins/GitHub Actions pipeline. My service, <code>[X]</code>, handles <code>[responsibility]</code>; it does roughly <code>[N]</code> requests per second at peak with a p99 of <code>[N]</code>ms. I own it end to end: design, implementation, tests, on-call. The most interesting problem I solved recently was <code>[specific problem]</code>, where <code>[what you did]</code> and it <code>[measurable result]</code>."</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then have ready:</p>
<ul>
<li>A hand-drawn architecture diagram you can reproduce.</li>
<li>Numbers: RPS, data volume, team size, latency, uptime.</li>
<li>Two hard technical problems with the reasoning and outcome.</li>
<li>What you'd change if you rebuilt it (shows judgement).</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-3-behavioural-questions-use-star-situation-task-action-resul">3. Behavioural questions (use STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-3-behavioural-questions-use-star-situation-task-action-resul">#</a></h2>
<p>Prepare <strong>one concrete story each</strong> for:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A production incident you resolved.</strong> → Emphasise: how you detected it, how you mitigated first vs root-caused after, the postmortem, the prevention you added.</li>
<li><strong>A performance problem you fixed.</strong> → "API p99 was 1.8s; profiling showed an N+1 loading 200 child rows per request; I added an <code>@EntityGraph</code> and a Caffeine cache for reference data, taking it to 120ms and cutting DB CPU by 40%."</li>
<li><strong>A technical disagreement with a colleague.</strong> → Show you argued with data, sought a decision mechanism (spike/POC/ADR), and committed to the outcome even when it wasn't yours.</li>
<li><strong>A time you missed a deadline / made a mistake.</strong> → Own it plainly, describe the recovery and what changed structurally. Never blame.</li>
<li><strong>Mentoring someone / leading a piece of work.</strong> → Expected at 5+ years.</li>
<li><strong>Pushing back on a requirement.</strong> → Shows you engage with the "why", not just the ticket.</li>
<li><strong>The hardest bug you've debugged.</strong> → Show method: reproduce → narrow → hypothesis → verify, not guess-and-check.</li>
<li><strong>Working with an unclear or changing requirement.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Something you learned recently outside work.</strong> → Name something real (virtual threads, a book, a side project).</li>
<li><strong>Why are you leaving / why this company?</strong> → Growth-oriented, never negative about the current employer.<ul>
<li><em>EPAM:</em> variety of domains, engineering culture, exposure to large-scale client systems, opportunity to work full stack.</li>
<li><em>Entain:</em> scale and latency challenges of live betting, working on systems where correctness genuinely matters, a strong product with real traffic.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Delivery tips:</strong> 60–90 seconds per story. Lead with the outcome if the story is long. Use "I" more than "we" when describing your contribution — but credit the team.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-4-questions-you-should-ask-asked-in-almost-every-round-not-a">4. Questions YOU should ask (asked in almost every round; not asking is a red flag)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-4-questions-you-should-ask-asked-in-almost-every-round-not-a">#</a></h2>
<p><strong>Technical:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>What does the service estate look like — how many services, and how do they communicate?</li>
<li>What's the biggest technical challenge the team is facing right now?</li>
<li>How do you handle releases and rollbacks? What's the deploy frequency?</li>
<li>What's the test strategy — where does the team sit on the pyramid?</li>
<li>How is on-call structured?</li>
<li>How much technical debt is there, and is there space to pay it down?</li>
<li>(EPAM) How is the project allocation process — what kind of client work would I start on?</li>
<li>(Entain) How do the trading/risk side and engineering collaborate?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Team/growth:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>What does success look like in the first 3 and 6 months?</li>
<li>How are technical decisions made — is there an ADR/architecture forum?</li>
<li>What's the code review culture like?</li>
<li>What's the career path from here?</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid asking only about salary/WFH in the technical round — save that for HR.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-5-rapid-fire-self-test-cover-the-answer-and-say-it-aloud">5. Rapid-fire self-test (cover the answer and say it aloud)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-5-rapid-fire-self-test-cover-the-answer-and-say-it-aloud">#</a></h2>
<p><strong>Java:</strong> HashMap internals · fail-fast vs fail-safe · <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> contract · String immutability · <code>volatile</code> vs <code>synchronized</code> · happens-before · ThreadPoolExecutor queue behaviour · CompletableFuture composition · Optional misuse · stream laziness · <code>map</code> vs <code>flatMap</code> · type erasure · PECS · GC generations · memory leak causes · virtual threads · records · sealed classes.</p>
<p><strong>Spring:</strong> how auto-configuration works · <code>@Transactional</code> self-invocation · propagation levels · bean scopes and the prototype-in-singleton trap · bean lifecycle · why constructor injection · AOP proxying · filter chain · <code>@ControllerAdvice</code> · profiles · Actuator.</p>
<p><strong>JPA:</strong> N+1 detection and fixes · LazyInitializationException · open-in-view · optimistic vs pessimistic locking · L1 vs L2 cache · <code>merge</code> semantics · cascade + orphanRemoval · Hikari sizing.</p>
<p><strong>SQL:</strong> joins · window functions · index leftmost prefix · what kills index usage · isolation levels · EXPLAIN.</p>
<p><strong>Microservices:</strong> saga choreography vs orchestration · outbox · circuit breaker states · idempotency key · Kafka delivery semantics · consumer rebalance · ordering guarantees · distributed tracing.</p>
<p><strong>Design:</strong> all five SOLID with a violation and fix · Strategy vs State vs Command · Decorator vs Proxy · Factory vs Abstract Factory vs Builder · patterns in Spring.</p>
<p><strong>System design:</strong> the 9-step framework · CAP/PACELC · sharding · consistent hashing · rate limiting algorithms · cache strategies + stampede · read replicas + lag · zero-downtime migration.</p>
<p><strong>Frontend (EPAM):</strong> event loop output ordering · closures · <code>this</code> · hooks rules · useEffect cleanup · keys · memoisation · Angular change detection OnPush · RxJS <code>switchMap</code> vs <code>concatMap</code> vs <code>exhaustMap</code> · unsubscribing · reactive forms · DI providedIn.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-6-a-realistic-study-plan">6. A realistic study plan<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-6-a-realistic-study-plan">#</a></h2>
<p><strong>If you have ~1 week:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Day 1–2: Part 1 (Java core, collections, streams) + write 5 coding problems by hand.</li>
<li>Day 3: Part 1 concurrency + Part 2 Spring core &amp; transactions.</li>
<li>Day 4: Part 2 JPA, SQL, Kafka, microservices.</li>
<li>Day 5: Part 3 SOLID + patterns (write the code for Strategy, Builder, Observer, Template Method) + 2 system designs out loud.</li>
<li>Day 6: Part 4 (EPAM only: React + Angular; Entain: skim) + DevOps.</li>
<li>Day 7: Behavioural stories written out, project pitch rehearsed, mock interview, re-read the rapid-fire list.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>If you have ~2–3 days:</strong> Prioritise in this order — (1) your own project explanation, (2) concurrency + collections, (3) <code>@Transactional</code> and JPA pitfalls, (4) SOLID + 6 patterns, (5) one system design (betting for Entain / e-commerce for EPAM), (6) behavioural stories.</p>
<p><strong>Daily habit:</strong> 2 coding problems + explain one concept out loud as if teaching it. If you can't explain it simply, you don't have it yet.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-7-interview-day-tactics">7. Interview-day tactics<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-7-interview-day-tactics">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Think aloud.</strong> For coding, state your approach and complexity <em>before</em> writing. Ask about constraints and edge cases (nulls, empty input, duplicates, overflow, concurrency).</li>
<li><strong>Never bluff.</strong> "I haven't used that in production, but my understanding is X — how do you use it here?" scores far better than a confident wrong answer. Interviewers at both companies will dig until you're at your limit; hitting your limit gracefully is expected and fine.</li>
<li><strong>Answer in layers.</strong> Give the direct answer in one sentence, then the detail, then the trade-off. Don't monologue for four minutes on question one.</li>
<li><strong>Anchor to experience.</strong> "In my current project we hit exactly this — we..." is worth more than textbook recall.</li>
<li><strong>Always mention trade-offs.</strong> At 5+ years they're testing judgement, not recall. Every design answer should end with "the cost of this is…".</li>
<li><strong>If you blank:</strong> say "let me think for a second", restate the question, and reason from first principles out loud. Silence is worse than visible reasoning.</li>
<li><strong>For system design:</strong> clarify before designing, and manage the clock — don't spend 15 minutes on the API contract.</li>
<li><strong>Have your setup ready:</strong> stable connection, quiet room, notepad and pen, a shared-editor-friendly browser, and your project diagram open.</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p5-8-final-checklist-before-the-call">8. Final checklist before the call<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p5-8-final-checklist-before-the-call">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Can I draw and explain my current system architecture in 3 minutes?</li>
<li>[ ] Do I have 3 real numbers (RPS, data size, latency) memorised?</li>
<li>[ ] Do I have 5 STAR stories ready?</li>
<li>[ ] Can I write <code>HashMap</code> internals, a thread-safe singleton, and a producer-consumer from memory?</li>
<li>[ ] Can I state all 5 SOLID principles with an example each?</li>
<li>[ ] Can I explain <code>@Transactional</code> proxying and its 4 gotchas?</li>
<li>[ ] Can I explain the N+1 problem and 3 fixes?</li>
<li>[ ] Can I do one full system design out loud in 30 minutes?</li>
<li>[ ] (EPAM) Can I write a React component with hooks and an Angular component with an RxJS pipeline?</li>
<li>[ ] (Entain) Can I explain idempotent bet placement and the wallet concurrency problem?</li>
<li>[ ] Do I have 4 questions to ask them?</li>
</ul>
<p>Good luck — you've cleared round 1 at both, which means the bar now is depth and judgement rather than whether you know Java. Lead with trade-offs and concrete experience and you'll be in good shape.</p></div></section>
<section class="panel" id="panel-6" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-6" hidden><header class="part-head"><span class="part-n">Part 06</span><h1>Collections, Generics &amp; Gotchas</h1><p class="kicker">The overlooked details <span class="dot">·</span> 62 questions</p></header><div class="prose">
<blockquote>
<p>This is the "you use it every day but never read the docs" section. At 5+ years, these are exactly the questions used to separate candidates, because everyone knows <code>ArrayList</code> exists and almost nobody knows what <code>subList</code> actually returns.
Applies to <strong>both companies</strong>. EPAM in particular is known for these.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr/>
<h1>SECTION A — Collections, Properly</h1>
<h2 id="p6-a1-internals-you-should-be-able-to-recite">A1. Internals you should be able to recite<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-a1-internals-you-should-be-able-to-recite">#</a></h2>
<p><strong>ArrayList</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Backed by <code>Object[] elementData</code>. Default capacity 10, but <strong>lazily allocated</strong> — <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()</code> starts with a shared empty array and allocates on first <code>add</code>.</li>
<li>Growth: <code>newCapacity = oldCapacity + (oldCapacity &gt;&gt; 1)</code> → 1.5×. Growth copies via <code>Arrays.copyOf</code> (an O(n) <code>System.arraycopy</code>).</li>
<li><code>add(E)</code> is <strong>amortised</strong> O(1), not O(1) — a single add can be O(n) when it resizes.</li>
<li><code>remove(int)</code> shifts everything left — O(n).</li>
<li><code>ensureCapacity(n)</code> up front avoids repeated resizes if you know the size.</li>
<li><code>trimToSize()</code> shrinks — ArrayList <strong>never shrinks automatically</strong>. A list that held 1M elements and now holds 3 still owns the 1M array. Real memory leak source.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>LinkedList</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Doubly linked, implements both <code>List</code> and <code>Deque</code>. Each node = 3 references + object header ≈ 40 bytes overhead per element vs 4–8 bytes in ArrayList.</li>
<li><code>get(int)</code> walks from the nearer end — still O(n).</li>
<li>Even for mid-list insertion, ArrayList usually wins in practice below ~10k elements because <code>System.arraycopy</code> is a fast intrinsic and LinkedList's pointer-chasing destroys CPU cache locality. <strong>Say this</strong> — it shows real understanding rather than a textbook answer.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>HashMap</strong> (recap + the extra bits)</p>
<ul>
<li><code>hash(key) = h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code> — XORs the high 16 bits into the low bits, because the index is <code>(n-1) &amp; hash</code> and only the low bits are used. Without it, keys differing only in high bits all collide.</li>
<li>Table length is always a power of two, so <code>&amp;</code> replaces the expensive <code>%</code>.</li>
<li>Treeify at 8 nodes <strong>and</strong> table length ≥ 64; otherwise it resizes instead. Untreeify at 6 (hysteresis prevents thrash).</li>
<li>Tree bins compare by hash, then by <code>Comparable</code> if the key implements it, then by an arbitrary tie-break.</li>
<li>Threshold = capacity × loadFactor. <code>new HashMap&lt;&gt;(100)</code> does <strong>not</strong> hold 100 without resize — it resizes at 75. If you know the size, use <code>new HashMap&lt;&gt;(expected / 0.75f + 1)</code>, or <code>HashMap.newHashMap(n)</code> in Java 19+.</li>
<li>Iteration order is unspecified and <strong>changes on resize</strong>. Never depend on it.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>LinkedHashMap</strong> — HashMap + a doubly-linked list through entries. <code>accessOrder=true</code> + <code>removeEldestEntry</code> = LRU cache in 3 lines.</p>
<p><strong>TreeMap</strong> — red-black tree, O(log n), sorted. Uses <code>compareTo</code>/<code>Comparator</code> for equality — <strong>not <code>equals</code></strong>. So a TreeSet can consider two objects duplicates that <code>equals</code> says are different, and vice versa. This is a legitimate documented inconsistency with the <code>Set</code> contract.</p>
<p><strong>ArrayDeque</strong> — circular resizable array. Faster than <code>LinkedList</code> as a queue and faster than <code>Stack</code> as a stack. <strong>Use it instead of <code>Stack</code> and <code>Vector</code></strong> (both legacy and synchronised). No nulls allowed — that's how it distinguishes empty slots.</p>
<p><strong>PriorityQueue</strong> — binary heap in an array. <code>peek</code>/<code>poll</code> O(1)/O(log n). <strong>Its <code>Iterator</code> and <code>toString</code> are NOT in sorted order</strong> — only polling is. Classic trap question.</p>
<p><strong>CopyOnWriteArrayList</strong> — every mutation copies the whole array. Reads are lock-free and never throw CME. Only for read-dominated, small collections (listener lists). Its iterator is a snapshot and does not support <code>remove</code>.</p>
<p><strong>EnumMap / EnumSet</strong> — backed by an array indexed by <code>ordinal()</code> / a bit vector. Dramatically faster and smaller than HashMap/HashSet for enum keys. Nearly free performance win almost nobody uses.</p>
<p><strong>WeakHashMap</strong> — keys held weakly, entries vanish when the key is GC'd. Use for canonicalising caches. Gotcha: if the <strong>value</strong> strongly references the key, the entry never dies.</p>
<p><strong>IdentityHashMap</strong> — uses <code>==</code> not <code>equals</code>. Used by serialisation frameworks for cycle detection.</p>
<h2 id="p6-a2-collections-pitfalls-high-yield-trick-questions">A2. Collections pitfalls (high-yield trick questions)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-a2-collections-pitfalls-high-yield-trick-questions">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q6-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P1</span><span class="qtext"><code>Arrays.asList</code> is not an ArrayList.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">l</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">asList</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"a"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"b"</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">l</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"c"</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">     </span><span class="c1">// UnsupportedOperationException</span>
<span class="n">l</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">set</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"z"</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// works! it's a fixed-size *view* over the array</span>
</code></pre></div><p>It returns <code>java.util.Arrays$ArrayList</code>, a fixed-size view backed by the original array — writing through <code>set</code> mutates the array. To get a real list: <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(Arrays.asList(...))</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P2</span><span class="qtext"><code>Arrays.asList</code> with a primitive array.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">3</span><span class="p">};</span>
<span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[]&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">l</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">asList</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// size 1! one element: the array itself</span>
<span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">3</span><span class="p">};</span>
<span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ok</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">asList</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="c1">// size 3</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Because generics can't hold primitives, <code>int[]</code> is treated as a single <code>T</code>. Fix: <code>Arrays.stream(a).boxed().toList()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P3</span><span class="qtext"><code>List.remove(int)</code> vs <code>List.remove(Object)</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ArrayList</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">of</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">10</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">20</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">30</span><span class="p">));</span>
<span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">remove</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">                       </span><span class="c1">// removes INDEX 1 → [10, 30]</span>
<span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">remove</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">valueOf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">));</span><span class="w">      </span><span class="c1">// removes the OBJECT 1 → no change</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Overload resolution picks the primitive <code>int</code> version. This has caused real production bugs.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P4</span><span class="qtext"><code>subList</code> is a view, not a copy.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">sub</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">subList</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">3</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">sub</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">clear</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w">          </span><span class="c1">// clears those elements from the ORIGINAL list</span>
<span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">99</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">         </span><span class="c1">// now sub.get(0) → ConcurrentModificationException</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Structurally modifying the backing list invalidates the sublist. To copy: <code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(list.subList(0,3))</code>. Also a memory leak: a small sublist keeps the whole backing list alive.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P5</span><span class="qtext"><code>List.of</code> / <code>Map.of</code> reject nulls and are truly immutable.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">of</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"a"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">            </span><span class="c1">// NullPointerException</span>
<span class="n">Map</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">of</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"k"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"v1"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"k"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"v2"</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// IllegalArgumentException: duplicate key</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Whereas <code>Arrays.asList</code> allows nulls, and <code>Collections.unmodifiableList</code> is only a <em>view</em> — mutating the backing list is visible through it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P6</span><span class="qtext"><code>Collectors.toMap</code> NPEs on null values.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="c1">// throws NullPointerException if any getName() is null</span>
<span class="n">emps</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toMap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getId</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getName</span><span class="p">));</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>groupingBy</code> handles nulls in values fine but NPEs on a <strong>null classifier key</strong>. <code>toMap</code> also throws <code>IllegalStateException</code> on duplicate keys unless you pass a merge function. Fix with a merge function, or <code>Collectors.toMap(k, v, (a,b)-&gt;a, HashMap::new)</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P7</span><span class="qtext">Mutating a key after insertion into a HashMap/HashSet.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Set</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Point</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">set</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">HashSet</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="n">Point</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">p</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Point</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">set</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">p</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">setX</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">99</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">set</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">contains</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">p</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// false — and you can never remove it either</span>
</code></pre></div><p>The entry now sits in a bucket that no longer matches its hash. Permanent orphan → memory leak. <strong>Keys must be effectively immutable.</strong></p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P8</span><span class="qtext">ConcurrentModificationException is not just about threads.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">isEmpty</span><span class="p">())</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">remove</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// CME</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Fixes: <code>iterator.remove()</code>, <code>list.removeIf(String::isEmpty)</code>, or iterate a copy.
Devious detail: removing the <strong>second-to-last</strong> element does <em>not</em> throw, because <code>hasNext()</code> returns false before <code>checkForComodification</code> runs. So the bug can hide in tests and appear in prod.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P9</span><span class="qtext"><code>HashSet</code> of arrays never works.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Arrays use identity <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code>. <code>set.contains(new int[]{1,2})</code> is always false. Use <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, a record, or wrap with <code>Arrays.hashCode</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P10</span><span class="qtext">"Comparison method violates its general contract!"</span></p><div class="a"><p>Thrown by TimSort when your <code>Comparator</code> is inconsistent (not transitive, or <code>compare(a,b)</code> and <code>compare(b,a)</code> disagree). Usual cause: <code>return (int)(a.getScore() - b.getScore())</code> — <strong>subtraction overflows</strong>. Always use <code>Integer.compare</code> / <code>Double.compare</code> / <code>Comparator.comparing</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P11</span><span class="qtext">Sorting a list containing nulls.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Comparator.comparing(Employee::getName)</code> NPEs on a null name. Use <code>Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder())</code> or <code>Comparator.comparing(Employee::getName, nullsLast(naturalOrder()))</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P12</span><span class="qtext"><code>Collections.synchronizedList</code> doesn't make iteration safe.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Each method is synchronised, but a compound operation (iterate, check-then-act) is not. You must manually synchronise on the list during iteration. And <code>synchronizedMap.get()</code> then <code>put()</code> is still a race — use <code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P13</span><span class="qtext"><code>ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfAbsent</code> deadlock.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Recursively updating the <em>same</em> map inside the lambda, or blocking inside it, can deadlock or corrupt the map — the bin is locked while your function runs. Keep the mapping function short, pure, and non-blocking.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P14</span><span class="qtext"><code>ConcurrentHashMap.size()</code> is approximate</span></p><div class="a"><p>under concurrent modification, and <code>isEmpty()</code>/<code>containsValue()</code> are weakly consistent. Don't build logic on them.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P15</span><span class="qtext"><code>HashMap</code> allows one null key, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> and <code>Hashtable</code> allow none.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Why? In a concurrent map, <code>get(k) == null</code> would be ambiguous — absent, or present-with-null-value — and you can't disambiguate atomically with <code>containsKey</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P16</span><span class="qtext"><code>Map.getOrDefault</code> vs <code>computeIfAbsent</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>getOrDefault</code> does <strong>not</strong> insert. <code>computeIfAbsent</code> does. Using <code>getOrDefault(k, new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()).add(x)</code> silently discards the addition — a very common bug.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">map</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">computeIfAbsent</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">key</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">k</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ArrayList</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">()).</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">value</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// correct multimap idiom</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P17</span><span class="qtext">Iterating a <code>HashMap</code> — use <code>entrySet</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>for (K k : map.keySet()) { map.get(k); }</code> does two hash lookups per entry. <code>entrySet()</code> does one.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P18</span><span class="qtext"><code>Iterator.remove()</code> must be preceded by <code>next()</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>and can only be called once per <code>next()</code>, else <code>IllegalStateException</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P19</span><span class="qtext"><code>Collection.toArray()</code> gotcha.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>list.toArray()</code> returns <code>Object[]</code>, which cannot be cast to <code>String[]</code>. Use <code>list.toArray(new String[0])</code> (the zero-length form is actually the fastest — JIT-optimised) or <code>list.toArray(String[]::new)</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">P20</span><span class="qtext"><code>Set</code> of mutable objects, and <code>equals</code> inherited from Object.</span></p><div class="a"><p>A class without <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> overridden will happily allow "duplicates" in a HashSet, because each instance is unique by identity. Records fix this for free.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h1>SECTION B — Generics, Properly</h1>
<h2 id="p6-b1-erasure-and-its-consequences">B1. Erasure and its consequences<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-b1-erasure-and-its-consequences">#</a></h2>
<p>At compile time the compiler checks types, then <strong>erases</strong> them: <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> becomes <code>List</code>, <code>T</code> becomes its leftmost bound (<code>Object</code> by default), and casts are inserted at call sites. The <code>.class</code> file has no runtime type parameters (except in the signature attribute, which reflection can read for <em>fields/methods</em>, not for instances).</p>
<p>Consequences you must be able to list:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>new T()</code> — illegal. Workaround: pass a <code>Class&lt;T&gt;</code> or a <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>
<li><code>new T[10]</code> — illegal. Workaround: <code>(T[]) new Object[10]</code> with <code>@SuppressWarnings</code>, or <code>Array.newInstance(clazz, n)</code>.</li>
<li><code>x instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code> — illegal (only <code>List&lt;?&gt;</code> is legal).</li>
<li><strong>Overload clash:</strong> <code>void f(List&lt;String&gt;)</code> and <code>void f(List&lt;Integer&gt;)</code> don't compile — same erasure.</li>
<li>Static fields are shared across all parameterisations — <code>Box&lt;String&gt;</code> and <code>Box&lt;Integer&gt;</code> share the same static.</li>
<li>Cannot catch or throw a generic exception type (<code>catch (T e)</code> illegal).</li>
<li><code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> illegal — no primitives; use wrappers or the primitive stream specialisations.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="p6-b2-wildcards-pecs-and-capture">B2. Wildcards, PECS, and capture<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-b2-wildcards-pecs-and-capture">#</a></h2>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Number</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">producer</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// can READ as Number; can't add anything (except null)</span>
<span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">super</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">consumer</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">    </span><span class="c1">// can ADD Integer/subtypes; reads come back as Object</span>
<span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">unknown</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">                   </span><span class="c1">// read as Object only, add nothing</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Producer Extends, Consumer Super.</strong> Why can't you add to <code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code>? Because it might actually be a <code>List&lt;Double&gt;</code>, and adding an <code>Integer</code> would corrupt it.</p>
<p><strong>Capture error you will hit in real code:</strong></p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">swap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">set</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="p">));</span><span class="w">      </span><span class="c1">// compile error: capture of ?</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>Fix with a private generic helper — the "wildcard capture helper" idiom:</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">swap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">swapHelper</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">swapHelper</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">l</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">l</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">set</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">l</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">set</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">l</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="p">)));</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p><strong>Invariance:</strong> <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> is <strong>not</strong> a <code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code>, even though <code>String</code> is an <code>Object</code>. If it were, you could put an <code>Integer</code> into a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code>. Arrays are covariant and therefore unsafe:</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Object</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">arr</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="n">arr</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">42</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">                 </span><span class="c1">// compiles → ArrayStoreException at runtime</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>This is exactly why you should prefer <code>List</code> over arrays.</p>
<h2 id="p6-b3-bounded-types">B3. Bounded types<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-b3-bounded-types">#</a></h2>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Comparable</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">max</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w">             </span><span class="c1">// upper bound</span>
<span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Number</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&amp;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Comparable</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">f</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w">            </span><span class="c1">// multiple bounds, class first</span>
<span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Comparable</span><span class="o">&lt;?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">super</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">max</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// the real signature in the JDK</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>That last one is worth understanding: <code>? super T</code> allows a type whose <code>compareTo</code> is defined on a supertype (e.g. <code>class Manager extends Employee</code> where only <code>Employee</code> implements <code>Comparable&lt;Employee&gt;</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Recursive generic bound</strong> (used by every builder pattern):</p>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">abstract</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">Builder</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Builder</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">protected</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">abstract</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">self</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">T</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">name</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">this</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">self</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// fluent API survives subclassing</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id="p6-b4-generic-methods-vs-generic-classes">B4. Generic methods vs generic classes<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-b4-generic-methods-vs-generic-classes">#</a></h2>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">static</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">listOf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="p">...</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">items</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// type param on the method</span>
<span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">Box</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">                        </span><span class="c1">// on the class</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>A static method <strong>cannot</strong> use the class's type parameter — it must declare its own. Type inference usually removes the need for explicit <code>Collections.&lt;String&gt;emptyList()</code>.</p>
<h2 id="p6-b5-heap-pollution-safevarargs">B5. Heap pollution &amp; <code>@SafeVarargs</code><a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-b5-heap-pollution-safevarargs">#</a></h2>
<div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@SafeVarargs</span>
<span class="kd">static</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">listOf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">T</span><span class="p">...</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">items</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">asList</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">items</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div>
<p>Generic varargs create a <code>T[]</code>, which erasure makes an <code>Object[]</code> — the compiler warns about possible heap pollution. <code>@SafeVarargs</code> (on static, final, or private methods) suppresses it, and is only correct if you never write into the array or expose it.</p>
<h2 id="p6-b6-generics-pitfall-questions">B6. Generics pitfall questions<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p6-b6-generics-pitfall-questions">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>"Why does this compile?"</strong>
<code>java
  List&lt;String&gt; l = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
  List raw = l;
  raw.add(42);                 // unchecked warning only
  String s = l.get(0);         // ClassCastException at runtime</code>
  Raw types disable all generic checking. Never use them — this is what erasure lets slip through.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>"Is <code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code> the same as <code>List&lt;?&gt;</code>?"</strong> No. You can add anything to <code>List&lt;Object&gt;</code>; you can add nothing to <code>List&lt;?&gt;</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><strong>"Can a generic class extend <code>Exception</code>?"</strong> No — <code>class MyEx&lt;T&gt; extends Exception</code> is illegal, because catch clauses need reifiable types.</li>
<li><strong>"What does <code>Class&lt;?&gt;</code> mean vs <code>Class</code>?"</strong> The wildcard form keeps generic checking on; the raw form disables it everywhere in the expression.</li>
</ul>
<hr/>
<h1>SECTION C — Language-Level Gotchas Nobody Revises</h1>
<section class="qa" id="q6-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G1</span><span class="qtext">Integer caching / autoboxing identity.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">127</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">127</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// true  (IntegerCache: -128..127)</span>
<span class="n">Integer</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">128</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">d</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">128</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">d</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// false (new objects)</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>valueOf</code> caches small values. <strong>Always compare wrappers with <code>.equals()</code>.</strong> Same applies to <code>Long</code>, <code>Short</code>, <code>Byte</code>, <code>Character</code> (0–127) and <code>Boolean</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G2</span><span class="qtext">Autoboxing NPE.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">m</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">HashMap</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">x</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">m</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"missing"</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">     </span><span class="c1">// NullPointerException on unboxing</span>
</code></pre></div><p>And the nastiest version — the ternary:</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">flag</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// NPE! the ternary unboxes to int because one branch is int</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G3</span><span class="qtext">Floating point.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.30000000000000004</code>. <code>Math.abs(a-b) &lt; EPSILON</code> for comparison, or <code>BigDecimal</code> for money. Also: <code>NaN != NaN</code> is true, but <code>Double.valueOf(NaN).equals(Double.valueOf(NaN))</code> is <strong>true</strong> — because <code>equals</code> uses <code>doubleToLongBits</code>. And <code>0.0 == -0.0</code> is true, but <code>Double.compare(0.0, -0.0)</code> is negative. These break <code>TreeSet</code> and <code>HashMap</code> in surprising ways.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G4</span><span class="qtext">Integer overflow.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1 == Integer.MIN_VALUE</code> — silently. Classic bug: <code>int mid = (low + high) / 2</code> in binary search overflows for large arrays. Use <code>low + (high - low) / 2</code>. Use <code>Math.addExact</code> to fail loudly.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G5</span><span class="qtext"><code>char</code> arithmetic and string concatenation order.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">out</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">println</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="sc">'a'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="sc">'b'</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">       </span><span class="c1">// 195 (int)</span>
<span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">out</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">println</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">""</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="sc">'a'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="sc">'b'</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// ab</span>
<span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">out</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">println</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"3"</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">     </span><span class="c1">// 33</span>
<span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">out</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">println</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"1"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">3</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">     </span><span class="c1">// 123</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G6</span><span class="qtext"><code>finally</code> overriding a return.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">f</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">try</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">finally</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">2</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// returns 2, swallows exceptions</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Also: modifying a local in <code>finally</code> after <code>return x</code> does <strong>not</strong> change the returned value (it was already evaluated onto the stack).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G7</span><span class="qtext">String switch and <code>null</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>switch (s)</code> NPEs if <code>s</code> is null. (Java 21 pattern-matching switch can handle <code>case null</code>.)</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G8</span><span class="qtext"><code>String.split</code> drops trailing empty strings.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="s">"a,b,,"</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">split</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">","</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="w">         </span><span class="c1">// 2, not 4</span>
<span class="s">"a,b,,"</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">split</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">","</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="w">     </span><span class="c1">// 4</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Also, <code>split</code> takes a <strong>regex</strong>: <code>"a.b".split(".")</code> returns an empty array — you need <code>split("\\.")</code> or <code>Pattern.quote(".")</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G9</span><span class="qtext"><code>substring</code> and <code>replace</code> return new strings.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>s.replace("a","b");</code> on its own does nothing — Strings are immutable, you must reassign.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G10</span><span class="qtext"><code>==</code> on Strings built at runtime.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"ja"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"va"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">                 </span><span class="c1">// compile-time constant → pooled → == "java" true</span>
<span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">getPrefix</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"va"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">          </span><span class="c1">// runtime → new object → == "java" false</span>
</code></pre></div></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G11</span><span class="qtext">Field hiding vs method overriding.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">A</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"A"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">who</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"A"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">B</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">A</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">name</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"B"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">who</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"B"</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="n">A</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ref</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">B</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="n">ref</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">name</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// "A" — fields are resolved STATICALLY by reference type</span>
<span class="n">ref</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">who</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// "B" — methods are resolved DYNAMICALLY</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Same for <code>static</code> methods — they are <strong>hidden</strong>, not overridden, and resolved by reference type.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G12</span><span class="qtext">Constructor calling an overridable method.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">Parent</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Parent</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">init</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">init</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">Child</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">extends</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Parent</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">v</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"set"</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Override</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">init</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">System</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">out</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">println</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">v</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// prints null!</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>The parent constructor runs before the child's field initialisers. <strong>Never call an overridable method from a constructor.</strong></p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G13</span><span class="qtext">Static initialisation order and circular class init.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Static blocks run in textual order at class load; a static field referencing a not-yet-initialised class can observe default values (<code>0</code>/<code>null</code>).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G14</span><span class="qtext"><code>equals</code> without <code>hashCode</code> (and vice versa).</span></p><div class="a"><p>Breaks every hash-based collection. Also, <code>equals(Object)</code> vs a self-typed <code>equals(MyClass)</code> — the latter is an <strong>overload, not an override</strong>, so collections silently use <code>Object.equals</code>. Always <code>@Override</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G15</span><span class="qtext">Varargs ambiguity and null.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">f</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// with f(String...) → passes null as the ARRAY, not as one element</span>
<span class="n">f</span><span class="p">((</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="c1">// one element</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Also <code>f(Object...)</code> vs <code>f(String...)</code> — the most specific wins, causing surprising dispatch.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G16</span><span class="qtext">Labelled break/continue</span></p><div class="a"><p>exist and are the clean way out of nested loops — worth knowing, occasionally asked.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G17</span><span class="qtext"><code>try-with-resources</code> closes in reverse order</span></p><div class="a"><p>, and if both body and <code>close()</code> throw, the close exception is <strong>suppressed</strong> (retrievable via <code>getSuppressed()</code>), not propagated.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G18</span><span class="qtext">Catching <code>Exception</code> catches <code>RuntimeException</code> too</span></p><div class="a"><p>— including <code>NullPointerException</code> from your own bug, which then gets logged as "downstream service error". Catch narrowly.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G19</span><span class="qtext"><code>Optional</code> gotchas.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Optional.of(null)</code> throws NPE — use <code>ofNullable</code>. <code>Optional</code> is not <code>Serializable</code>, so never use it as a field. <code>orElse(expensive())</code> always evaluates the argument even when a value is present — use <code>orElseGet</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-40"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G20</span><span class="qtext">Streams are single-use.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Reusing one throws <code>IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon or closed</code>. Also, <code>peek</code> may be <strong>skipped entirely</strong> if the pipeline can short-circuit without it — never use <code>peek</code> for side effects that must happen.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-41"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G21</span><span class="qtext"><code>Collectors.groupingBy</code> returns a <code>HashMap</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>— unordered. Use the three-arg form with <code>LinkedHashMap::new</code> or <code>TreeMap::new</code> if order matters.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-42"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G22</span><span class="qtext"><code>Stream.sorted()</code> on an infinite stream</span></p><div class="a"><p>hangs — stateful operations need the whole stream.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-43"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G23</span><span class="qtext"><code>Files.lines()</code> / <code>Stream</code> over a resource must be closed</span></p><div class="a"><p>— use try-with-resources, or you leak file handles.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-44"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G24</span><span class="qtext"><code>SimpleDateFormat</code> is not thread-safe.</span></p><div class="a"><p>A shared static instance in a multi-threaded service produces silently corrupted dates. Use <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> (immutable and thread-safe) and the whole <code>java.time</code> API. Related: <code>Date</code> is mutable and <code>Calendar</code> months are 0-indexed.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-45"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G25</span><span class="qtext"><code>Instant</code> vs <code>LocalDateTime</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>LocalDateTime</code> has <strong>no timezone</strong> — it's a wall-clock reading, useless for ordering across zones. Store timestamps as <code>Instant</code>/<code>timestamptz</code> (UTC) and convert at the edges. This causes real production bugs around DST.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-46"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G26</span><span class="qtext"><code>hashCode</code> is not a unique ID and is not stable across JVM runs.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Never persist it or use it as a database key.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-47"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G27</span><span class="qtext"><code>interrupt()</code> doesn't stop a thread</span></p><div class="a"><p>— it sets a flag / throws only at interruptible points. A tight CPU loop ignores it unless you check <code>Thread.interrupted()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-48"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G28</span><span class="qtext"><code>synchronized</code> on a <code>String</code> literal or an <code>Integer</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>locks a pooled/shared object that other unrelated code may also lock. Always lock a <code>private final Object lock = new Object();</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-49"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G29</span><span class="qtext">Non-atomic 64-bit reads.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Without <code>volatile</code>, reads/writes of <code>long</code>/<code>double</code> are not guaranteed atomic on all JVMs — you can observe a torn value.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-50"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">G30</span><span class="qtext"><code>System.out.println</code> in a hot path</span></p><div class="a"><p>is synchronised and blocking — it will serialise your entire thread pool. Real cause of production latency spikes.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h1>SECTION D — Spring/JPA gotchas in the same spirit</h1>
<section class="qa" id="q6-51"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S1</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Transactional</code> on a private/self-invoked/final method silently does nothing.</span></p><div class="a"><p>(Covered in Part 2, but it's the #1 real-world gotcha — worth saying unprompted.)</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-52"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S2</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Transactional</code> doesn't roll back on checked exceptions</span></p><div class="a"><p>by default.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-53"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S3</span><span class="qtext">Injecting a prototype bean into a singleton</span></p><div class="a"><p>gives you one instance forever.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-54"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S4</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Value</code> in a <code>@PostConstruct</code> vs constructor</span></p><div class="a"><p>— field injection isn't done yet in the constructor, so <code>@Value</code> fields are null there. Another reason for constructor injection.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-55"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S5</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Cacheable</code> and <code>@Async</code> have the same proxy limitation as <code>@Transactional</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>— self-invocation bypasses them, and <code>@Async</code> on a method returning <code>void</code> swallows exceptions unless you set an <code>AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-56"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S6</span><span class="qtext"><code>save()</code> on an entity with an assigned (non-generated) ID issues a SELECT first</span></p><div class="a"><p>, because Spring Data can't tell insert from update. Implement <code>Persistable.isNew()</code> to avoid it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-57"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S7</span><span class="qtext"><code>saveAll()</code> is not batched by default.</span></p><div class="a"><p>You need <code>hibernate.jdbc.batch_size</code>, <code>order_inserts</code>, <code>order_updates</code>, and a non-IDENTITY generator (IDENTITY disables JDBC batching entirely — use SEQUENCE).</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-58"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S8</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Transactional(readOnly = true)</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>isn't just a hint — it skips dirty checking and lets Hibernate avoid snapshotting, and routes to a read replica if configured. Free performance on read paths.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-59"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S9</span><span class="qtext">Calling an external HTTP API inside a <code>@Transactional</code> method</span></p><div class="a"><p>holds a DB connection for the duration of the network call. Under load this exhausts the Hikari pool. Move IO outside the transaction.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-60"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S10</span><span class="qtext"><code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> on JPA entities using the generated ID</span></p><div class="a"><p>breaks before the entity is persisted (ID is null) and breaks in <code>Set</code> collections. Use a business key or a UUID assigned at construction.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-61"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S11</span><span class="qtext">Bidirectional relationships and <code>toString</code>/Lombok <code>@Data</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>→ infinite recursion / StackOverflowError, and <code>@Data</code> on an entity generates <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> over <em>all</em> fields including lazy collections, triggering loads. Never put <code>@Data</code> on an entity.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q6-62"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">S12</span><span class="qtext"><code>@OneToMany</code> without <code>mappedBy</code></span></p><div class="a"><p>creates a join table you didn't ask for.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h1>SECTION E — 20 rapid trick questions to self-test</h1>
<p>Cover the answers and say them out loud.</p>
<ol>
<li>What does <code>Arrays.asList(1,2,3).add(4)</code> do? → <em>UnsupportedOperationException.</em></li>
<li><code>list.remove(1)</code> on <code>List&lt;Integer&gt;</code> — index or value? → <em>Index.</em></li>
<li><code>Integer a=128, b=128; a==b</code>? → <em>false.</em></li>
<li>Can a <code>HashMap</code> have a null key? A <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>? → <em>Yes; no.</em></li>
<li>When does HashMap treeify? → <em>Bin ≥8 AND table ≥64.</em></li>
<li>Is <code>PriorityQueue</code>'s iterator sorted? → <em>No.</em></li>
<li>What's wrong with <code>(int)(a.score - b.score)</code> in a comparator? → <em>Overflow → contract violation.</em></li>
<li>Is <code>subList</code> a copy? → <em>No, a view.</em></li>
<li><code>"a,b,,".split(",").length</code>? → <em>2.</em></li>
<li>What does <code>Optional.of(null)</code> do? → <em>NPE.</em></li>
<li>Can you catch <code>T extends Exception</code>? → <em>No.</em></li>
<li>Why can't you add to <code>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;</code>? → <em>It might be a <code>List&lt;Double&gt;</code>.</em></li>
<li>What's the erasure of <code>&lt;T extends Comparable&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>? → <em><code>Comparable</code>.</em></li>
<li><code>finally { return 2; }</code> after <code>return 1</code> → <em>returns 2, swallows exceptions.</em></li>
<li>Field access on <code>A ref = new B()</code> — which <code>name</code>? → <em>A's; fields are static-bound.</em></li>
<li>Why is <code>SimpleDateFormat</code> dangerous? → <em>Not thread-safe.</em></li>
<li>What does <code>peek</code> guarantee? → <em>Nothing; it may be elided.</em></li>
<li>Does <code>@Transactional</code> work on <code>this.method()</code>? → <em>No, proxy bypass.</em></li>
<li>What does <code>hibernate</code> do with <code>saveAll</code> and IDENTITY ids? → <em>No JDBC batching.</em></li>
<li>Difference between <code>Instant</code> and <code>LocalDateTime</code>? → <em>LocalDateTime has no timezone.</em></li>
</ol>
<hr/>
<p><strong>Why this section matters in the interview:</strong> when you volunteer one of these unprompted — "I'd use <code>computeIfAbsent</code> here rather than <code>getOrDefault</code>, because the latter doesn't insert" — it signals production experience more strongly than any amount of textbook recall. Drop two or three naturally and the tone of the interview changes.</p></div></section>
<section class="panel" id="panel-7" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-7" hidden><header class="part-head"><span class="part-n">Part 07</span><h1>Spring Boot, Security &amp; Config</h1><p class="kicker">Annotations and runtime <span class="dot">·</span> 15 questions</p></header><div class="prose">
<blockquote>
<p>Part 2 covered Spring conceptually. This part is the <strong>reference layer</strong>: every annotation you'll be asked about, Spring Security end to end, and <code>application.yml</code> in detail.
Relevant to <strong>both companies</strong>. Entain will focus on security, config management and the runtime; EPAM will quiz annotations directly ("what does <code>@X</code> do, and what's the difference between <code>@X</code> and <code>@Y</code>?").</p>
</blockquote>
<hr/>
<h1>SECTION A — The Annotation Reference</h1>
<h2 id="p7-a1-bootstrapping-configuration">A1. Bootstrapping &amp; configuration<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a1-bootstrapping-configuration">#</a></h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Annotation</th>
<th>What it does</th>
<th>Worth knowing</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>@SpringBootApplication</code></td>
<td><code>@Configuration</code> + <code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code> + <code>@ComponentScan</code></td>
<td>Scans <strong>its own package and below</strong> — putting it in a sub-package is the #1 "my bean isn't found" bug</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Configuration</code></td>
<td>Marks a class as a source of bean definitions</td>
<td>CGLIB-proxied so inter-<code>@Bean</code> calls return the singleton; <code>proxyBeanMethods=false</code> ("lite mode") is faster but loses that</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Bean</code></td>
<td>Declares a bean from a method</td>
<td>Bean name = method name; supports <code>initMethod</code>/<code>destroyMethod</code>; params are auto-injected</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@ComponentScan</code></td>
<td>Where to scan</td>
<td><code>basePackages</code>, <code>excludeFilters</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Import</code></td>
<td>Pull in another config class</td>
<td>How auto-configuration wires itself</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code></td>
<td>Triggers auto-config</td>
<td><code>exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@PropertySource</code></td>
<td>Load an extra <code>.properties</code> file</td>
<td>Doesn't support YAML</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@EnableConfigurationProperties</code></td>
<td>Registers a <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> class</td>
<td>Not needed if the class is a <code>@Component</code> or you use <code>@ConfigurationPropertiesScan</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2 id="p7-a2-stereotypes-dependency-injection">A2. Stereotypes &amp; dependency injection<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a2-stereotypes-dependency-injection">#</a></h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Annotation</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>@Component</code></td>
<td>Generic bean</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Service</code></td>
<td>Semantic only — no extra behaviour</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Repository</code></td>
<td><strong>Adds exception translation</strong>: vendor <code>SQLException</code> → Spring <code>DataAccessException</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Controller</code></td>
<td>Returns view names</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@RestController</code></td>
<td><code>@Controller</code> + <code>@ResponseBody</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Autowired</code></td>
<td>Optional on a single constructor since 4.3; <code>required=false</code> for optional deps</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Qualifier("name")</code></td>
<td>Disambiguates by bean name</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Primary</code></td>
<td>Default choice when multiple candidates exist</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Value("${app.x:default}")</code></td>
<td>Injects a property; supports SpEL <code>#{...}</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Lazy</code></td>
<td>Defer creation until first use; also breaks a circular dependency</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Scope</code></td>
<td><code>singleton</code>/<code>prototype</code>/<code>request</code>/<code>session</code>; use <code>proxyMode</code> for a prototype in a singleton</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@DependsOn</code></td>
<td>Force initialisation order</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Order</code> / <code>Ordered</code></td>
<td>Ordering for lists of beans, filters, aspects — <strong>lower value = higher priority</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@PostConstruct</code> / <code>@PreDestroy</code></td>
<td>Lifecycle callbacks (now <code>jakarta.annotation</code> in Boot 3)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Profile("dev")</code></td>
<td>Bean only active in that profile; supports <code>!prod</code>, <code>dev &amp; !cloud</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Interview favourite:</strong> <em>"How do you inject a specific implementation when there are three?"</em> → <code>@Qualifier</code>, <code>@Primary</code>, matching the parameter name to the bean name, or <code>@Profile</code>. And to get <strong>all</strong> of them: <code>List&lt;PaymentProcessor&gt;</code> (ordered by <code>@Order</code>) or <code>Map&lt;String, PaymentProcessor&gt;</code> (bean name → bean).</p>
<h2 id="p7-a3-conditional-annotations-how-auto-config-decides">A3. Conditional annotations (how auto-config decides)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a3-conditional-annotations-how-auto-config-decides">#</a></h2>
<p><code>@ConditionalOnClass</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnMissingClass</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnBean</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> (this is why <em>your</em> bean always beats the auto-configured one), <code>@ConditionalOnProperty(name="app.feature.x", havingValue="true", matchIfMissing=false)</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnWebApplication</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnResource</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnExpression</code> (SpEL), plus custom <code>@Conditional(MyCondition.class)</code>.</p>
<h2 id="p7-a4-web-mvc">A4. Web / MVC<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a4-web-mvc">#</a></h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Annotation</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>@RequestMapping</code></td>
<td>Class- and method-level; <code>method</code>, <code>produces</code>, <code>consumes</code>, <code>params</code>, <code>headers</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@GetMapping</code> etc.</td>
<td>Shortcuts for GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@PathVariable</code></td>
<td><code>/users/{id}</code> → set <code>name</code> explicitly if you compile without <code>-parameters</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@RequestParam</code></td>
<td>Query/form param; <code>required</code>, <code>defaultValue</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@RequestBody</code></td>
<td>Deserialises the body via Jackson</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@ResponseBody</code></td>
<td>Serialises the return value</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@ResponseStatus</code></td>
<td>Sets the status on a method or an exception class</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@RequestHeader</code>, <code>@CookieValue</code></td>
<td>Header/cookie binding</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@ModelAttribute</code></td>
<td>Binds request params onto an object</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@CrossOrigin</code></td>
<td>Per-controller CORS</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@RestControllerAdvice</code></td>
<td>Global exception handling + <code>@InitBinder</code> + <code>@ModelAttribute</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@ExceptionHandler</code></td>
<td>Handles specific exception types</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Valid</code> / <code>@Validated</code></td>
<td>Triggers bean validation; <code>@Validated</code> also enables validation <strong>groups</strong> and method-level validation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — <code>@Valid</code> on a <code>@RequestBody</code> throws <code>MethodArgumentNotValidException</code>; on a <code>@RequestParam</code>/<code>@PathVariable</code> (with <code>@Validated</code> on the class) it throws <code>ConstraintViolationException</code>. Two different handlers needed.</p>
<h2 id="p7-a5-data-jpa">A5. Data / JPA<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a5-data-jpa">#</a></h2>
<p><code>@Entity</code>, <code>@Table(name=, indexes=)</code>, <code>@Id</code>, <code>@GeneratedValue(strategy=)</code>, <code>@Column(nullable=, length=, unique=)</code>, <code>@Embedded</code>/<code>@Embeddable</code>, <code>@Transient</code>, <code>@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)</code> (<strong>never <code>ORDINAL</code></strong> — reordering the enum silently corrupts data), <code>@Temporal</code> (legacy), <code>@Lob</code>, <code>@Version</code> (optimistic locking), <code>@OneToMany</code>/<code>@ManyToOne</code>/<code>@OneToOne</code>/<code>@ManyToMany</code>, <code>@JoinColumn</code>, <code>@JoinTable</code>, <code>@MappedSuperclass</code>, <code>@Inheritance</code>, <code>@NamedQuery</code>, <code>@EntityGraph</code>, <code>@Query</code>, <code>@Modifying</code> (required for update/delete <code>@Query</code>, usually with <code>clearAutomatically=true</code>), <code>@Lock</code>, <code>@CreatedDate</code>/<code>@LastModifiedDate</code> + <code>@EnableJpaAuditing</code>, <code>@EnableJpaRepositories</code>.</p>
<h2 id="p7-a6-transactions-async-scheduling-caching-aop">A6. Transactions, async, scheduling, caching, AOP<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a6-transactions-async-scheduling-caching-aop">#</a></h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Annotation</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>@Transactional</code></td>
<td><code>propagation</code>, <code>isolation</code>, <code>readOnly</code>, <code>timeout</code>, <code>rollbackFor</code>, <code>noRollbackFor</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@EnableTransactionManagement</code></td>
<td>Auto-configured by Boot</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Async</code></td>
<td>Runs on a <code>TaskExecutor</code>; needs <code>@EnableAsync</code>; return <code>void</code> or <code>CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Scheduled</code></td>
<td><code>fixedRate</code>, <code>fixedDelay</code>, <code>initialDelay</code>, <code>cron="0 0 2 * * *"</code>; needs <code>@EnableScheduling</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Cacheable</code> / <code>@CachePut</code> / <code>@CacheEvict</code></td>
<td>Needs <code>@EnableCaching</code>; <code>key</code>, <code>condition</code>, <code>unless</code>, <code>sync</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@Aspect</code> + <code>@Around</code>/<code>@Before</code>/<code>@AfterReturning</code>/<code>@AfterThrowing</code></td>
<td>Needs <code>@EnableAspectJAutoProxy</code> (auto in Boot)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>@EventListener</code> / <code>@TransactionalEventListener</code></td>
<td>The latter fires only <code>AFTER_COMMIT</code> — the correct way to publish after a DB write</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>The one gotcha that covers all of these:</strong> <code>@Transactional</code>, <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code> and <code>@PreAuthorize</code> are all <strong>proxy-based</strong>. Self-invocation (<code>this.method()</code>), private methods, and final classes/methods bypass them entirely and fail silently. Say this unprompted and it lands well.</p>
<p><strong><code>fixedRate</code> vs <code>fixedDelay</code>:</strong> <code>fixedRate</code> measures from the <em>start</em> of the previous run (can overlap or queue if a run is slow); <code>fixedDelay</code> measures from the <em>end</em>. In a clustered deployment <code>@Scheduled</code> runs on <strong>every instance</strong> — use ShedLock or leader election.</p>
<h2 id="p7-a7-testing">A7. Testing<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a7-testing">#</a></h2>
<p><code>@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT)</code>, <code>@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)</code>, <code>@DataJpaTest</code>, <code>@RestClientTest</code>, <code>@JsonTest</code>, <code>@MockBean</code> / <code>@SpyBean</code> (Boot 3.4+: <code>@MockitoBean</code>/<code>@MockitoSpyBean</code>), <code>@TestConfiguration</code>, <code>@ActiveProfiles("test")</code>, <code>@TestPropertySource</code>, <code>@DirtiesContext</code>, <code>@Sql</code>, <code>@Transactional</code> on a test (rolls back automatically), <code>@AutoConfigureMockMvc</code>, <code>@Testcontainers</code> + <code>@Container</code> + <code>@ServiceConnection</code>.</p>
<h2 id="p7-a8-lombok-used-everywhere-know-the-traps">A8. Lombok (used everywhere; know the traps)<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p7-a8-lombok-used-everywhere-know-the-traps">#</a></h2>
<p><code>@Getter</code>/<code>@Setter</code>, <code>@ToString</code>, <code>@EqualsAndHashCode</code>, <code>@Data</code> (all of the above + <code>@RequiredArgsConstructor</code>), <code>@Builder</code>, <code>@NoArgsConstructor</code>/<code>@AllArgsConstructor</code>/<code>@RequiredArgsConstructor</code>, <code>@Slf4j</code>, <code>@Value</code> (immutable — <strong>not</strong> Spring's <code>@Value</code>), <code>@SneakyThrows</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Traps:</strong> never <code>@Data</code> on a JPA entity (recursive <code>toString</code> on bidirectional relations, and <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> over lazy collections triggers loads); <code>@Builder</code> skips field initialisers unless you add <code>@Builder.Default</code>; <code>@EqualsAndHashCode</code> on entities should use a business key, not the generated ID.</p>
