<h2 id="p10-1-the-reported-process">1. The reported process<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-1-the-reported-process">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Recruiter screen</strong> — background, stack, notice period. Several candidates mention being sent a study plan.</li>
<li><strong>Online assessment</strong> (common at junior/mid, often skipped at senior) — proctored coding, frequently Codility, plus MCQs on Java, OOP, collections, SQL, DBMS and networking.</li>
<li><strong>Technical Round 1</strong> — 1 to 1.5 hours. Core Java theory plus live coding.</li>
<li><strong>Technical Round 2</strong> — microservices, distributed systems, design patterns, database design.</li>
<li><strong>Techno-managerial / Managerial</strong> — architecture deep dive plus team and delivery questions.</li>
<li><strong>HR</strong> — logistics and fit.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — several reports describe the third round as <strong>techno-managerial</strong>, not purely behavioural. One candidate was asked to draw their architecture live in draw.io, then grilled component by component. Prepare your MR expecting both the project/team questions in Part 09 <em>and</em> an architecture defence.</p>
<p>⚠️ Multiple reports note the coding happens in the interviewer's plain editor — <strong>no autocomplete, no auto-indent, and sometimes the run button disabled for you.</strong> Write Java by hand at least once before the day.</p>
