<h2 id="p10-3-core-java">3. Core Java<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-3-core-java">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q1</span><span class="qtext">Explain the exception hierarchy.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — <code>Throwable</code> splits into <code>Error</code> and <code>Exception</code>, and <code>Exception</code> splits again into checked and unchecked.</p><ul>
<li><code>Error</code> — <code>OutOfMemoryError</code>, <code>StackOverflowError</code>. JVM-level, don't catch them.</li>
<li><code>RuntimeException</code> and subclasses — <strong>unchecked</strong>, no compiler enforcement.</li>
<li>Everything else under <code>Exception</code> — <strong>checked</strong>, must be declared or handled.</li>
<li>In a Spring service: unchecked custom exceptions plus a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — <code>@Transactional</code> rolls back on <strong>unchecked only</strong> by default. A checked exception commits unless you set <code>rollbackFor</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q2</span><span class="qtext">Checked vs unchecked — which do you use in production?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — mostly unchecked with a global handler, because checked exceptions force every intermediate layer to catch or declare and buy nothing for it.</p><ul>
<li>Checked — genuinely recoverable, and the caller can actually act on it.</li>
<li>Unchecked — programming errors, and domain failures the caller can't fix.</li>
<li>Checked exceptions don't compose with lambdas or streams, which is a real cost in modern code.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q3</span><span class="qtext">Explain polymorphism and how dynamic dispatch works.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — two kinds — compile-time via overloading, runtime via overriding through the object's vtable.</p><ul>
<li>Overloading resolves from the <strong>declared</strong> type, at compile time.</li>
<li>Overriding resolves from the <strong>actual object</strong> type, at runtime — so <code>A ref = new B()</code> runs B's version.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — <strong>fields are not polymorphic</strong> — <code>ref.name</code> resolves statically to A's field. And <code>static</code> methods are <em>hidden</em>, not overridden, also resolved by reference type.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q4</span><span class="qtext">Method overloading vs overriding.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Overloading</th>
<th>Overriding</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Resolved</td>
<td>Compile time</td>
<td>Runtime</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Signature</td>
<td>Different parameter list</td>
<td>Identical</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Return type</td>
<td>Can't distinguish overloads alone</td>
<td>Same or covariant</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Visibility</td>
<td>Any</td>
<td>Can't be reduced</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exceptions</td>
<td>Any</td>
<td>No broader <strong>checked</strong> exceptions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Blocked on</td>
<td>—</td>
<td><code>private</code>, <code>static</code>, <code>final</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q5</span><span class="qtext">Explain the String pool and why String is immutable.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — literals are interned in a heap-based pool so identical literals share one object, and immutability is what makes that safe.</p><ul>
<li><code>new String("x")</code> deliberately creates a separate object; <code>.intern()</code> puts it back in the pool.</li>
<li>Immutability gives: safety for class names, file paths and DB URLs after validation; thread safety with no synchronisation; a cached hash code; pool sharing.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — the pool makes <code>==</code> <em>sometimes</em> appear to work. <code>"ja" + "va" == "java"</code> is true because it's a compile-time constant, but a runtime-built string is false. Always <code>.equals()</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q6</span><span class="qtext">The <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code> contract.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — equal objects must have equal hash codes; equal hash codes do <strong>not</strong> imply equality.</p><ul>
<li><code>equals</code> must be reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent, and <code>x.equals(null)</code> is false.</li>
<li>Both must stay consistent while the object sits in a hash-based collection.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — mutate a field used in <code>hashCode</code> after insertion into a <code>HashMap</code> and the entry is stranded in the wrong bucket — you can never retrieve <strong>or remove</strong> it. That's a genuine memory leak.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q7</span><span class="qtext">How do generics work under the hood?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — type erasure — the compiler checks types, then erases the parameters and inserts casts, so the bytecode carries no runtime type information.</p><p>Consequences you live with:</p><ul>
<li>No <code>new T()</code> and no <code>new T[]</code></li>
<li><code>instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code> is illegal; only <code>List&lt;?&gt;</code> is</li>
<li><code>f(List&lt;String&gt;)</code> and <code>f(List&lt;Integer&gt;)</code> clash — same erasure</li>
<li>Static fields are shared across all parameterisations</li>
<li>You can't catch or throw a generic exception type</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q8</span><span class="qtext">Abstract class vs interface.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — abstract classes carry state and a constructor with single inheritance; interfaces carry contracts with multiple inheritance.</p><ul>
<li>Since Java 8, interfaces can have <code>default</code> and <code>static</code> methods — so they carry <strong>behaviour</strong>, but never <strong>state</strong>. That's the real remaining distinction.</li>
<li>Abstract class when subclasses share fields and a lifecycle; interface for a capability contract.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q9</span><span class="qtext"><code>Runnable</code> vs <code>Callable</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — <code>Callable</code> returns a value and can throw checked exceptions; <code>Runnable</code> does neither.</p><ul>
<li><code>Callable</code> goes to <code>ExecutorService.submit()</code> and hands back a <code>Future</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — with <code>submit()</code> an exception is <strong>captured in the Future</strong> and only surfaces when you call <code>get()</code>. Ignore the Future and your failures are silent. <code>execute()</code> sends it to the thread's uncaught handler instead.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q10</span><span class="qtext">What is <code>transient</code>, and when have you used it?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — it excludes a field from Java's default serialization — on deserialize it returns as null or zero.</p><ul>
<li>Use for: cached derived values, open connections and streams, threads, loggers, secrets.</li>
<li><code>static</code> fields aren't serialized either — they belong to the class, not the instance.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — it applies to <code>Serializable</code> only. Jackson ignores it entirely — that's <code>@JsonIgnore</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q11</span><span class="qtext">How do you optimise memory for large datasets?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — stream rather than materialise, pick the right structure, and pre-size.</p><ul>
<li>Process in chunks with a cursor or keyset pagination instead of loading everything.</li>
<li>Primitive arrays or <code>IntStream</code> over boxed collections; <code>EnumMap</code>/<code>EnumSet</code> for enum keys.</li>
<li>Pre-size collections to avoid repeated resize-and-copy.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — the retention traps — an <code>ArrayList</code> never shrinks, <code>subList</code> keeps the whole backing list alive, and a <code>ThreadLocal</code> in a pooled thread leaks unless you <code>remove()</code>. Measure with a heap dump rather than guessing.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-4-collections">4. Collections<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-4-collections">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q12</span><span class="qtext">How does HashMap work internally?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — an array of buckets indexed by a spread hash, with collision chains that turn into trees.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Hash spreading:</strong> <code>h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code> pushes high bits down, because the index is <code>(n-1) &amp; hash</code> and only the low bits participate.</li>
<li>Table length is always a power of two, so the mask replaces a modulo.</li>
<li>Collisions chain in a linked list.</li>
<li><strong>Treeify at 8+ nodes in a bin AND table ≥ 64</strong> → red-black tree, worst case O(n) → O(log n). Untreeify at 6.</li>
<li>Default capacity 16, load factor 0.75 → resizes and rehashes at 12 entries.</li>
<li>One null key allowed, in bucket 0.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — keys must be effectively immutable, and iteration order is unspecified — it <strong>changes on resize</strong>. Never rely on it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q13</span><span class="qtext">HashMap vs Hashtable vs ConcurrentHashMap.</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Hashtable</th>
<th>HashMap</th>
<th>ConcurrentHashMap</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Thread safe</td>
<td>Yes, whole-map lock</td>
<td>No</td>
<td>Yes, per-bucket</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Locking</td>
<td><code>synchronized</code> every method</td>
<td>—</td>
<td>CAS for first node, <code>synchronized</code> on bin head</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Reads</td>
<td>Blocked</td>
<td>—</td>
<td><strong>Lock-free</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Nulls</td>
<td>None</td>
<td>1 null key, many null values</td>
<td>None</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Iterator</td>
<td>Fail-fast</td>
<td>Fail-fast</td>
<td>Weakly consistent</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — CHM forbids nulls because <code>get</code> returning null would be ambiguous — absent, or present-with-null — and you couldn't disambiguate atomically. Also <code>size()</code> is only an estimate under concurrent modification.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q14</span><span class="qtext">HashSet vs HashMap.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — <code>HashSet</code> <em>is</em> a <code>HashMap</code> — every element is a key, and all values point at one shared dummy object.</p><ul>
<li>Same hashing, same resize behaviour, same immutable-key requirement.</li>
<li>Set gives uniqueness and O(1) <code>contains</code>; Map gives key→value.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q15</span><span class="qtext">ArrayList vs LinkedList — which do you actually use?</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>ArrayList</th>
<th>LinkedList</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Structure</td>
<td>Dynamic array, grows 1.5×</td>
<td>Doubly linked</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>get(i)</code></td>
<td>O(1)</td>
<td>O(n)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Insert middle</td>
<td>O(n) shift</td>
<td>O(1) <strong>given the node</strong>, O(n) to find it</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Memory</td>
<td>Compact</td>
<td>~40 bytes overhead per element</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cache</td>
<td>Friendly</td>
<td>Pointer-chasing, cache-hostile</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — ArrayList wins almost always in practice, because <code>System.arraycopy</code> is a fast intrinsic while LinkedList destroys cache locality. For a queue or stack I use <code>ArrayDeque</code> — never <code>LinkedList</code>, never <code>Stack</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q16</span><span class="qtext">How do you choose a collection?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Indexed reads → <code>ArrayList</code></li>
<li>Queue or stack → <code>ArrayDeque</code></li>
<li>Uniqueness → <code>HashSet</code> · plus order → <code>LinkedHashSet</code> · sorted → <code>TreeSet</code></li>
<li>Key lookup → <code>HashMap</code> · insertion/access order → <code>LinkedHashMap</code> (that's the LRU cache) · sorted or range → <code>TreeMap</code></li>
<li>Concurrent → <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></li>
<li>Enum keys → <code>EnumMap</code>/<code>EnumSet</code> — array-backed, dramatically faster</li>
<li>Read-dominated listener list → <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code></li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q17</span><span class="qtext">Fail-fast vs fail-safe iterators.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — fail-fast tracks a <code>modCount</code> and throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code>; fail-safe iterates a snapshot or tolerates change.</p><ul>
<li>Fail-fast: <code>ArrayList</code>, <code>HashMap</code>. Fail-safe: <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code>, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — this isn't only about threads. Removing inside an enhanced for loop in a <strong>single</strong> thread throws it too. Use <code>Iterator.remove()</code> or <code>removeIf</code>.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-5-concurrency">5. Concurrency<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-5-concurrency">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q18</span><span class="qtext">Explain the Java Memory Model and happens-before.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — the JMM defines when one thread's write becomes visible to another, and happens-before is the guarantee that gives you that visibility.</p><p>Edges that create happens-before:</p><ul>
<li>Program order within a single thread</li>
<li>Unlocking a monitor → a later lock of the same monitor</li>
<li>A <code>volatile</code> write → a later read of that variable</li>
<li><code>Thread.start()</code> → everything in that thread</li>
<li>Everything in a thread → <code>join()</code> returning</li>
<li>Final field initialisation → the constructor completing</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — anything not on that list is unordered. Without an edge, the compiler and CPU may reorder and cache in registers, so a write may never become visible at all.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q19</span><span class="qtext">What does <code>volatile</code> guarantee — and what doesn't it?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — visibility and a reordering barrier. <strong>Not atomicity.</strong></p><ul>
<li>Guarantees: reads and writes reach main memory; no reordering across the access.</li>
<li>Does not: <code>count++</code> is read-modify-write and stays racy on a volatile field.</li>
<li>Use for: flags, safe publication of an immutable object, double-checked-locking singleton.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — in DCL, without <code>volatile</code> another thread can see a <strong>partially constructed</strong> object, because allocate/construct/assign can be reordered.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q20</span><span class="qtext">What does <code>synchronized</code> lock?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — an instance method locks <code>this</code>, a static method locks the <code>Class</code> object, a block locks whatever object you name.</p><ul>
<li>Reentrant. Gives mutual exclusion <strong>and</strong> visibility.</li>
<li>Prefer a <code>private final Object lock = new Object()</code> so outside code can't interfere.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — never synchronise on a String literal or a boxed <code>Integer</code> — they're pooled and shared, so unrelated code may be locking the same object.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q21</span><span class="qtext">Explain ExecutorService and thread pool sizing.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — the pool grows to core, then <strong>fills the queue</strong>, then grows to max, then rejects — and that ordering is what trips people up.</p><ul>
<li>Because the queue fills before max is reached, an <strong>unbounded</strong> queue means max is never used and you OOM instead of shedding load.</li>
<li>That's why I build <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> directly with a bounded queue and <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code> for back-pressure, rather than <code>Executors.newFixedThreadPool</code>.</li>
<li>Sizing: CPU-bound ≈ cores + 1. IO-bound ≈ cores × (1 + wait/service). Then measure.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q22</span><span class="qtext">What are atomic classes and how do they work?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a volatile value plus <code>compareAndSet</code> on the CPU's CAS instruction — optimistic, lock-free, retry on failure.</p><ul>
<li>Problems: the ABA problem (use <code>AtomicStampedReference</code>), and wasted spinning under heavy contention.</li>
<li>For hot counters use <code>LongAdder</code> — it stripes across cells and sums on read.</li>
</ul></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-6-java-8-11-17">6. Java 8 / 11 / 17<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-6-java-8-11-17">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q23</span><span class="qtext">What came in Java 8?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Lambdas and functional interfaces</li>
<li>Stream API</li>
<li><code>Optional</code></li>
<li><code>default</code> and <code>static</code> interface methods</li>
<li>Method references</li>
<li><code>java.time</code></li>
<li><code>CompletableFuture</code></li>
<li>PermGen → Metaspace</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q24</span><span class="qtext">What came in Java 11 and Java 17?</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Java 11 (LTS)</th>
<th>Java 17 (LTS)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Built-in HTTP Client</td>
<td><strong>Records</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>String.isBlank/lines/strip/repeat</code></td>
<td><strong>Sealed classes</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>Files.readString</code></td>
<td><strong>Text blocks</strong></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>var</code> in lambda parameters</td>
<td>Switch expressions, <code>instanceof</code> pattern matching</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Single-file source launch</td>
<td>Helpful NullPointerExceptions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — the reported question is often the <em>contrast</em> — "what's in 8 versus what's in 17". Be able to draw that line cleanly rather than listing features at random.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q25</span><span class="qtext">Which functional interfaces came in Java 8?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code>, <code>BiFunction</code></li>
<li><code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>BiConsumer</code></li>
<li><code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>BiPredicate</code></li>
<li><code>UnaryOperator</code>, <code>BinaryOperator</code></li>
<li>Primitive specialisations (<code>IntPredicate</code>, <code>ToIntFunction</code>) that avoid boxing</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — a functional interface has exactly one abstract method; <code>@FunctionalInterface</code> makes the compiler enforce it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q26</span><span class="qtext">What are lambdas used for?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — they let you pass behaviour as a value, so you parameterise an algorithm instead of duplicating it.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">sort</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">comparing</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Employee</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">salary</span><span class="p">));</span>
<span class="n">list</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">removeIf</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">isBlank</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="n">executor</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">submit</span><span class="p">(()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">process</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">job</span><span class="p">));</span>
</code></pre></div><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — they compile to an <code>invokedynamic</code> call site, not an anonymous inner class — so no extra class file per lambda.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q27</span><span class="qtext">What is the Stream API for?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — declarative processing of a sequence — you say what you want, not how to loop.</p><ul>
<li>Intermediate ops are <strong>lazy</strong>; nothing runs until a terminal op.</li>
<li>Elements push through all stages one at a time, which is what allows short-circuiting on <code>findFirst</code>.</li>
<li>Stateful ops (<code>sorted</code>, <code>distinct</code>) break that — they must buffer everything.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — parallel streams all share the <strong>common ForkJoinPool</strong>, so blocking IO inside one starves the whole JVM.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q28</span><span class="qtext">Why <code>Optional</code>, and how should it be used?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — it makes "may be absent" part of the type instead of a null you have to remember.</p><ul>
<li>Use as a <strong>return type</strong>. Not for fields (not <code>Serializable</code>), not for parameters, not instead of an empty collection.</li>
<li>Prefer <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>orElseGet</code>, <code>orElseThrow</code> over <code>isPresent</code>/<code>get</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — <code>orElse</code> always evaluates its argument even when a value is present; <code>orElseGet</code> only on absence. That matters when the fallback is expensive. And <code>Optional.of(null)</code> throws — use <code>ofNullable</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q29</span><span class="qtext">What are records, sealed classes and text blocks?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><strong>Record</strong> — immutable data carrier; generates constructor, accessors, <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code>. Compact constructor for validation. Ideal for DTOs and value objects.</li>
<li><strong>Sealed</strong> — restricts which classes may extend it, so a <code>switch</code> can be <strong>exhaustive</strong> without a <code>default</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Text block</strong> — triple-quoted multi-line string, removes escaping. Good for embedded JSON and SQL.</li>
</ul></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-7-spring-spring-boot">7. Spring &amp; Spring Boot<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-7-spring-spring-boot">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q30</span><span class="qtext">What is IoC and DI, and how does Spring Core work internally?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — the container creates and wires objects instead of your code calling <code>new</code>.</p><p>Startup order:</p><ol>
<li><code>BeanFactoryPostProcessor</code> phase — adjusts bean <em>definitions</em></li>
<li>Singletons instantiated, dependencies injected</li>
<li><code>BeanPostProcessor</code>s run — <strong>this is where AOP proxies are created</strong></li>
<li><code>@PostConstruct</code> → bean is live</li>
</ol><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — I use constructor injection — dependencies are final and mandatory, the object is never half-built, circular deps fail fast at startup, and it's testable without Spring.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q31</span><span class="qtext">What is AOP and what do you use it for?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — separating cross-cutting concerns from business logic, implemented in Spring by proxying.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Aspect</strong> the module · <strong>join point</strong> a method execution · <strong>pointcut</strong> the selecting expression · <strong>advice</strong> <code>@Before</code>/<code>@Around</code>/etc · <strong>weaving</strong> applying it.</li>
<li>Used for: timing and metrics, audit logging, retry.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — Spring weaves at runtime with JDK dynamic proxies or CGLIB — which is exactly <em>why</em> <code>@Transactional</code> has its self-invocation limitation.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q32</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Qualifier</code> vs <code>@Primary</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — <code>@Primary</code> is the default declared on the bean; <code>@Qualifier</code> is the explicit choice declared at the injection point, and it wins.</p><ul>
<li><code>@Primary</code> — one decision covering every injection point.</li>
<li><code>@Qualifier("name")</code> — overrides <code>@Primary</code> where you need the exception.</li>
<li>Parameter name matched against bean name is the fallback.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — injecting <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> gives you <strong>every</strong> implementation — that's how you wire a Strategy pattern. <code>Map&lt;String,T&gt;</code> gives you name→bean.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q33</span><span class="qtext">Explain the stereotype annotations.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — all four are <code>@Component</code>; only <code>@Repository</code> does anything extra.</p><ul>
<li><code>@Service</code>, <code>@Controller</code> — semantic markers, no added behaviour.</li>
<li><code>@Repository</code> — adds <strong>exception translation</strong>, converting vendor <code>SQLException</code>s into Spring's <code>DataAccessException</code> hierarchy.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q34</span><span class="qtext">How does transaction management work in Spring and Hibernate?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a proxy opens a transaction via the <code>PlatformTransactionManager</code>, binds the <code>EntityManager</code> to a ThreadLocal, and commits or rolls back around your method.</p><ul>
<li>Hibernate flushes the persistence context before commit, using dirty checking to generate updates.</li>
<li><strong>Propagation</strong> — <code>REQUIRED</code> joins or creates; <code>REQUIRES_NEW</code> suspends the outer one (use for audit records that must survive a rollback).</li>
<li><strong>Isolation</strong> — controls which anomalies are prevented: dirty read, non-repeatable read, phantom.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q35</span><span class="qtext">Why doesn't <code>@Transactional</code> work on private methods or self-invocation?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — it's proxy-based, and a private or self-invoked call never passes through the proxy.</p><ul>
<li>Spring wraps the bean in a JDK proxy or CGLIB subclass; the proxy starts the transaction before delegating.</li>
<li><code>this.method()</code> calls the raw object directly — the annotation <strong>silently does nothing</strong>, which is worse than failing loudly.</li>
<li>Fixes: move it to another bean, self-inject the proxy, or <code>AopContext.currentProxy()</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — the same limitation hits <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code> and <code>@PreAuthorize</code>. One sentence covers four questions — the highest-value single answer in this kit.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q36</span><span class="qtext">How do you handle exceptions globally in Spring Boot?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code> with <code>@ExceptionHandler</code> methods per exception type, returning a consistent error body.</p><ul>
<li>In Spring 6, return <code>ProblemDetail</code> (RFC 7807) rather than a hand-rolled DTO.</li>
<li>Include the trace ID from MDC so a user-reported error maps back to logs.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — <code>@Valid</code> on a <code>@RequestBody</code> throws <code>MethodArgumentNotValidException</code>, but on a <code>@RequestParam</code> or <code>@PathVariable</code> it throws <code>ConstraintViolationException</code>. Two different handlers needed.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q37</span><span class="qtext">What do Spring Boot starters do?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — curated, version-aligned dependency bundles that also trigger auto-configuration.</p><ul>
<li><code>starter-web</code> → Spring MVC + Jackson + embedded Tomcat</li>
<li><code>starter-data-jpa</code> → Hibernate + transaction manager</li>
<li>The real value is that the versions are tested together, so you're not resolving conflicts yourself.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q38</span><span class="qtext">Explain Spring Boot auto-configuration.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — <code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code> imports candidate config classes listed on the classpath, each guarded by <code>@Conditional</code> annotations.</p><ul>
<li>Registry file: <code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code> (was <code>spring.factories</code>).</li>
<li>Guards: <code>@ConditionalOnClass</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code>, <code>@ConditionalOnWebApplication</code>, and critically <strong><code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code></strong>.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> is <em>why</em> defining your own bean always wins. Run with <code>--debug</code> for the condition evaluation report.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q39</span><span class="qtext">Do you use Actuator? Which endpoints, and how do you secure them?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — yes — health wired to Kubernetes probes, metrics to Prometheus, and <code>/loggers</code> to change log level live during an incident.</p><ul>
<li><code>/health</code> with liveness and readiness groups · <code>/info</code> · <code>/metrics</code> and <code>/prometheus</code> via Micrometer · <code>/loggers</code> · <code>/threaddump</code> and <code>/heapdump</code> for diagnosis.</li>
<li>Securing: expose selectively, separate management port, require auth, <code>show-details: when-authorized</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — keep liveness <strong>cheap</strong>. A liveness check that touches the database restarts every pod during a DB blip and turns a small problem into an outage.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-40"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q40</span><span class="qtext">What are REST API good practices?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Resource-oriented plural nouns; verbs carried by HTTP methods</li>
<li>Correct status codes: 201 + <code>Location</code>, 409 conflict, 422 validation, 429 + <code>Retry-After</code></li>
<li>Stateless, versioned from day one, paginated with sensible defaults</li>
<li>Consistent error format across the whole API</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the idempotency table — GET/PUT/DELETE idempotent, POST/PATCH not — which is why a retried POST needs an <code>Idempotency-Key</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-41"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q41</span><span class="qtext">Design a POST endpoint and discuss status codes.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@PostMapping</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"/api/orders"</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="n">ResponseEntity</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">OrderResponse</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">create</span><span class="p">(</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="nd">@Valid</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nd">@RequestBody</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">CreateOrderRequest</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">req</span><span class="p">,</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="nd">@RequestHeader</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">value</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"Idempotency-Key"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">required</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">false</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">key</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">OrderResponse</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">created</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">orderService</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">create</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">req</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">key</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ResponseEntity</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">created</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">URI</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">create</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"/api/orders/"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">created</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">id</span><span class="p">())).</span><span class="na">body</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">created</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><ul>
<li><strong>201</strong> with a <code>Location</code> header on success</li>
<li><strong>400</strong> malformed body · <strong>422</strong> semantically invalid</li>
<li><strong>401</strong> unauthenticated · <strong>403</strong> authenticated but not allowed</li>
<li><strong>409</strong> conflicts with existing state · <strong>429</strong> rate-limited</li>
<li><strong>500</strong> genuine server faults only</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the idempotency key makes a client retry safe — that's the detail they're listening for.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-42"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q42</span><span class="qtext">How do you secure a REST API in Spring?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Stateless JWT bearer tokens via <code>oauth2ResourceServer</code>, HTTPS only</li>
<li><code>SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS</code></li>
<li>Method security with <code>@PreAuthorize</code></li>
<li>CORS restricted to known origins — never <code>*</code> with credentials</li>
<li>Rate limiting; never log tokens</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — CSRF disabled here is <strong>correct</strong>, precisely because the token sits in a header rather than a cookie, so the browser doesn't attach it automatically. Say <em>why</em>, don't just say you disabled it.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-8-hibernate-jpa-databases">8. Hibernate, JPA &amp; databases<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-8-hibernate-jpa-databases">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-43"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q43</span><span class="qtext">JPA vs Hibernate.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — JPA is the specification, Hibernate is the implementation.</p><ul>
<li>JPA gives the annotations, <code>EntityManager</code> and JPQL.</li>
<li>Hibernate generates the SQL, manages the persistence context, handles lazy loading.</li>
<li>Spring Data JPA is a further layer: repositories, derived queries, paging, auditing.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-44"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q44</span><span class="qtext">Explain lazy vs eager loading.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — lazy fetches an association on first access via a proxy; eager fetches it with the parent.</p><ul>
<li><code>@ManyToOne</code> and <code>@OneToOne</code> default to <strong>EAGER</strong> — a bad default. Set them LAZY.</li>
<li><code>@OneToMany</code>/<code>@ManyToMany</code> default to LAZY.</li>
<li>Touching a lazy association after the session closes → <code>LazyInitializationException</code>.</li>
<li>Fix with <code>JOIN FETCH</code>, <code>@EntityGraph</code>, or a DTO projection.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — the fix is <strong>not</strong> <code>open-in-view=true</code>. That holds a DB connection for the whole request and hides N+1s. Turn it off.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-45"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q45</span><span class="qtext">What is the N+1 problem and how do you fix it?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — one query loads N parents, then N more fire — one per association.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Detect:</strong> log SQL, enable Hibernate statistics, or assert query counts in a test.</li>
<li><strong>Fix:</strong> <code>JOIN FETCH</code> · <code>@EntityGraph(attributePaths=…)</code> · <code>@BatchSize(25)</code>, turning N queries into N/25 <code>IN</code> queries · DTO projection on read paths.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — <code>JOIN FETCH</code> plus pagination triggers <code>HHH000104</code> — Hibernate pages <strong>in memory</strong>. Fetch the ID page first, then the entities by those IDs.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-46"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q46</span><span class="qtext">Explain cascade types and <code>orphanRemoval</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — cascade propagates an operation parent→child; <code>orphanRemoval</code> deletes a child removed from the collection.</p><ul>
<li>Types: <code>PERSIST</code>, <code>MERGE</code>, <code>REMOVE</code>, <code>REFRESH</code>, <code>DETACH</code>, <code>ALL</code>.</li>
<li><code>orphanRemoval=true</code> is stronger than <code>CascadeType.REMOVE</code> — it fires on collection removal, not just parent deletion.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — never cascade <code>REMOVE</code> across <code>@ManyToMany</code> — you'll delete rows another parent still references.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-47"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q47</span><span class="qtext">What is normalisation, and why normalise?</span></p><div class="a"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Form</th>
<th>Rule</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1NF</td>
<td>Atomic values, no repeating groups</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2NF</td>
<td>No partial dependency on part of a composite key</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3NF</td>
<td>No transitive dependency — non-key attributes depend only on the key</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table><ul>
<li><strong>Benefits:</strong> no duplicated data to drift out of sync, smaller writes, integrity enforced by constraints.</li>
<li><strong>Cost:</strong> more joins on read.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — that cost is exactly why reporting and feed paths are often deliberately <strong>denormalised</strong>. Naming the trade-off is the senior part of the answer.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-48"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q48</span><span class="qtext">How does indexing work, and when does it hurt?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — usually a B-tree, turning an O(n) scan into an O(log n) seek — paid for on every write.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Composite order matters:</strong> an index on <code>(a,b,c)</code> serves <code>a</code>, <code>a,b</code>, <code>a,b,c</code> — but <strong>not <code>b</code> alone</strong>.</li>
<li>A <strong>covering</strong> index includes every column the query needs, so no table lookup.</li>
<li>Costs: slower INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, plus storage.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — index usage is killed by a function on the column, a leading wildcard <code>LIKE '%x'</code>, implicit type conversion, or very low cardinality.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-49"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q49</span><span class="qtext">How do you read and use an execution plan?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>, then compare <strong>estimated rows to actual rows</strong> — that gap is usually where the answer is.</p><p>Look for:</p><ul>
<li>Sequential scans on large tables</li>
<li>Bad row estimates → stale statistics, run <code>ANALYZE</code></li>
<li>Nested loops over large sets</li>
<li>Sorts spilling to disk</li>
</ul><p>Then act: add or reorder an index, make the predicate sargable, select fewer columns, replace a large <code>OFFSET</code> with keyset pagination.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-50"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q50</span><span class="qtext">Explain the join types.</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><strong>INNER</strong> — matching rows only</li>
<li><strong>LEFT OUTER</strong> — all left rows, nulls where no match · <strong>RIGHT</strong> is the mirror</li>
<li><strong>FULL OUTER</strong> — both sides</li>
<li><strong>CROSS</strong> — Cartesian product</li>
<li><strong>SELF</strong> — a table joined to itself, classically employees to managers on <code>e.manager_id = m.id</code></li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-51"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q51</span><span class="qtext">Tables vs views — when would you use a view?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a table stores data; a view is a stored query evaluated at read time.</p><ul>
<li>Use for: simplifying complex joins, a stable interface over a changing schema, restricting access to a row or column subset (you can grant on the view alone).</li>
<li>A <strong>materialised</strong> view <em>does</em> store results and can be indexed — real speed, at the cost of staleness and a refresh strategy.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — a plain view is just query substitution. It makes <strong>nothing</strong> faster, and nested views produce terrible plans.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-52"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q52</span><span class="qtext">Where have you used caching and Redis?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Cache-aside for reference data and expensive reads</li>
<li>Session storage · rate limiting with an atomic Lua script · distributed locks</li>
<li>Idempotency keys · sorted sets for leaderboards</li>
<li>In Spring: <code>@Cacheable</code>/<code>@CacheEvict</code>, Caffeine locally or Redis distributed</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — name the failure modes unprompted — stale data, <strong>cache stampede</strong> on expiry (jitter TTLs or single-flight), hot keys. And <code>@Cacheable</code> has the same self-invocation limitation as <code>@Transactional</code>.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-9-microservices-distributed-systems">9. Microservices &amp; distributed systems<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-9-microservices-distributed-systems">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-53"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q53</span><span class="qtext">What is the CAP theorem?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — under a network partition you get consistency or availability, not both — and partitions are a fact, not a choice.</p><ul>
<li>The real decision is what happens <strong>during</strong> a partition: refuse requests to stay correct, or serve stale data to stay up.</li>
<li><strong>PACELC</strong> extends it: <em>else</em>, with no partition, you still trade latency against consistency.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — make it concrete — a wallet or payment ledger is CP; a product catalogue or odds display is AP.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-54"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q54</span><span class="qtext">Explain the Saga pattern.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a distributed transaction replaced by a sequence of local transactions, each publishing an event, with compensating transactions to undo on failure.</p><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th></th>
<th>Choreography</th>
<th>Orchestration</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Flow lives</td>
<td>Implicitly, across services</td>
<td>Explicitly, in a coordinator</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Good for</td>
<td>2–3 services</td>
<td>Complex flows</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Downside</td>
<td>Nobody can see the whole process</td>
<td>Everyone couples to the orchestrator</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-55"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q55</span><span class="qtext">What are the drawbacks of Saga?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — you trade atomicity and isolation for availability, and pay for it in complexity.</p><ul>
<li><strong>No isolation</strong> — local transactions commit as they go, so intermediate states are visible. Mitigate with semantic locks or a pending-status flag.</li>
<li><strong>Compensations aren't rollbacks</strong> — they're new forward actions. Some things can't be undone: you issue a refund, not a reversal.</li>
<li><strong>Compensations can fail too</strong> — needing retries, idempotency, and a manual intervention path.</li>
<li><strong>Debuggability</strong> suffers, especially with choreography.</li>
<li><strong>Eventual consistency leaks into the UI</strong>, which must show pending states honestly.</li>
<li><strong>Testing every failure branch</strong> is genuinely hard.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — you accept all that because the alternative, 2PC, blocks resources and has a coordinator single point of failure. This is the reported senior follow-up — naming the downsides <em>is</em> the answer.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-56"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q56</span><span class="qtext">How do 2PC and CQRS fit in?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li><strong>2PC</strong> — coordinator runs prepare then commit across participants. Real atomicity, but holds locks through the exchange, blocks if the coordinator dies mid-protocol, scales poorly. Avoided across services.</li>
<li><strong>CQRS</strong> — separate write model (normalised, aggregate-based) from read model (denormalised, query-shaped), synced by events. Use when read and write shapes differ sharply; cost is eventual consistency and more moving parts.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-57"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q57</span><span class="qtext">How does a service registry work, and what's a heartbeat?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — instances register on startup, prove they're alive with periodic heartbeats, and get evicted when they stop.</p><ul>
<li>Clients <strong>fetch and cache</strong> the registry, then load-balance locally — which is why they survive a brief registry outage.</li>
<li>Eureka knobs: <code>lease-renewal-interval-in-seconds</code> (30) · <code>lease-expiration-duration-in-seconds</code> (90) · <code>registry-fetch-interval-seconds</code> (30).</li>
<li>Self-preservation: if Eureka loses many heartbeats at once it assumes a network problem and stops evicting.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — on Kubernetes you often don't need any of this — Services plus kube-dns do discovery, readiness probes do the liveness part. Knowing when the pattern is redundant is the senior signal.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-58"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q58</span><span class="qtext">How does an API gateway route to a specific instance?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — it matches a predicate, resolves the logical service name through discovery, and a load balancer picks an instance.</p><ul>
<li>Predicates: path, host, header, method, query param.</li>
<li>In Spring Cloud Gateway the target is <code>uri: lb://order-service</code> — the <code>lb://</code> scheme means "resolve via discovery, then load-balance".</li>
<li>Filters run around it: auth, rate limiting, header rewriting, circuit breaking.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the gateway's real purpose is putting cross-cutting concerns in <strong>one</strong> place instead of duplicating them in every service.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-59"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q59</span><span class="qtext">What does a config server store and how do services fetch it?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — externalised configuration in a versioned store, usually Git, returned to the client at startup as a high-precedence property source.</p><ul>
<li>Files named by application and profile: <code>order-service-prod.yml</code>, plus <code>application.yml</code> for shared defaults.</li>
<li>Client sends its application name, active profiles and an optional label (Git branch).</li>
<li>Secrets encrypted (<code>{cipher}…</code>) or from Vault — never plain in Git.</li>
<li><code>@RefreshScope</code> beans rebuild on <code>/actuator/refresh</code>; Spring Cloud Bus broadcasts it to all instances.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — it's a <strong>startup dependency</strong>. Configure retry and fail-fast, or a config server blip takes down every deploy.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-60"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q60</span><span class="qtext">What would you change to add a new microservice?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a ten-step checklist, and the first step is deciding whether it should exist at all.</p><ol>
<li><strong>Justify the boundary</strong> — a real bounded context with its own data and reason to change?</li>
<li>Scaffold from the team's parent POM and conventions</li>
<li><strong>Own its data</strong> — its own schema, Flyway migrations, no shared tables</li>
<li>Register with discovery; add a gateway route</li>
<li>Publish a <strong>versioned</strong> OpenAPI contract</li>
<li>Config per profile; secrets from Vault</li>
<li>Integration — sync for query-and-answer, Kafka for state changes; contract tests both ways</li>
<li>Resilience — timeouts everywhere, circuit breaker, retry only where idempotent</li>
<li>Observability <strong>before</strong> it takes traffic — probes, metrics, logs, dashboard, alerts</li>
<li>CI job, image, chart, resource limits, HPA, canary</li>
</ol><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — finish by checking you haven't built a <strong>distributed monolith</strong> — two services that must always deploy together.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-61"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q61</span><span class="qtext">Sync vs async — <code>RestTemplate</code> vs <code>FeignClient</code>?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — commands that need an answer go sync; state changes others care about go as events.</p><ul>
<li>Sync — simple, immediately consistent, but couples availability. Callee down means you're down.</li>
<li>Async — decouples, absorbs spikes, survives outages; costs eventual consistency and harder debugging.</li>
</ul><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Client</th>
<th>Status</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>RestTemplate</code></td>
<td>Blocking, maintenance mode</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>RestClient</code></td>
<td>Modern synchronous replacement (Spring 6.1)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>WebClient</code></td>
<td>Reactive, non-blocking</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Feign</strong></td>
<td>Declarative; integrates with discovery, load balancing, Resilience4j</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-62"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q62</span><span class="qtext">Explain the circuit breaker, and where load balancers fit.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — CLOSED → OPEN when the failure rate crosses a threshold → HALF_OPEN to test → back to CLOSED or OPEN.</p><ul>
<li>OPEN fails fast with a fallback, protecting <strong>both</strong> you and the struggling downstream.</li>
<li>Pair with: timeouts on every call, a bulkhead so one dependency can't exhaust the pool, retries only where idempotent.</li>
<li>Load balancers: at the edge (L4/L7, round-robin or least-connections with health checks) and client-side inside the estate via discovery.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-63"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q63</span><span class="qtext">Explain Kafka — consumer groups, partitions, delivery semantics.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — partitions are the unit of both parallelism and ordering, and each is consumed by exactly one consumer in a group.</p><ul>
<li>Producers pick a partition by key hash, so <strong>the same key always lands in the same partition</strong> — that's how you get per-entity ordering.</li>
<li>Max useful parallelism = partition count.</li>
<li><strong>At-most-once</strong> commit before processing · <strong>at-least-once</strong> process then commit (the normal choice) · <strong>exactly-once</strong> via transactions + <code>enable.idempotence</code>, holding only within Kafka.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — end-to-end exactly-once with an external database doesn't exist. At-least-once plus idempotent consumers is the real answer.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-64"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q64</span><span class="qtext">What do you do when a Kafka consumer fails?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — classify first — transient failures retry with backoff, permanent ones go straight to a dead letter topic.</p><ul>
<li>Transient (downstream timeout) → exponential backoff with jitter.</li>
<li>Permanent (deserialization, validation) → DLT immediately, because retrying forever <strong>blocks the partition</strong>.</li>
<li>Spring Kafka: <code>DefaultErrorHandler</code> + <code>DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer</code>, non-retryable exceptions registered explicitly.</li>
<li>Commit offsets <strong>after</strong> processing, auto-commit off.</li>
<li>Consumers must be <strong>idempotent</strong> — at-least-once means duplicates are normal.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — blocking retries that exceed <code>max.poll.interval.ms</code> trigger a rebalance and make things worse. Use non-blocking retry topics for long backoffs. And alert on the DLT — it's not a bin.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-65"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q65</span><span class="qtext">How is logging done centrally?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — structured JSON to stdout with a correlation ID, shipped by an agent, indexed in Elasticsearch, queried in Kibana.</p><ol>
<li>Service logs JSON with a correlation ID from MDC</li>
<li>Shipper (Filebeat/Fluent Bit, a DaemonSet on K8s) tails the log stream, tracks its offset so it resumes after restart, batches and forwards</li>
<li>Logstash optionally parses, enriches, masks sensitive fields</li>
<li>Elasticsearch indexes into time-based indices with an ILM policy that rolls over and expires them</li>
<li>Kibana queries</li>
</ol><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the <strong>correlation ID</strong> is what makes it useful — it's what lets you reconstruct one user journey across services. Logs answer "what happened"; traces answer "where did the time go".</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-66"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q66</span><span class="qtext">How is monitoring done in your project?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Micrometer → Prometheus → Grafana</li>
<li><strong>RED metrics</strong> per endpoint: Rate, Errors, Duration — plus JVM, GC, pools, Kafka lag</li>
<li>Alerts on <strong>SLO burn rate</strong>, not raw CPU</li>
<li>Traces via OpenTelemetry → Jaeger</li>
<li>Health endpoints backing the Kubernetes probes</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the test I apply — could a new on-call engineer diagnose a p99 spike at 3am from the dashboards alone? If not, it isn't observable.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-67"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q67</span><span class="qtext">How does DNS work?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — the resolver checks caches, then a recursive resolver walks root → TLD → authoritative, caching the answer for its TTL.</p><ul>
<li>Records: A/AAAA addresses · CNAME alias · MX mail · TXT verification · NS delegation · SRV service location.</li>
<li>Why it matters here: it's how Kubernetes service discovery works, and TTLs govern how fast a failover actually takes effect.</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — the <strong>JVM has its own DNS cache</strong> — <code>networkaddress.cache.ttl</code>. Historically it kept services hammering a dead IP long after a failover. Set it explicitly.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-10-design-patterns-solid">10. Design patterns &amp; SOLID<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-10-design-patterns-solid">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-68"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q68</span><span class="qtext">Which design patterns have you used in real projects?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — answer with three, each with context — not a list of twenty.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Strategy</strong> — a <code>List&lt;Handler&gt;</code> injected by Spring, replacing a growing switch on payment or promotion type. Adding a type becomes adding a class.</li>
<li><strong>Chain of Responsibility</strong> — a validation pipeline. Note that servlet filters and Spring Security's filter chain <em>are</em> this pattern.</li>
<li><strong>Builder</strong> — immutable request objects with many optional fields, avoiding telescoping constructors.</li>
<li>If pressed for a fourth: <strong>Template Method</strong> for a family of nightly jobs sharing a skeleton.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-69"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q69</span><span class="qtext">Explain Singleton and how you'd implement it safely.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — the enum is the safest form; the static holder idiom is the best lazy one.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">enum</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Config</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">INSTANCE</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span><span class="w">                    </span><span class="c1">// serialization- and reflection-proof</span>
</code></pre></div><ul>
<li>Static holder: lazy <strong>and</strong> thread-safe with no locking.</li>
<li>Double-checked locking works but <strong>requires <code>volatile</code></strong>, or reordering lets another thread see a partially constructed object.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — in Spring you rarely write any of this — beans are singleton-scoped already. And Singleton is often an anti-pattern: global mutable state, hard to test.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-70"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q70</span><span class="qtext">Explain the Strategy pattern with an example.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — encapsulate interchangeable algorithms behind one interface so the caller stops branching.</p><ul>
<li><code>PaymentProcessor</code> with card, PayPal and crypto implementations.</li>
<li>Spring injects them all as a <code>List</code> or a <code>Map</code> keyed by type; the service looks up the right one.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — this <em>is</em> the Open/Closed Principle in practice — a new payment type is a new class and zero edits to existing code.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-71"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q71</span><span class="qtext">Explain Liskov Substitution with a violation and a fix.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — subtypes must be substitutable for their base type without breaking correctness.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Violation:</strong> <code>Bird</code> with <code>fly()</code>, and <code>Ostrich</code> overriding it to throw <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code>. Anything holding a <code>Bird</code> reference now breaks at runtime.</li>
<li><strong>Fix:</strong> model the capability separately — <code>Bird</code> with <code>eat()</code>, and a <code>FlyingBird</code> interface extending it.</li>
<li><strong>Rule:</strong> a subclass may weaken preconditions and strengthen postconditions, never the reverse.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-72"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q72</span><span class="qtext">Explain Interface Segregation with a violation and a fix.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — no client should be forced to depend on methods it doesn't use.</p><ul>
<li><strong>Violation:</strong> a fat <code>Worker</code> interface with <code>work()</code> and <code>eat()</code>; <code>Robot</code> implements it by throwing on <code>eat()</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Fix:</strong> split into <code>Workable</code> and <code>Eatable</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — the two violations look identical — a method that throws — because a fat interface <strong>forces</strong> LSP violations. Fixing the interface fixes both. Making that connection is what separates a memorised answer from an understood one.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p10-11-devops-cloud-git">11. DevOps, cloud &amp; Git<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p10-11-devops-cloud-git">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q10-73"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q73</span><span class="qtext">Walk me through your CI/CD pipeline.</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a declarative <code>Jenkinsfile</code> in the repo, triggered on PR and merge, promoting one artifact through environments.</p><ol>
<li>Checkout → build and unit test</li>
<li>Static analysis: SonarQube quality gate + coverage threshold</li>
<li>Dependency vulnerability scan</li>
<li>Integration tests with Testcontainers</li>
<li>Build and push image, tagged with the commit SHA</li>
<li>Deploy to dev → smoke tests → staging → manual approval → production (rolling or canary)</li>
</ol><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — secrets from the credentials store or Vault, never the Jenkinsfile. The <strong>same artifact is promoted</strong> rather than rebuilt per environment. Rollback is <code>helm rollback</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-74"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q74</span><span class="qtext">How do you containerise a Spring Boot app?</span></p><div class="a"><p class="tldr"><strong>In one line</strong> — a multi-stage Dockerfile — a build stage with the JDK, then a slim JRE runtime stage that copies only the jar.</p><ul>
<li>Cache dependencies in their own layer; order layers by change frequency</li>
<li>Non-root user, <code>.dockerignore</code>, pinned base image versions</li>
</ul><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — use <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage</code>, not <code>-Xmx</code>. Heap should scale with the container limit instead of being hardcoded.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-75"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q75</span><span class="qtext">Which Kubernetes concepts do you use day to day?</span></p><div class="a"><ul>
<li>Deployments with rolling updates (<code>maxSurge</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code>), Services, Ingress</li>
<li>ConfigMaps and Secrets, HPA, resource requests and limits, Helm</li>
</ul><p class="bonus"><strong>Say this too</strong> — <strong>readiness vs liveness</strong> is the distinction they're checking. Readiness pulls a pod out of the Service when it can't serve; liveness <strong>restarts</strong> it.</p><p class="gotcha"><strong>Gotcha</strong> — an expensive or DB-dependent liveness probe causes restart loops under load, turning a blip into an outage. Pair <code>server.shutdown=graceful</code> with a <code>preStop</code> sleep so the load balancer deregisters first.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q10-76"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q76</span><span class="qtext">Which cloud services have you used?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Name five or six specifically rather than saying "AWS" or "Azure".</p><table>
