<hr/>
<h2 id="p11-1-the-high-frequency-items-as-you-d-say-them">1. The high-frequency items, as you'd say them<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p11-1-the-high-frequency-items-as-you-d-say-them">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q11-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q1</span><span class="qtext">How does HashMap work internally?</span></p><div class="a"><p>It's an array of buckets. On <code>put</code>, the key's <code>hashCode()</code> is spread with <code>h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code> — that XORs the high bits down into the low bits, because the bucket index is <code>(n-1) &amp; hash</code> and only the low bits participate. Table length is always a power of two so the mask replaces a modulo. If two keys land in the same bucket, entries chain in a linked list; once a bin holds eight or more nodes <em>and</em> the table is at least 64, that bin converts to a red-black tree, so worst-case lookup goes from O(n) to O(log n). Default capacity is 16 with a load factor of 0.75, so at 12 entries it doubles and rehashes. One null key is allowed. And the practical consequence: keys must be effectively immutable, because mutating a field used in <code>hashCode</code> after insertion strands the entry in the wrong bucket and you can never retrieve or remove it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-2"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q2</span><span class="qtext">HashMap vs ConcurrentHashMap — how does CHM achieve thread safety?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Hashtable synchronises every method on the whole map, so it serialises everything. Java 8's ConcurrentHashMap locks at bucket granularity: it uses a CAS to install the first node in an empty bin, and <code>synchronized</code> on the bin's head node when there's already a chain. Reads are lock-free entirely — the nodes' <code>val</code> and <code>next</code> are volatile. So readers never block and writers only contend when they hit the same bucket. Trade-offs: no null keys or values, because <code>get</code> returning null would be ambiguous and you couldn't disambiguate atomically; <code>size()</code> is an estimate under concurrent modification; iterators are weakly consistent rather than fail-fast. And use the atomic compound methods — <code>computeIfAbsent</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>putIfAbsent</code> — because check-then-act with <code>containsKey</code> then <code>put</code> is still a race.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q3</span><span class="qtext">Explain the Java Memory Model and happens-before.</span></p><div class="a"><p>The JMM defines when a write by one thread becomes visible to another. Without it, the compiler and CPU are free to reorder instructions and cache values in registers, so a write in thread A may never be seen by thread B. Happens-before is the ordering guarantee: if action X happens-before Y, X's effects are visible to Y. The edges are program order within a single thread, unlocking a monitor before a subsequent lock of the same monitor, a volatile write before a subsequent read of that variable, <code>Thread.start()</code> before everything in that thread, everything in a thread before <code>join()</code> returns, and final field initialisation before the constructor completes. Everything else is unordered and therefore unsafe.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q4</span><span class="qtext"><code>volatile</code> vs <code>synchronized</code> — when do you use which?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>volatile</code> guarantees visibility and prevents reordering across the access, but gives you no atomicity — <code>count++</code> is read-modify-write and stays racy even on a volatile field. So it's for flags, for safely publishing an immutable object, and for the double-checked-locking singleton. <code>synchronized</code> gives mutual exclusion <em>and</em> visibility, is reentrant, and is what you need for any compound operation. If all you need is a single atomic read-modify-write, an <code>AtomicInteger</code> beats both — it's CAS-based and lock-free.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q5</span><span class="qtext">Method overloading vs overriding, and how does dynamic dispatch actually work?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Overloading is compile-time: same name, different parameter list, and the compiler picks the most specific match using the <em>declared</em> type of the reference. Return type alone can't distinguish overloads. Overriding is runtime: the JVM looks up the method in the actual object's vtable, so <code>A ref = new B()</code> calling <code>ref.method()</code> runs B's version. The distinction worth adding: <strong>fields are not polymorphic</strong> — <code>ref.name</code> resolves statically to A's field, and <code>static</code> methods are hidden rather than overridden, also resolved by reference type.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q6</span><span class="qtext">How do generics work under the hood?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Type erasure. The compiler checks types, then erases the parameters to their leftmost bound — <code>Object</code> by default — and inserts casts at the call sites. The bytecode has no runtime type parameters. Consequences you live with: you can't do <code>new T()</code> or <code>new T[]</code>, <code>instanceof List&lt;String&gt;</code> is illegal, <code>f(List&lt;String&gt;)</code> and <code>f(List&lt;Integer&gt;)</code> clash because they have the same erasure, static fields are shared across all parameterisations, and you can't catch or throw a generic exception type. Erasure is also why raw types can silently poison a collection and blow up later with a ClassCastException at a line that looks innocent.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q7</span><span class="qtext">Explain the exception hierarchy.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>Throwable</code> splits into <code>Error</code> and <code>Exception</code>. Errors like <code>OutOfMemoryError</code> and <code>StackOverflowError</code> signal JVM-level problems you shouldn't catch. Under <code>Exception</code>, <code>RuntimeException</code> and its subclasses are unchecked; everything else is checked and must be declared or handled. In practice on a Spring service I use unchecked custom exceptions extending <code>RuntimeException</code> plus a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code>, because checked exceptions pollute every signature and don't compose with lambdas or streams. The one thing to be careful about: <code>@Transactional</code> only rolls back on unchecked exceptions by default, so a checked exception commits unless you set <code>rollbackFor</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q8</span><span class="qtext">Why doesn't <code>@Transactional</code> work on private methods or self-invocation?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Because it's proxy-based. Spring wraps the bean in a JDK dynamic proxy or a CGLIB subclass, and the transaction is opened by the proxy before delegating to your object. A private method is never proxied, and <code>this.method()</code> calls the raw object directly, bypassing the proxy entirely — so the annotation silently does nothing, which is worse than failing. Fixes: move the method to a separate bean, self-inject the proxy, or get it from the ApplicationContext. And the same limitation applies to <code>@Async</code>, <code>@Cacheable</code> and <code>@PreAuthorize</code>, because they're all proxy-based.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q9</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Qualifier</code> vs <code>@Primary</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Both resolve the "which of these beans" ambiguity, but from different directions. <code>@Primary</code> sits on the bean and says "pick me by default when nothing more specific is asked for" — one decision covering every injection point. <code>@Qualifier</code> sits at the injection point and names the bean explicitly, overriding <code>@Primary</code>. So <code>@Primary</code> is for the sensible default and <code>@Qualifier</code> for the exception. Two others worth mentioning: the parameter name is matched against the bean name as a fallback, and injecting <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> gives you every implementation, which is how you wire a Strategy pattern.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q10</span><span class="qtext">What is the CAP theorem?</span></p><div class="a"><p>In a distributed system you can have at most two of consistency, availability and partition tolerance. Since network partitions are a fact rather than a choice, the real decision is what you do <em>during</em> a partition: refuse requests to stay consistent, or serve possibly-stale data to stay available. In practice I'd cite PACELC, which extends it — else, when there's no partition, you're still trading latency against consistency. Concretely: a wallet or payment ledger is CP, because a wrong balance is unacceptable; a product catalogue or an odds display is AP, because briefly stale is fine.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q11</span><span class="qtext">Which design patterns have you used, and where?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Answer with three real ones, not a list of twenty. A good default set: <strong>Strategy</strong>, injected as a <code>List&lt;Handler&gt;</code> by Spring to replace a growing switch on payment or promotion type; <strong>Chain of Responsibility</strong>, for a validation or filter pipeline — and note that Spring Security's filter chain and servlet filters are exactly this; <strong>Builder</strong>, for immutable request objects with many optional fields. Then one more if pressed: <strong>Template Method</strong> for a family of nightly jobs sharing a skeleton, or <strong>Observer</strong> via <code>ApplicationEventPublisher</code> with <code>@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)</code> so events only fire after the DB commit.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q12</span><span class="qtext">Explain Liskov Substitution and Interface Segregation with a violation and a fix.</span></p><div class="a"><p><em>LSP:</em> subtypes must be substitutable for their base type without breaking correctness. The classic violation is a <code>Bird</code> class with <code>fly()</code> and an <code>Ostrich</code> subclass that throws <code>UnsupportedOperationException</code> — anything holding a <code>Bird</code> reference now breaks at runtime. The fix is to model the capability separately: <code>Bird</code> with <code>eat()</code>, and a <code>FlyingBird</code> interface extending it. Rule: a subclass may weaken preconditions and strengthen postconditions, never the reverse.
<em>ISP:</em> no client should depend on methods it doesn't use. The violation is a fat <code>Worker</code> interface with <code>work()</code> and <code>eat()</code> that a <code>Robot</code> implements by throwing on <code>eat()</code>. Split it into <code>Workable</code> and <code>Eatable</code>. In practice this means many small role interfaces rather than one god interface. Note the two failures look identical — a method that throws — because a fat interface <em>forces</em> LSP violations. Fixing the interface fixes both.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p11-2-the-gaps-reported-questions-not-answered-elsewhere-in-this">2. The gaps — reported questions not answered elsewhere in this kit<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p11-2-the-gaps-reported-questions-not-answered-elsewhere-in-this">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q11-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q13</span><span class="qtext">What is <code>transient</code>, and when have you used it?</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>transient</code> excludes a field from Java's default serialization — on deserialization it comes back as the type's default, so <code>null</code> or <code>0</code>. Use it for anything that shouldn't or can't cross the wire: cached derived values, an open connection or stream, a thread, a logger, or sensitive data like a password or key. It applies only to <code>Serializable</code> serialization, not to Jackson — Jackson uses <code>@JsonIgnore</code>. Note also that <code>static</code> fields aren't serialized either, since they belong to the class rather than the instance.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q14</span><span class="qtext">How does a service registry work? What's a heartbeat, and where is it configured?</span></p><div class="a"><p>The registry is a lookup service mapping logical service names to live instance addresses. Each instance <strong>registers</strong> itself on startup with its host, port, and health URL. It then sends a periodic <strong>heartbeat</strong> — a lightweight renewal call — proving it's still alive; if the registry misses heartbeats for a configured window, it evicts the instance so no traffic is routed there. Clients <strong>fetch</strong> the registry and cache it locally, then load-balance across the instances themselves (client-side discovery), which is why a client keeps working briefly even if the registry is down.
With Eureka the relevant properties are <code>eureka.client.service-url.defaultZone</code> (where the registry lives), <code>eureka.instance.lease-renewal-interval-in-seconds</code> (heartbeat frequency, default 30), <code>eureka.instance.lease-expiration-duration-in-seconds</code> (how long before eviction, default 90), and <code>eureka.client.registry-fetch-interval-seconds</code> (default 30). Eureka also has <strong>self-preservation</strong>: if it loses too many heartbeats at once it assumes a network problem rather than mass instance death and stops evicting, favouring availability over accuracy.
Worth adding: <strong>on Kubernetes you usually don't need any of this</strong> — the Service object plus kube-dns does discovery, and readiness probes do what heartbeats do. Saying that shows you know when the pattern is redundant.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q15</span><span class="qtext">How does an API gateway route a request to a specific instance?</span></p><div class="a"><p>The gateway matches the incoming request against a <strong>predicate</strong> — usually a path, but it can be host, header, method or query param — and maps it to a route with a target URI. When that URI is a logical service name rather than a host, the gateway resolves it through service discovery to the current list of instances, then a <strong>load balancer</strong> picks one, typically round-robin by default. Filters run before and after: authentication, rate limiting, header rewriting, circuit breaking, retries.
In Spring Cloud Gateway the route target is written as <code>lb://order-service</code>, where the <code>lb://</code> scheme tells it to go through Spring Cloud LoadBalancer rather than treating it as a literal host. Concretely:</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nt">spring</span><span class="p">:</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="nt">cloud</span><span class="p">:</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nt">gateway</span><span class="p">:</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="nt">routes</span><span class="p">:</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p p-Indicator">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nt">id</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="l l-Scalar l-Scalar-Plain">orders</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="nt">uri</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="l l-Scalar l-Scalar-Plain">lb://order-service</span><span class="w">          </span><span class="c1"># lb:// = resolve via discovery, then load-balance</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="nt">predicates</span><span class="p">:</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="p p-Indicator">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="l l-Scalar l-Scalar-Plain">Path=/api/orders/**</span>
<span class="w">          </span><span class="nt">filters</span><span class="p">:</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="p p-Indicator">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="l l-Scalar l-Scalar-Plain">StripPrefix=1</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="p p-Indicator">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nt">name</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="l l-Scalar l-Scalar-Plain">CircuitBreaker</span>
<span class="w">              </span><span class="nt">args</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p p-Indicator">{</span><span class="nt"> name</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nv">ordersCb</span><span class="p p-Indicator">,</span><span class="nt"> fallbackUri</span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nv">forward</span><span class="p p-Indicator">:</span><span class="nv">/fallback/orders</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p p-Indicator">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Also mention what the gateway is <em>for</em>: one entry point, so cross-cutting concerns — auth, rate limiting, CORS, tracing headers, TLS termination — live in one place instead of being duplicated in every service.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q16</span><span class="qtext">What does a config server store, and how do services fetch it?</span></p><div class="a"><p>It centralises externalised configuration so the same image runs in every environment. The server is backed by a versioned store — usually a Git repo — holding files named by application and profile: <code>order-service-prod.yml</code>, <code>application.yml</code> for shared defaults. On startup a client calls the server with its <code>spring.application.name</code>, active profiles and an optional label (Git branch), and the returned properties are inserted as a high-precedence property source, so they override the packaged <code>application.yml</code>. Secrets don't go in Git — they're encrypted with the server's key (<code>{cipher}...</code>) or pulled from Vault.
For refresh without restart: beans annotated <code>@RefreshScope</code> are re-created when <code>/actuator/refresh</code> is called, and Spring Cloud Bus can broadcast that to every instance over a message broker instead of you calling each one.
The honest caveat: it's a startup dependency, so you need retry and fail-fast configured, and on Kubernetes many teams use ConfigMaps and Secrets instead.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q17</span><span class="qtext">Walk me through adding a new microservice to your existing system.</span></p><div class="a"><p>This is reported as an in-depth question, so answer it as a sequence, and anchor it to your own system.</p><ol>
<li><strong>Justify the boundary</strong> — is this a distinct bounded context with its own data and its own reason to change? If it just shares the same aggregate, it belongs in an existing service.</li>
<li><strong>Scaffold</strong> — new repo or module, the standard starter set, the team's parent POM, the shared logging/error/tracing conventions.</li>
<li><strong>Own its data</strong> — its own schema or database, with Flyway migrations. No shared tables with another service.</li>
<li><strong>Register and expose</strong> — discovery client config, a route added to the gateway, and its API contract published (OpenAPI), versioned from day one.</li>
<li><strong>Configuration</strong> — add its config files to the config repo per profile; wire secrets from Vault or Secrets Manager.</li>
<li><strong>Integration</strong> — define how it talks to others: sync REST/Feign for query-and-answer, Kafka topics for state changes it publishes. Consumer contract tests both ways.</li>
<li><strong>Resilience</strong> — timeouts on every outbound call, circuit breaker, bulkhead, retry only where idempotent.</li>
<li><strong>Observability</strong> — health endpoints wired to readiness and liveness probes, metrics scraped, structured logs shipping with the correlation ID, a dashboard and alerts before it takes traffic.</li>
<li><strong>Pipeline and deploy</strong> — CI job, image build, Helm chart or manifests, resource requests and limits, HPA, then a canary or a dark launch behind a flag.</li>
<li><strong>Security</strong> — its own service identity, authorisation rules on the gateway and in the service, network policy.
Finish by naming what you'd check afterwards: is the dependency graph still acyclic, and did we accidentally create a distributed monolith by making two services that must always deploy together.</li>
</ol></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q18</span><span class="qtext">How does DNS resolution work?</span></p><div class="a"><p>The resolver checks caches first — browser, OS, then the configured recursive resolver. On a miss the recursive resolver walks the hierarchy: a root server tells it which TLD servers handle <code>.com</code>, the TLD server points at the domain's authoritative nameservers, and the authoritative server returns the actual record. The answer is cached at each level for the record's TTL. Record types worth naming: A and AAAA for addresses, CNAME for aliases, MX for mail, TXT for verification, NS for delegation, SRV for service location.
Why it matters in our context: DNS is how service discovery works on Kubernetes, TTLs determine how fast a failover or a blue/green cutover actually takes effect, and the JVM has its own DNS cache — <code>networkaddress.cache.ttl</code> — which historically caused services to keep hammering a dead IP after a failover, so it's worth setting explicitly.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q19</span><span class="qtext">How does centralised logging work internally — the ELK path?</span></p><div class="a"><p>Each service logs locally to stdout in <strong>structured JSON</strong>, including a correlation/trace ID pulled from MDC. A <strong>shipper</strong> running alongside — Filebeat or Fluentd or Fluent Bit, as a DaemonSet on Kubernetes — tails those files or the container log stream, tracks its read offset in a registry file so it resumes after a restart, batches lines, and forwards them. Optionally <strong>Logstash</strong> sits in the middle to parse, enrich with metadata, drop noise, and mask sensitive fields. <strong>Elasticsearch</strong> indexes them — typically into time-based indices with an ILM policy that rolls over daily and moves old indices to cheaper storage then deletes them. <strong>Kibana</strong> queries and visualises.
The parts that make it useful rather than just voluminous: the correlation ID propagated across service hops so you can reconstruct one user journey, index lifecycle management so you don't pay to store six months of DEBUG, back-pressure in the shipper so a slow Elasticsearch doesn't crash the app, and sampling or level-based filtering for high-volume paths. Then note that logs pair with traces (OpenTelemetry → Jaeger) and metrics (Micrometer → Prometheus) — logs alone answer "what happened", traces answer "where was the time spent".</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q20</span><span class="qtext">Explain your CI/CD pipeline in Jenkins, in depth.</span></p><div class="a"><p>A declarative <code>Jenkinsfile</code> in the repo, triggered by a webhook on PR and on merge. Stages: checkout → build and unit test (<code>mvn clean verify</code>) → static analysis and coverage gate (SonarQube quality gate, JaCoCo threshold) → dependency vulnerability scan → integration tests with Testcontainers → build the image and push to the registry tagged with the commit SHA → deploy to dev → smoke tests → promote to staging → manual approval → production via rolling update or canary. Post-build always publishes test results and notifies the channel.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">pipeline</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="n">agent</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">label</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s1">'docker'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="n">options</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">timeout</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="nl">time:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">30</span><span class="o">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nl">unit:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s1">'MINUTES'</span><span class="o">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">disableConcurrentBuilds</span><span class="o">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="n">environment</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">IMAGE</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"registry.example.com/order-service:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(8)}"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="n">stages</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">stage</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s1">'Build &amp; Test'</span><span class="o">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">steps</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">sh</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s1">'mvn -B clean verify'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">stage</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s1">'Quality Gate'</span><span class="o">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="n">steps</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">withSonarQubeEnv</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s1">'sonar'</span><span class="o">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">sh</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s1">'mvn sonar:sonar'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">timeout</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="nl">time:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">5</span><span class="o">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nl">unit:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s1">'MINUTES'</span><span class="o">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">waitForQualityGate</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nl">abortPipeline:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">true</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">      </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">stage</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s1">'Image'</span><span class="o">)</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">steps</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">sh</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"docker build -t $IMAGE . &amp;&amp; docker push $IMAGE"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">stage</span><span class="o">(</span><span class="s1">'Deploy'</span><span class="o">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">steps</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">sh</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s2">"helm upgrade --install order-service ./chart --set image.tag=${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(8)}"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="n">post</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">always</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">junit</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s1">'**/target/surefire-reports/*.xml'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">}</span>
<span class="o">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Points worth volunteering: secrets come from the Jenkins credentials store or Vault, never the Jenkinsfile; agents are ephemeral containers with a cached Maven repo; the same artifact is promoted through environments rather than rebuilt per environment; and rollback is <code>helm rollback</code> to the previous release.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q21</span><span class="qtext">What are the drawbacks of the Saga pattern?</span></p><div class="a"><p>This is the senior follow-up, and naming the downsides is the whole point of the question.</p><ul>
<li><strong>No isolation.</strong> Local transactions commit as they go, so intermediate states are visible to other transactions — you get dirty reads and lost updates that a real ACID transaction would prevent. You mitigate with semantic locks, a status field marking a record pending, or commutative updates.</li>
<li><strong>Compensations aren't rollbacks.</strong> They're new forward transactions, and some actions genuinely can't be undone — an email is sent, money has left the building. You end up with "counter-actions" like a refund rather than a true reversal.</li>
<li><strong>Compensations can fail too</strong>, so they need retries, idempotency and eventually a manual intervention path with alerting.</li>
<li><strong>Complexity and debuggability.</strong> With choreography, the business process exists nowhere explicitly — you have to read every service to understand the flow. With orchestration you get an explicit flow but a central component that everyone couples to.</li>
<li><strong>Eventual consistency leaks into the product</strong>, so the UI has to represent pending states honestly.</li>
<li><strong>Testing is hard</strong> — you have to test every failure point and its compensation path.
Close with when you'd use it anyway: when the alternative is 2PC, which blocks resources, has a coordinator single point of failure, and scales badly.</li>
</ul></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q22</span><span class="qtext">What do you do when a Kafka consumer fails to process a message?</span></p><div class="a"><p>First classify the failure. <strong>Transient</strong> — a downstream timeout, a DB blip — retry with exponential backoff and jitter. <strong>Permanent</strong> — a deserialization failure, a validation error, a bug — retrying forever just blocks the partition, so it goes straight to a dead letter topic.
The mechanism in Spring Kafka is a <code>DefaultErrorHandler</code> with a backoff and a <code>DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer</code>, and you tell it which exceptions are not retryable:</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@Bean</span>
<span class="n">DefaultErrorHandler</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">errorHandler</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">KafkaTemplate</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Object</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">template</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">recoverer</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">template</span><span class="p">,</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">record</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ex</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">TopicPartition</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">record</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">topic</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">".DLT"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">record</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">partition</span><span class="p">()));</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">handler</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">DefaultErrorHandler</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">recoverer</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">3</span><span class="p">));</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">handler</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">addNotRetryableExceptions</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">DeserializationException</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ValidationException</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">handler</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>Things to say alongside: commit offsets <strong>after</strong> successful processing, not before, and turn off auto-commit — that's what makes it at-least-once. Because it's at-least-once, <strong>consumers must be idempotent</strong>: dedupe on a message key or event ID with a unique constraint, or make the operation naturally idempotent. Watch out for blocking retries exceeding <code>max.poll.interval.ms</code>, which triggers a rebalance and makes things worse — for long backoffs use non-blocking retry topics instead. And the DLT isn't a bin: alert on it, and have a tool to inspect and replay.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q23</span><span class="qtext">Tables vs views — when would you use a view?</span></p><div class="a"><p>A table stores data; a view is a stored query with no data of its own, evaluated at read time. Views are for simplifying complex joins, presenting a stable interface over a changing schema, and restricting access to a column or row subset — you can grant on the view without granting on the base table. The cost is that a plain view is just query substitution, so it doesn't make anything faster and nested views can produce terrible plans. A <strong>materialised view</strong> does store results and can be indexed, so it's genuinely faster for expensive aggregates, at the cost of staleness and a refresh strategy. Updatable views exist but are restricted — generally no aggregation, no DISTINCT, single base table.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q24</span><span class="qtext">Why Swagger/OpenAPI, and how do you integrate it?</span></p><div class="a"><p>It generates live, always-current API documentation from the code, so the contract can't drift from the implementation the way a wiki page does. Frontend and third-party consumers can explore and call endpoints without asking backend for a curl example, and the spec can drive generated clients or contract tests. In a Spring Boot 3 service it's <code>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</code>; the endpoints are discovered automatically and you enrich them with <code>@Tag</code>, <code>@Operation</code> and <code>@ApiResponse</code>. Two things worth adding: prefer <strong>contract-first</strong> for public APIs — design the spec, review it, then generate stubs — and secure or disable the UI in production.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q25</span><span class="qtext"><code>@Mock</code> vs <code>@Spy</code> in Mockito.</span></p><div class="a"><p><code>@Mock</code> creates a bare double: every method returns a default — null, zero, empty — until you stub it with <code>when(...).thenReturn(...)</code>. <code>@Spy</code> wraps a <em>real</em> object, so unstubbed methods run the real implementation and you override only what you choose. Use <code>@Mock</code> by default because it gives full control and isolation; use <code>@Spy</code> when you need most of the real behaviour and want to stub or verify one part — often when testing legacy code you can't easily decompose. Two gotchas: to stub a spy you should use <code>doReturn(x).when(spy).method()</code> rather than <code>when(spy.method())</code>, because the latter actually calls the real method first; and <code>@InjectMocks</code> will inject a spy just as it does a mock.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p11-3-the-reported-coding-problems-solved">3. The reported coding problems, solved<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p11-3-the-reported-coding-problems-solved">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q11-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q26</span><span class="qtext">Group anagrams.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">grouped</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kt">char</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toCharArray</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">sort</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}));</span>
<span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">result</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ArrayList</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">grouped</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">values</span><span class="p">());</span>
</code></pre></div><p>O(n·k log k) for n strings of length k. If asked to improve it, replace the sorted key with a 26-slot frequency count rendered as a string — that's O(n·k) and worth mentioning even if you don't write it.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q27</span><span class="qtext">Longest common prefix.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">longestCommonPrefix</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">||</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">""</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">++</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">while</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">!</span><span class="n">strs</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">startsWith</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="p">))</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">substring</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">isEmpty</span><span class="p">())</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">""</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">prefix</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>O(S) where S is the total character count. Edge cases to say out loud: empty array, one element, an empty string in the array, no common prefix at all.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q28</span><span class="qtext">Maximum subarray sum (Kadane).</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">maxSubArray</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">best</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">current</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">++</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">current</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Math</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">max</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">current</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// extend, or start fresh here</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">best</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Math</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">max</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">best</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">current</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">best</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>O(n) time, O(1) space. Initialise from <code>nums[0]</code>, not from 0 — starting at 0 silently returns the wrong answer for an all-negative array, which is the edge case they check.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q29</span><span class="qtext">First repeating (and first non-repeating) character.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Character</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">firstRepeating</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">Set</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Character</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">seen</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">HashSet</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">char</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toCharArray</span><span class="p">())</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="o">!</span><span class="n">seen</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">))</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// add returns false on duplicate</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>

<span class="n">Character</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">firstNonRepeating</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Character</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Long</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">chars</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">mapToObj</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">char</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Function</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">identity</span><span class="p">(),</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedHashMap</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">counting</span><span class="p">()));</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">entrySet</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">e</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">e</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getValue</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">map</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Map</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">Entry</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">getKey</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">findFirst</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">orElse</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>LinkedHashMap::new</code> is the detail that matters — a plain <code>groupingBy</code> gives a HashMap and loses encounter order, so "first" becomes arbitrary.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q30</span><span class="qtext">Print all missing numbers in a range.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">missing</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">lo</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">hi</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="n">Set</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">present</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">boxed</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toSet</span><span class="p">());</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">IntStream</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">rangeClosed</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">lo</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">hi</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">!</span><span class="n">present</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">contains</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">)).</span><span class="na">boxed</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">toList</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>O(n + range). If the range is 1..n and the array is the same length, the follow-up is the O(1)-space version: negate the value at index <code>|v|-1</code> to mark presence, then report the indices still positive.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q31</span><span class="qtext">Bucket integers into ranges 0–9, 10–19, … using Java 8.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">buckets</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">nums</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">boxed</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">collect</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">groupingBy</span><span class="p">(</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">n</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">lo</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Math</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">floorDiv</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">10</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">*</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">10</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">lo</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"-"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">lo</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">9</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">},</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">TreeMap</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="k">new</span><span class="p">,</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">Collectors</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toList</span><span class="p">()));</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>floorDiv</code> rather than <code>/</code> so negatives bucket correctly; <code>TreeMap::new</code> so the buckets come out in order rather than in HashMap order. Both are the details an interviewer is watching for.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q32</span><span class="qtext">Distinct words starting with <code>#</code>, across sentences, using streams.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">tags</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">sentences</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">flatMap</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">split</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"\\s+"</span><span class="p">)))</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">map</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">w</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">w</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">replaceAll</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"[^\\w#]"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">""</span><span class="p">))</span><span class="w">     </span><span class="c1">// strip trailing punctuation like "sentence."</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">w</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">w</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">startsWith</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"#"</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&amp;&amp;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">w</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// excludes a bare "#"</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">distinct</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toList</span><span class="p">();</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>flatMap</code> is the point of the exercise — one sentence produces many words. The cleanup and the <code>length() &gt; 1</code> guard handle the trailing full stop and the lone <code>#</code> that the sample input deliberately contains.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q33</span><span class="qtext">Average grade, then filter students below it.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">double</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">average</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">students</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">mapToDouble</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Student</span><span class="p">::</span><span class="n">grade</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">average</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">orElse</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">0.0</span><span class="p">);</span>

<span class="n">List</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Student</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">belowAverage</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">students</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">()</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">s</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">s</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">grade</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">average</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">toList</span><span class="p">();</span>
</code></pre></div><p>It has to be two passes — the filter depends on a value derived from the whole stream, and a stream can't be consumed twice. Say that explicitly; trying to do it in one pipeline is the trap. If you want the stats in a single traversal, <code>summarizingDouble</code> gives count, sum, min, max and average together.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q34</span><span class="qtext">Task Scheduler (LeetCode 621).</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">leastInterval</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">char</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">tasks</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">n</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="mi">26</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">char</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">tasks</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">t</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="sc">'A'</span><span class="o">]++</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">max</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">max</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">orElse</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kt">long</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">maxCount</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Arrays</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">stream</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">filter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">c</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">max</span><span class="p">).</span><span class="na">count</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="c1">// frame the most frequent task, then fill the gaps</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Math</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">max</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">tasks</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">max</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">*</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">n</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">maxCount</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>The reasoning to narrate: the most frequent task defines the skeleton — <code>max - 1</code> gaps of size <code>n + 1</code> — plus one slot for each task tied at that frequency. If there are enough other tasks to fill the idle slots, no idling is needed, hence the <code>max</code> against <code>tasks.length</code>.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q35</span><span class="qtext">Design a popularity counter: <code>incCounter</code>, <code>decCounter</code>, <code>getMostPopularContent</code>.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Start with the simple version and let them push you.</p><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">PopularityCounter</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Map</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">HashMap</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">final</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">TreeMap</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Integer</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedHashSet</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="o">&gt;&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byCount</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">TreeMap</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">();</span>

<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">incCounter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">move</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">decCounter</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">move</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>

<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">public</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">getMostPopularContent</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">top</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byCount</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">lastEntry</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w">                       </span><span class="c1">// O(log n)</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">top</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kc">null</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">top</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getValue</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">iterator</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">next</span><span class="p">();</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>

<span class="w">    </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">move</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">delta</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">old</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getOrDefault</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">now</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">old</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">delta</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">old</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">!=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="kd">var</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">bucket</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byCount</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">get</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">old</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="n">bucket</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">remove</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">bucket</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">isEmpty</span><span class="p">())</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">byCount</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">remove</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">old</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">if</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">now</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">remove</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">);</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">counts</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">put</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">now</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">byCount</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">computeIfAbsent</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">now</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">k</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">LinkedHashSet</span><span class="o">&lt;&gt;</span><span class="p">()).</span><span class="na">add</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">uid</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p><code>inc</code>/<code>dec</code> are O(log n), <code>getMostPopular</code> is O(log n). The follow-up is "make it O(1)" — the answer is the LFU-cache structure: a doubly linked list of frequency buckets, each holding a set of keys, so incrementing moves a key to the adjacent bucket in O(1) and the max is always the tail. Mention thread safety too: if it's shared, either synchronise <code>move</code> or accept approximate reads.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q36</span><span class="qtext">Longest common subsequence.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">lcs</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[][]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dp</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="k">new</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="o">][</span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">++</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="k">for</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="kt">int</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">&lt;=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">();</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">j</span><span class="o">++</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">            </span><span class="n">dp</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">][</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">charAt</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">==</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">charAt</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="w">                </span><span class="o">?</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dp</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="o">][</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">+</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span>
<span class="w">                </span><span class="p">:</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">Math</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">max</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">dp</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="o">][</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dp</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">i</span><span class="o">][</span><span class="n">j</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="k">return</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">dp</span><span class="o">[</span><span class="n">a</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="o">][</span><span class="n">b</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">length</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="o">]</span><span class="p">;</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>O(m·n) time and space; mention you can reduce to two rows if only the length is needed.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q37</span><span class="qtext">Write a unit test using <code>@Spy</code> for a service that calls an autowired collaborator.</span></p><div class="a"><div class="hl"><pre><span></span><code><span class="nd">@ExtendWith</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">MockitoExtension</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">)</span>
<span class="kd">class</span> <span class="nc">StudentServiceTest</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>

<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Spy</span><span class="w">  </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">TeacherService</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">teacherService</span><span class="p">;</span><span class="w">   </span><span class="c1">// real object, partially overridable</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@InjectMocks</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="kd">private</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">StudentService</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">studentService</span><span class="p">;</span>

<span class="w">    </span><span class="nd">@Test</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="kt">void</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nf">registersTeacherAndDelegatesToSave</span><span class="p">()</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">{</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">String</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">result</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">studentService</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">registerTeacher</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"Mr. Smith"</span><span class="p">);</span>

<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">assertEquals</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"Teacher Registered"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">result</span><span class="p">);</span>

<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">ArgumentCaptor</span><span class="o">&lt;</span><span class="n">Teacher</span><span class="o">&gt;</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">captor</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">=</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">ArgumentCaptor</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">forClass</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Teacher</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">class</span><span class="p">);</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">verify</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">teacherService</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">times</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)).</span><span class="na">save</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">captor</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">capture</span><span class="p">());</span>
<span class="w">        </span><span class="n">assertEquals</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"Mr. Smith"</span><span class="p">,</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">captor</span><span class="p">.</span><span class="na">getValue</span><span class="p">().</span><span class="na">getName</span><span class="p">());</span>
<span class="w">    </span><span class="p">}</span>
<span class="p">}</span>
</code></pre></div><p>The <code>ArgumentCaptor</code> is what lifts this above the baseline answer — it verifies not just <em>that</em> <code>save</code> was called but that it received the right object. If asked to stub the spy, use <code>doNothing().when(teacherService).save(any())</code>, not <code>when(teacherService.save(...))</code>, since the latter would invoke the real method.</p></div></section><hr/>
<h2 id="p11-4-two-more-you-ll-almost-certainly-be-asked">4. Two more you'll almost certainly be asked<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p11-4-two-more-you-ll-almost-certainly-be-asked">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q11-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q38</span><span class="qtext">Explain the architecture of your current project.</span></p><div class="a"><p>There's no generic answer — this is the one you must build yourself, and it's reported as the opener of the techno-managerial round. Use the four-move structure in Part 09 §2, be able to <strong>draw it from memory</strong>, and for every box on the diagram be ready for "how does that actually work internally". Part 10 §13 lists the five follow-ups to rehearse per component.</p></div></section><section class="qa" id="q11-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q39</span><span class="qtext">Tell me about two challenging features you implemented or fixed.</span></p><div class="a"><p>Reported verbatim as a prompt. Prepare two, each about 90 seconds: the constraint that made it hard, the options you weighed, what you chose and what you gave up, and the measured outcome. One should be a performance or correctness problem with before-and-after numbers; the other should involve a design decision where reasonable people could have disagreed.</p></div></section><hr/>
<p><em>Sources for the questions are listed in Part 10 §16. The answers here are written for this kit, not reproduced from those reports.</em></p></div></section>
