<section class="qa" id="q12-1"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q1</span><span class="qtext">Who is actually going to interview me?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Short answer</strong> — on public evidence the named person is Entain India's <strong>tech recruiter</strong>, not an engineer. Treat the name on the invite as the <em>organiser</em> and prepare as if an unnamed Entain engineer will run the technical hour.</p>
<ul>
<li>A search for that name returns one Entain India profile: <strong>Vamsi Krishna Kumar</strong>, LinkedIn vanity handle <code>vamsitechrecruiter</code>, Hyderabad, described as a Talent Acquisition Specialist leading the end-to-end recruitment lifecycle for tech roles. Candidate recommendations there praise his communication and transparency during hiring.</li>
<li>The invitation is <em>signed</em> "Thanks, Boddu — Entain India Group Resourcing". The same human both scheduled the call and appears in the Interviewer field — the classic signature of a recruiter-populated calendar template.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>So plan for one of two shapes:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>He hosts, an engineer interviews</strong> (most likely). He opens, introduces the panel, maybe takes notes, and hands over. The engineer's name appears only when they join.</li>
<li><strong>He runs a structured screen</strong> from a question sheet written by the team. You'd notice fast: questions in a fixed order, shallow follow-ups, and he writes while you talk.</li>
</ol>
<p class="bonus"><strong>How to use this</strong> — in shape 2, <em>do not</em> go deep unprompted. Answer crisply, then offer the depth: "the short answer is X — happy to go into how G1 actually decides that, if it's useful." In shape 1, take the depth without being asked. The first ninety seconds tell you which room you're in.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Don't over-index on this.</strong> It's an inference from a public profile, not a confirmed fact. Prepare for a full engineering interview either way — the cost of being over-prepared is zero.</p>
