<h2 id="p12-2-entain-in-ninety-seconds">2. Entain in ninety seconds<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-2-entain-in-ninety-seconds">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">You won't be quizzed on this. But one accurate, specific sentence about the business — dropped naturally when you ask a question — separates you from the candidate who Googled the logo that morning.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>What it is</strong> — Entain plc is one of the world's largest sports betting and gaming groups, listed on the London Stock Exchange. CEO is <strong>Stella David</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Brands</strong> — Ladbrokes, Coral, bwin, Sportingbet, Eurobet, Crystalbet, PartyPoker, PartyCasino, Ladbrokes Australia / Neds, and BetMGM in the US.</li>
<li><strong>BetMGM</strong> — a 50/50 joint venture with MGM Resorts, <strong>powered by Entain's technology stack</strong>. Q1 2026 revenue ≈ <strong>US$696m, up 6% year on year</strong>, EBITDA ≈ US$25m. FY26 guidance: net revenue US$2.9–3.1bn, adjusted EBITDA toward the lower end of US$300–350m.</li>
<li><strong>Group trading</strong> — Q1 2026 net gaming revenue up ~3% at constant currency; group reiterated FY26 online NGR growth guidance of <strong>5–7%</strong> and confidence in at least <strong>£500m of annual adjusted cashflow by 2028</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>Pressure points</strong> — regulatory remediation across many jurisdictions, and the <strong>UK Remote Gaming Duty increase from April 2026</strong>. That's why cost discipline and platform consolidation matter to their engineering roadmap.</li>
<li><strong>The moat is the platform</strong> — a proprietary global stack: sportsbook, casino, <strong>wallet, payments</strong>, CRM, retail EPOS and self-service betting terminals, with third-party game content (Pragmatic Play, Light &amp; Wonder, IGT, Push Gaming) integrated on top.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Say "gaming", not "gambling".</strong> Internally and externally it's a sports betting and gaming group. And if responsible gambling comes up, take it seriously — it's a genuine engineering concern there (deposit limits, self-exclusion, affordability checks), not a compliance footnote.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-3-entain-india">3. Entain India — where you'd actually sit<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-3-entain-india">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Entain India</strong> is the group's engineering and delivery centre, <strong>established in Hyderabad in 2001</strong>, with a second site in <strong>Pune</strong>. One of Entain's largest technology hubs.</li>
<li><strong>The Ivy Comptech question — get the mechanism right, because "Ivy was renamed Entain" isn't quite it.</strong> The chain is: <strong>Ivy Comptech</strong> (Hyderabad) was the technology arm of PartyGaming, which merged into <strong>bwin.party</strong>. <strong>GVC Holdings acquired bwin.party in 2016</strong>, so Ivy came with it. <strong>GVC then renamed itself Entain in December 2020.</strong> The Hyderabad operation now trades as <strong>Entain India</strong>, while <em>Ivy Comptech Pvt Ltd</em> survives as the legal entity and on plenty of paperwork. So: the <em>parent</em> was renamed (GVC → Entain) and the Hyderabad arm was <em>rebranded</em> to match — it wasn't a company called Ivy deciding to call itself Entain.</li>
<li>Practical consequence: if you see "Ivy" on job boards, on a Glassdoor page, or an <code>@ivycomptech.com</code> address, it's the same organisation — so <strong>Ivy Comptech interview reports are Entain India interview reports</strong>, and worth reading as such. Knowing the lineage is a small, real signal that you did your homework; don't over-claim the detail if they push on dates.</li>
<li>Ivy / Entain India's platform work spans <strong>15+ brands</strong>: end-to-end online gaming, <strong>transaction processing</strong>, real-time CRM and data intelligence.</li>
<li>Disciplines hiring there: Engineering, Product, Design, <strong>Data &amp; AI</strong>, Quality Engineering.</li>
<li><strong>Stack signals from public Entain India postings</strong> — Java, Spring Boot, REST; Spring Security with OAuth2 / JWT; React or Angular on full-stack roles; AWS/Azure cloud-native; performance tuning and observability. There's also a parallel <strong>.NET/C#</strong> estate (separate SDE II (.NET) postings exist), which is why some Glassdoor reports mention C# questions. <strong>Kafka and Redis</strong> appear in reported technical rounds.</li>
</ul>
