<h3 id="p12-3-2-what-employees-say">3.2 What employees say — read this before you accept anything</h3>
<p>Glassdoor ratings from Hyderabad employees: work-life balance <strong>3.9</strong>, culture &amp; values <strong>3.7</strong>, diversity &amp; inclusion <strong>3.6</strong>, <strong>career opportunities 3.3</strong>. <strong>65%</strong> would recommend it to a friend — that's middling, not bad.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistent positives</strong> — genuine work-life balance, flexible hybrid model, good learning curve, solid benefits and transport.</li>
<li><strong>Consistent negatives, and they're specific</strong> — <em>"frequent organizational restructuring and layoffs"</em>, <em>"repeated team restructures"</em>, <em>"frequent pause in promotion cycle"</em>, <em>"increasing levels of micromanagement in some teams"</em>, and several reviews naming <strong>low job security</strong> directly.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Don't raise this in the interview, and don't treat it as disqualifying</strong> — every large listed company with a cost programme collects reviews like these, and the Remote Gaming Duty rise from April 2026 (§2) explains a lot of the cost pressure. But it <em>is</em> the thing to diligence before you sign, and you can probe it without ever using the word "layoffs":</p>
<ul>
<li>"How long has this team existed in its current shape, and how has it changed over the last year?"</li>
<li>"Is this role backfilling someone or is it net new headcount?"</li>
<li>"What does the promotion cycle look like, and when did this team last promote someone?"</li>
</ul>
<p>Those are normal, reasonable questions. The <em>answers</em> — and how comfortably they're given — tell you what the reviews are pointing at. Ask them of the recruiter, not in the technical round.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Use it like this</strong> — "I read that the India centre goes back to the Ivy Comptech days and now covers wallet and transaction processing across the brands. Is this team on the sportsbook side or the platform/wallet side?" That one question demonstrates research and gets you information you actually want.</p>
