<p class="bonus"><strong>Your instinct about memory management is worth following</strong> — not because it tops the reported list, but because it's the topic where a 5-year engineer most visibly out-classes the SDE II bar. "Java Concepts" as a named topic, asked by a platform company running a high-throughput wallet and sportsbook, very plausibly means heap, GC and leaks. §6 is built for that. Just don't let it crowd out collections and concurrency, which the reports name explicitly.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-6-memory-management">6. Java Concepts I — Memory management, end to end<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-6-memory-management">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">The section you asked for. Twenty-four questions that take you from "what's in the JVM's memory" to "here's how I found and fixed a leak in production". If you only rehearse one thing on Wednesday, make it <strong>Q3, Q7, Q11, Q17 and Q22</strong> — those five carry the rest.</p>
<section class="qa" id="q12-3"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q3</span><span class="qtext">Walk me through the JVM memory model — what areas exist and what lives where?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Lead with the split</strong> — "The JVM divides memory into areas that are <em>shared across all threads</em> and areas that are <em>per-thread</em>. That split is the thing that explains most of the behaviour."</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Scope</th><th>What's in it</th><th>Failure</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Heap</strong></td><td>Shared</td><td>Every object instance and every array. Divided into Young (Eden + 2 Survivor spaces) and Old/Tenured.</td><td><code>OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Metaspace</strong></td><td>Shared</td><td>Class metadata — the runtime representation of loaded classes, method bytecode, the runtime constant pool. <strong>Native memory since Java 8</strong>, not heap.</td><td><code>OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>JVM Stack</strong></td><td>Per thread</td><td>One frame per method call: local variables, operand stack, return address. Frames pop on return.</td><td><code>StackOverflowError</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>PC register</strong></td><td>Per thread</td><td>Address of the currently executing JVM instruction.</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Native method stack</strong></td><td>Per thread</td><td>Frames for JNI / native calls.</td><td><code>StackOverflowError</code> / native OOM</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Code cache</strong></td><td>Shared</td><td>JIT-compiled native code.</td><td>Code cache full → JIT falls back to interpreter, throughput drops</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The senior addition</strong> — "a common trap is thinking <code>-Xmx</code> bounds the process. It bounds the heap only. Metaspace, thread stacks (<code>-Xss</code> × thread count), the code cache, direct <code>ByteBuffer</code>s and GC's own structures are all outside it. That's why a container with <code>-Xmx2g</code> and a 2 GB memory limit gets OOM-killed by the kernel — the RSS is heap plus all of that."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-4"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q4</span><span class="qtext">Stack vs heap — what actually goes where?</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li><strong>Stack</strong> — primitives declared as locals, and <strong>references</strong> to objects. Per-thread, so inherently thread-safe. Allocation is a pointer bump; deallocation is automatic when the frame pops. Fast and LIFO. Sized with <code>-Xss</code> (commonly 512 KB–1 MB per thread).</li>
<li><strong>Heap</strong> — every <code>new</code>, every array, every boxed value, every lambda capture that escapes. Shared, so it needs synchronisation and a garbage collector.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>void demo() {
    int x = 42;                 // x itself: stack
    int[] a = new int[10];      // 'a' (reference): stack. The array: heap.
    Point p = new Point(1, 2);  // 'p': stack. Point object: heap.
}                               // frame pops; array and Point become unreachable
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The follow-up they hope you miss</strong> — "an object's <em>fields</em> live on the heap even if they're primitives, because they're part of the object. Only <em>local</em> primitives sit on the stack." And: <strong>escape analysis</strong> means the JIT may prove an object never escapes the method and <em>scalar-replace</em> it — effectively stack-allocating it. So "objects are always on the heap" is true of the spec, not always of the running machine.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-5"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q5</span><span class="qtext">Is Java pass-by-value or pass-by-reference?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Always pass-by-value.</strong> For objects, the <em>value being copied is the reference</em>. That's the whole answer — but say the second half, because "pass by reference" is the wrong-answer trap.</p>
<pre><code>void mutate(StringBuilder sb) { sb.append("x"); }  // caller SEES this
void reassign(StringBuilder sb) { sb = new StringBuilder("y"); } // caller does NOT
</code></pre>
<p>In <code>mutate</code>, the copied reference points at the same heap object, so mutation is visible. In <code>reassign</code>, you repoint the <em>local copy</em>; the caller's reference is untouched.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Closing line</strong> — "if Java were pass-by-reference, a classic <code>swap(a, b)</code> would work. It can't be written in Java. That's the proof."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-6"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q6</span><span class="qtext">Explain the generational heap and why it exists.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>The weak generational hypothesis</strong> — the overwhelming majority of objects die young. So instead of scanning the whole heap, collect the young part often and cheaply, and the old part rarely.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Eden</strong> — where <code>new</code> allocates (via a thread-local allocation buffer, so it's a pointer bump with no lock).</li>
<li><strong>Survivor S0 / S1</strong> — a Minor GC copies the live objects out of Eden into the empty survivor space; Eden is then wholesale empty. Objects ping-pong between S0 and S1, aging by one each cycle.</li>
<li><strong>Old / Tenured</strong> — once an object survives <code>MaxTenuringThreshold</code> cycles (default 15 on HotSpot), it's <strong>promoted</strong>. Large objects can be allocated straight here.</li>
</ul>
<p>The payoff: Minor GC is a <strong>copying collector</strong> over live objects only. If 98% of Eden is garbage, the cost is proportional to the 2% that survives, not to the size of Eden. Dead objects cost nothing to collect.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Premature promotion</strong> is the pathology to name. If survivor spaces are too small, objects that <em>would</em> have died get promoted to Old, and now only a Major GC can reclaim them. Symptom: Old gen growing steadily under steady load, with frequent full GCs. That's a sizing bug, not a leak.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-7"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q7</span><span class="qtext">How does the garbage collector decide an object is garbage?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Reachability from GC roots</strong> — not reference counting. An object is garbage if there is no path to it from any root.</p>
<p><strong>GC roots are:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Local variables and parameters in every live thread's stack frames</li>
<li><code>static</code> fields of loaded classes</li>
<li>Active Java threads themselves</li>
<li>JNI global and local references</li>
<li>Objects held as monitors (currently synchronized on)</li>
<li>Classes loaded by the bootstrap classloader</li>
</ul>
<p>The collector does a mark phase from those roots, then reclaims everything unmarked.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Why this matters practically</strong> — "reference counting can't collect cycles; reachability can. Two objects pointing at each other with nothing else pointing at them are both unreachable, so both are collected. That's why a doubly-linked list or a parent↔child object graph isn't a leak in Java, but would be in a naive refcounted runtime."</p>
<p class="gotcha">And the corollary you should volunteer: <strong>"a memory leak in Java is never about forgetting to free. It's about accidentally staying reachable."</strong> That one sentence sets up every leak question in this section.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-8"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q8</span><span class="qtext">Minor GC vs Major GC vs Full GC?</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Collects</th><th>Cost</th><th>Stop-the-world?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Minor / Young GC</strong></td><td>Young gen only</td><td>Milliseconds</td><td>Yes, but short</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Major GC</strong></td><td>Old gen</td><td>Much slower — Old is big and objects there are mostly live</td><td>Partly, depending on collector</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Full GC</strong></td><td>Young + Old + Metaspace, usually with compaction</td><td>Can be seconds on a large heap</td><td>Yes, fully</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Frequent Full GCs are the alarm bell.</strong> They mean one of: a real leak; Old gen undersized; premature promotion; Metaspace pressure from classloader churn; or someone calling <code>System.gc()</code>. Under a betting workload, a multi-second Full GC during a live in-play market is a customer-visible outage — which is exactly why this topic is worth knowing at a company like Entain.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-9"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q9</span><span class="qtext">Which garbage collectors do you know, and when would you pick each?</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Collector</th><th>Shape</th><th>Pick it when</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Serial</strong></td><td>Single-threaded, STW</td><td>Tiny heaps, single-core containers, CLI tools. <code>-XX:+UseSerialGC</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Parallel</strong> (Throughput)</td><td>Multi-threaded STW, compacting</td><td>Batch jobs where total throughput matters and a pause is fine. <code>-XX:+UseParallelGC</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>CMS</strong></td><td>Mostly-concurrent mark-sweep, no compaction</td><td><strong>Deprecated in 9, removed in 14.</strong> Know it exists and that fragmentation eventually forced a Full GC.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>G1</strong> (Garbage-First)</td><td>Region-based, concurrent marking, incremental compaction, pause-time <em>target</em></td><td><strong>The default since Java 9.</strong> Heaps from a few GB up, latency-sensitive services. <code>-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=200</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>ZGC</strong></td><td>Concurrent, coloured pointers + load barriers, region-based</td><td>Sub-millisecond pauses, heaps from small to multi-terabyte. Generational since 21. <code>-XX:+UseZGC</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shenandoah</strong></td><td>Concurrent compaction via Brooks forwarding pointers</td><td>Same niche as ZGC; pause time independent of heap size. <code>-XX:+UseShenandoahGC</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Epsilon</strong></td><td>No-op — allocates, never collects</td><td>Benchmarking and allocation-profiling only.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="bonus"><strong>G1 in one paragraph, because they'll ask</strong> — "G1 splits the heap into ~2048 equal regions, each dynamically labelled Eden, Survivor, Old or Humongous. It marks concurrently, then collects the regions with the <em>most garbage first</em> — that's the name. Because it evacuates region by region, it compacts incrementally and it can hit a pause-time target by collecting only as many regions as fit in the budget."</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The honest trade-off to state</strong> — "ZGC and Shenandoah buy you low pause at the cost of throughput and extra memory for barriers. G1 is the sensible default; I'd only move to ZGC if GC-log evidence showed G1 pauses breaching our latency SLO, not on principle."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-10"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q10</span><span class="qtext">What is a stop-the-world pause, and what's a safepoint?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>A <strong>STW pause</strong> is a window where all application threads are suspended so the GC can work on a stable object graph. To suspend them, the JVM brings every thread to a <strong>safepoint</strong> — a known instruction boundary (method returns, loop back-edges, allocation sites) where the thread's stack can be walked accurately.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The senior detail: time-to-safepoint.</strong> A long counted loop with an <code>int</code> counter may be compiled <em>without</em> a safepoint poll, so that thread can't be stopped and <em>every other thread waits for it</em>. You then see a long pause that GC logs attribute to GC but that GC didn't cause. Diagnose with <code>-XX:+PrintSafepointStatistics</code> (older) or <code>-Xlog:safepoint</code> (JDK 9+). Fix: use a <code>long</code> counter, or break the loop up.</p>
<p class="bonus">Also worth naming: safepoints are used for more than GC — biased-lock revocation, deoptimisation, thread dumps and some JFR operations all use them.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-11"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q11</span><span class="qtext">What causes a memory leak in Java, if the GC reclaims everything unreachable?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>A Java leak is unintended reachability</strong> — objects you're finished with, still on a path from a GC root. The GC is working perfectly; you've just told it, accidentally, that you still care.</p>
<p><strong>The canonical causes — know all of these, name three unprompted:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Static collections that only grow.</strong> A <code>static Map</code> used as a cache with no eviction is a leak with a nice name. Statics are GC roots for the life of the classloader.</li>
<li><strong>Unregistered listeners / callbacks.</strong> You add an observer and never remove it; the subject holds it forever.</li>
<li><strong>ThreadLocal in a pooled thread.</strong> The value is reachable from the thread, and pool threads never die. See Q12.</li>
<li><strong>Unclosed resources.</strong> Streams, connections, <code>ResultSet</code>s — each holds native buffers and often a chain of references.</li>
<li><strong>Inner-class / lambda capture.</strong> A non-static inner class holds an implicit reference to its enclosing instance. A long-lived callback then pins a whole heavyweight object.</li>
<li><strong>Mutated keys in a HashMap.</strong> Mutate a field used in <code>hashCode</code> after insertion and the entry becomes unreachable <em>to you</em> but perfectly reachable to the GC. Unremovable garbage.</li>
<li><strong>ClassLoader leaks</strong> in app servers on redeploy — one lingering static reference into old classes keeps an entire classloader, and every class it loaded, alive in Metaspace.</li>
<li><strong>Interned strings and off-heap buffers</strong> — <code>String.intern()</code> on unbounded user input; <code>DirectByteBuffer</code> whose owner is retained.</li>
</ol>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Have one story ready.</strong> This is the question where a real production anecdote is worth more than the list. Shape: symptom (Old gen sawtooth that never returned to baseline) → tool (heap dump, MAT dominator tree) → culprit (the actual retaining path) → fix → what you changed so it couldn't recur (a bounded cache, an alert on Old gen after GC).</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-12"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q12</span><span class="qtext">Why is ThreadLocal a classic leak in a thread pool?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Each <code>Thread</code> owns a <code>ThreadLocalMap</code>. Its <strong>keys are weak references</strong> to the <code>ThreadLocal</code> object, but its <strong>values are strong</strong>. So when the <code>ThreadLocal</code> itself becomes unreachable, the key clears — but the <em>value</em> stays strongly held by the map until that slot is next touched.</p>
<p>In a <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> or a Tomcat request thread, the thread is recycled, never destroyed. So the value survives across unrelated requests — a leak, and in a multi-tenant system a <strong>correctness and security problem</strong> too: request B can read request A's context.</p>
<pre><code>try {
    CONTEXT.set(userContext);
    handle(request);
} finally {
    CONTEXT.remove();   // not set(null) — remove() clears the entry
}
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha"><code>set(null)</code> leaves a live entry holding a null value; <code>remove()</code> deletes the entry. Always <code>remove()</code>, always in a <code>finally</code>.</p>
<p class="bonus">If they're on Java 21: <strong><code>ScopedValue</code></strong> (preview) is the modern answer — immutable, explicitly scoped by <code>ScopedValue.where(...).run(...)</code>, so it can't leak past its block and works correctly with virtual threads.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-13"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q13</span><span class="qtext">Explain strong, soft, weak and phantom references.</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Collected when</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Strong</strong> — an ordinary reference</td><td>Never, while reachable</td><td>Everything normal</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Soft</strong> — <code>SoftReference</code></td><td>Only under memory pressure, before OOM</td><td>Memory-sensitive caches</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Weak</strong> — <code>WeakReference</code></td><td>At the next GC, once no strong refs remain</td><td>Canonicalising maps, metadata keyed by object identity</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Phantom</strong> — <code>PhantomReference</code></td><td><code>get()</code> always returns null; enqueued <em>after</em> finalization</td><td>Deterministic post-mortem cleanup of native resources</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><code>WeakHashMap</code> holds its <em>keys</em> weakly, so entries disappear when the key is collected. The classic gotcha: <strong>if the value strongly references the key, the entry never clears</strong> — the value keeps the key alive, defeating the point.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>On <code>SoftReference</code> caches</strong>, the mature opinion is worth stating: "in practice I'd rather use Caffeine or Guava with an explicit size and TTL. Soft references hand your eviction policy to the GC, they delay collection so they extend GC pause times, and behaviour differs between collectors. An explicit bounded cache is predictable; a soft cache is a promise the JVM makes on its own terms."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-14"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q14</span><span class="qtext">What does finalize() do, and what should you use instead?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><code>finalize()</code> was called by the GC before reclaiming an object. It is <strong>deprecated since Java 9 and removed from use in Java 18+</strong>, and you should never write one. Why it's broken:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No guarantee it ever runs</strong>, and no guarantee of when.</li>
<li>It makes objects survive <strong>at least two GC cycles</strong> — one to discover finalization is needed, one to actually collect — so it directly worsens GC.</li>
<li>Finalization runs on a single unbounded-queue daemon thread; if it blocks, objects pile up until OOM.</li>
<li>A finalizer can <strong>resurrect</strong> the object by storing <code>this</code> somewhere reachable.</li>
<li>Exceptions in a finalizer are swallowed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Use instead:</strong> <code>try-with-resources</code> and <code>AutoCloseable</code> for anything deterministic — that's the answer 95% of the time. For a genuine native-resource safety net, <code>java.lang.ref.Cleaner</code> with a cleanup action that <strong>doesn't capture the object</strong> (capture it and the object is never collected, so the cleaner never runs).</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-15"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q15</span><span class="qtext">Where does the String pool live, and how does intern() work?</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li>String literals are interned automatically at class load. Since <strong>Java 7 the pool moved from PermGen to the heap</strong>, which means interned strings are now ordinary garbage-collectable objects.</li>
<li><code>s.intern()</code> returns the canonical pooled instance, adding <code>s</code> to the pool if absent.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>String a = "bet";                    // pooled literal
String b = "bet";                    // same object
String c = new String("bet");        // new heap object
a == b;            // true
a == c;            // false
a == c.intern();   // true
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Never intern unbounded user input.</strong> Interning every incoming bet ID or session token grows the pool without limit — a leak with a hash-table performance cliff attached. And the pool is sized by <code>-XX:StringTableSize</code>; a too-small table with millions of entries turns O(1) lookups into long bucket walks.</p>
<p class="bonus">Compile-time constant folding is a good detail: <code>"be" + "t"</code> is folded to the literal <code>"bet"</code> at compile time and pooled, but <code>"be" + variable</code> builds a new object at runtime.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-16"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q16</span><span class="qtext">PermGen vs Metaspace — what changed in Java 8?</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>PermGen (≤7)</th><th>Metaspace (8+)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Location</td><td>Part of the heap</td><td><strong>Native memory</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Sizing</td><td><code>-XX:MaxPermSize</code>, fixed, notoriously hard to tune</td><td>Grows on demand; bounded by <code>-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize</code>, unlimited by default</td></tr>
<tr><td>Held</td><td>Class metadata, static fields, interned strings</td><td>Class metadata only — statics and the string pool moved to the heap in 7</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reclaim</td><td>Full GC only</td><td>Per-classloader — metadata is freed when its classloader is collected</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>"Unlimited by default" is the trap.</strong> A classloader leak — hot redeploys, heavy dynamic proxy or bytecode generation, a framework creating a classloader per request — will now consume native memory until the <em>container</em> is OOM-killed, with no <code>OutOfMemoryError</code> to point at. Always set <code>-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize</code> in production so you get a Java-level error you can diagnose instead of an opaque exit 137.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-17"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q17</span><span class="qtext">Name the OutOfMemoryError variants and what each one actually tells you.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">This is the question that separates people who've read about GC from people who've been paged by it. Know the <em>message text</em>, because the message is the diagnosis.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Message</th><th>Means</th><th>First move</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>Java heap space</code></td><td>Heap full and GC can't free enough</td><td>Heap dump → dominator tree. Leak or genuinely undersized?</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>GC overhead limit exceeded</code></td><td>&gt;98% of time in GC, &lt;2% heap recovered</td><td>Almost always a leak, caught slightly earlier than the above</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Metaspace</code></td><td>Class metadata exhausted</td><td>Classloader leak, or genuine class explosion from proxies/codegen</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>unable to create new native thread</code></td><td>OS refused a thread — ulimit or native memory</td><td>Count threads. Usually an unbounded pool or a leaked executor</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Requested array size exceeds VM limit</code></td><td>Array larger than <code>Integer.MAX_VALUE</code>-ish</td><td>A bug — an unbounded read of an untrusted length</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Direct buffer memory</code></td><td>Off-heap NIO limit hit</td><td><code>-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize</code>; look for retained <code>DirectByteBuffer</code>s (Netty, NIO)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The production line to add</strong> — "I always run with <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=…</code> on a mounted volume. An OOM you didn't capture a dump for is an OOM you get to have twice."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-18"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q18</span><span class="qtext">StackOverflowError vs OutOfMemoryError?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong><code>StackOverflowError</code></strong> — a single thread's stack exceeded <code>-Xss</code>. Cause is almost always unbounded recursion, mutual recursion, or a cyclic <code>toString()</code>/<code>equals()</code>. Fix the recursion; raising <code>-Xss</code> just moves the failure.</p>
<p><strong><code>OutOfMemoryError</code></strong> — a shared memory area is exhausted; the whole JVM is affected.</p>
<p class="gotcha">Both extend <code>Error</code>, not <code>Exception</code>. <strong>Don't catch them</strong> to "keep going" — after an OOM the JVM's state is unreliable and other threads may already have failed mid-operation. The correct response is to dump, exit, and let the orchestrator restart you. Catching <code>StackOverflowError</code> is occasionally defensible at a parser boundary; catching <code>OutOfMemoryError</code> almost never is.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-19"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q19</span><span class="qtext">What are TLABs and escape analysis?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>TLAB — Thread-Local Allocation Buffer.</strong> Each thread gets its own private chunk of Eden. Allocation inside it is a <strong>pointer bump with no synchronisation</strong>, which is why object allocation in Java is often cheaper than <code>malloc</code>. When a thread's TLAB is full it grabs another (that part does synchronise). Objects too large for a TLAB go straight to shared Eden or Old.</p>
<p><strong>Escape analysis.</strong> C2 (and now Graal) proves whether an object can be observed outside its allocating method. If it can't:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Scalar replacement</strong> — the object is dissolved into its fields, held in registers or the stack. It's never allocated at all.</li>
<li><strong>Lock elision</strong> — synchronisation on a non-escaping object is removed entirely.</li>
</ul>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Why to mention it</strong> — "it's the reason micro-optimising away small short-lived objects is usually wasted effort: the JIT often removes them for me. I'd rather write clear code and let profiling tell me where allocation actually hurts." That's the right seniority signal: you know the mechanism <em>and</em> when not to act on it.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-20"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q20</span><span class="qtext">What is the Java Memory Model, and what does happens-before mean?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">The JMM is the contract that says <strong>when a write by one thread is guaranteed visible to a read by another</strong>. Without it, the compiler, the JIT and the CPU are all free to reorder and cache — and each of them does.</p>
<p><strong>happens-before</strong> is the ordering relation. If A happens-before B, A's effects are visible to B. The edges you should be able to list:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Program order</strong> within a single thread.</li>
<li><strong>Monitor lock</strong> — unlocking a monitor happens-before any later lock of the same monitor.</li>
<li><strong>Volatile</strong> — a write to a volatile happens-before every later read of it.</li>
<li><strong>Thread start</strong> — <code>Thread.start()</code> happens-before anything in that thread.</li>
<li><strong>Thread join</strong> — everything in a thread happens-before another thread's successful <code>join()</code> on it.</li>
<li><strong>Final fields</strong> — correctly constructed final fields are visible without synchronisation.</li>
<li><strong>Transitivity</strong> — A hb B and B hb C implies A hb C.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The point to land:</strong> "<code>volatile</code> gives visibility and ordering, but <em>not</em> atomicity. <code>volatile int count; count++</code> is still a broken read-modify-write. For that I need <code>AtomicInteger</code>, a lock, or an <code>LongAdder</code> under contention." Reported Entain rounds ask multithreading in two separate stages — this is the line that shows you understand it rather than remember it.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-21"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q21</span><span class="qtext">Why is double-checked locking broken without volatile?</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>private static volatile Singleton instance;   // volatile is load-bearing

static Singleton get() {
    if (instance == null) {                    // no lock on the hot path
        synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            if (instance == null) {
                instance = new Singleton();
            }
        }
    }
    return instance;
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>instance = new Singleton()</code> is three steps: allocate, run the constructor, assign the reference. <strong>Without <code>volatile</code>, steps 2 and 3 can be reordered.</strong> Another thread then sees a non-null reference to a partially constructed object and uses it. <code>volatile</code> forbids that reordering and publishes the constructor's writes.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The better answers to offer</strong> — an <code>enum</code> singleton (serialization- and reflection-safe, initialised by the classloader), or the <strong>holder idiom</strong>: a private static nested class holding the instance, which the JVM initialises lazily and thread-safely with no synchronisation cost at all. If you're on Spring, the right answer is usually "the container manages the lifecycle; I don't hand-roll singletons."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-22"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q22</span><span class="qtext">Production is throwing OOM. Walk me through what you do.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">This is the highest-value question in this section, because it's the one where you sound like someone who has actually been on call. Answer it as a <strong>sequence</strong>, not a list of tools.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stabilise first.</strong> Is it one pod or all of them? Roll back if a deploy correlates. Customers first, root cause second.</li>
<li><strong>Read the error text.</strong> The variant (Q17) already narrows it to heap, Metaspace, threads, or direct memory.</li>
<li><strong>Look at the GC log before the dump.</strong> <code>-Xlog:gc*:file=gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags</code>. The signature you want is <strong>Old-gen occupancy <em>after</em> each Full GC</strong>. Sawtooth returning to a flat baseline = healthy, just busy. Baseline creeping upward monotonically = leak.</li>
<li><strong>Get the heap dump.</strong> <code>jcmd &lt;pid&gt; GC.heap_dump /path/dump.hprof</code>, or the one you already captured with <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Open it in Eclipse MAT.</strong> Run the <em>Leak Suspects</em> report, then the <strong>dominator tree</strong> — it shows what's <em>retaining</em> memory, not just what's big. Then "Path to GC Roots, excluding weak/soft references" on the top suspect. That path <em>is</em> the bug.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm the hypothesis in code</strong>, fix, and add a regression guard — a bounded cache, a metric on the collection's size, an alert on post-GC Old-gen occupancy.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Tools to name, with what each is for:</strong> <code>jcmd</code> (the modern front door — <code>VM.native_memory</code>, <code>GC.heap_info</code>, <code>Thread.print</code>), <code>jmap</code>/<code>jstat</code>, <strong>JFR</strong> (<code>-XX:StartFlightRecording</code>) for low-overhead always-on profiling, <strong>JDK Mission Control</strong>, <strong>Eclipse MAT</strong> for dumps, <strong>async-profiler</strong> for allocation flame graphs, and <strong>Native Memory Tracking</strong> (<code>-XX:NativeMemoryTracking=summary</code>) when RSS exceeds heap and you suspect off-heap.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The distinction that earns the mark:</strong> "the first thing I establish is whether it's a leak or a capacity problem, because the fixes are opposite. A leak needs a code change; more heap just delays it and makes the eventual Full GC longer. Post-GC Old-gen occupancy over time is the measurement that tells them apart."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-23"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q23</span><span class="qtext">How do you size a JVM running in a container?</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li>Modern JVMs are <strong>container-aware</strong> (<code>UseContainerSupport</code>, on by default since 10 and backported to 8u191) — they read cgroup limits rather than the host's.</li>
<li>Prefer <strong><code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage</code></strong> (e.g. 70–75) over a hard <code>-Xmx</code>, so the JVM tracks the container limit when it changes.</li>
<li><strong>Leave real headroom.</strong> Heap is not RSS. Budget for Metaspace, thread stacks, code cache, GC structures and direct buffers — typically 25–30% of the limit.</li>
<li>Set <code>-XX:MaxMetaspaceSize</code> explicitly (Q16), and cap <code>-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize</code> if you use Netty or NIO heavily.</li>
<li>Check <code>-XX:ActiveProcessorCount</code> if CPU limits are fractional — GC and <code>ForkJoinPool</code> thread counts derive from it, and getting it wrong sizes your pools wrong.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Exit code 137 is not an OOM you can debug from Java.</strong> It's the kernel OOM-killer, and it leaves no heap dump and no stack trace. If you're seeing 137s, you have a native/RSS budgeting problem, not a heap problem — reach for Native Memory Tracking, not MAT.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-24"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q24</span><span class="qtext">Should you call System.gc()?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>No.</strong> It's a <em>hint</em>, typically triggering a Full GC — the single most expensive thing the JVM does — at a moment you chose arbitrarily and the JVM didn't. In a latency-sensitive service that's a self-inflicted pause.</p>
<p>It can be disabled outright with <code>-XX:+DisableExplicitGC</code>, which many production estates set precisely because a library somewhere calls it.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The nuance that shows depth</strong> — the one legitimate-ish caller is NIO's <code>DirectByteBuffer</code> cleanup path, which historically relied on <code>System.gc()</code> to reclaim off-heap buffers. If you set <code>DisableExplicitGC</code>, pair it with <code>-XX:+ExplicitGCInvokesConcurrent</code> so the request becomes a concurrent cycle instead of being silently dropped.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-25"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q25</span><span class="qtext">What is false sharing, and have you ever hit it?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Two threads write to two <em>different</em> variables that happen to sit in the <strong>same 64-byte cache line</strong>. Nothing is logically shared, but the cache-coherence protocol invalidates the whole line on every write, so the cores ping-pong it and throughput collapses.</p>
<p><strong>Fixes:</strong> pad the fields apart, or annotate with <code>@jdk.internal.vm.annotation.Contended</code> (needs <code>-XX:-RestrictContended</code>). In practice, use the JDK classes that already solve it: <strong><code>LongAdder</code></strong> over <code>AtomicLong</code> under high contention — it keeps per-thread striped cells on separate lines and sums on read.</p>
<p class="gotcha">Say the trade-off: "<code>LongAdder</code> is faster to increment but its <code>sum()</code> isn't atomic against concurrent updates. For a hit counter that's fine. For a wallet balance it isn't — there I want a single source of truth with proper transactional semantics, not a striped counter."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-26"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q26</span><span class="qtext">Do virtual threads change anything about memory?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Yes, substantially — and it's a good thing to raise if the conversation reaches Java 21.</p>
<ul>
<li>A platform thread reserves a <strong>fixed OS stack</strong> (~1 MB of virtual address space). That's why a few thousand threads is a practical ceiling.</li>
<li>A <strong>virtual thread's stack lives on the heap</strong> as a continuation, starts tiny, and grows and shrinks on demand. Millions are viable. The scheduler is a <code>ForkJoinPool</code> of carrier threads.</li>
<li>Consequence: <strong>thread pools stop being the right pattern</strong> for blocking I/O. <code>Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()</code> replaces them; you no longer size a pool to protect the machine, so you move back-pressure to an explicit semaphore or rate limiter where it belongs.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Pinning</strong> is the detail to have ready: a virtual thread inside a <code>synchronized</code> block that blocks will <em>pin</em> its carrier thread, defeating the whole mechanism. Use <code>ReentrantLock</code> instead. (JDK 24 removes most of this limitation — worth saying you know it was addressed.) Also: <strong>don't put <code>ThreadLocal</code>s on virtual threads</strong> — with millions of threads the memory cost is real. That's exactly what <code>ScopedValue</code> (Q12) is for.</p>
</div></section>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-7-collections-concurrency">7. Java Concepts II — Collections &amp; concurrency<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-7-collections-concurrency">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">These two topics are named <em>explicitly</em> in multiple public Entain India reports — collections and threads with the Tech Lead, multithreading again with the Architect. Parts 01, 06 and 08 have the full treatment; this is the Entain-shaped subset, with the answers pitched at the level that wins the round.</p>
<section class="qa" id="q12-27"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q27</span><span class="qtext">Explain how HashMap works internally.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">Array of buckets → hash the key → index → walk the bucket. Everything else is detail on top of that sentence.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hashing</strong> — <code>hash = h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code>, spreading high bits into the low bits, because the index is <code>(n - 1) &amp; hash</code> and <code>n</code> is a power of two (so only the low bits would otherwise matter).</li>
<li><strong>Collisions</strong> — a linked list in the bucket. Since <strong>Java 8</strong>, a bucket with <strong>≥ 8</strong> entries converts to a <strong>red-black tree</strong> (given table size ≥ 64), taking worst case from O(n) to O(log n). It untreeifies below 6.</li>
<li><strong>Resize</strong> — at <code>size &gt; capacity × loadFactor</code> (default 0.75), capacity doubles and entries rehash. In Java 8 an entry either stays at index <code>i</code> or moves to <code>i + oldCap</code>, decided by one bit — no full recomputation.</li>
<li><strong>Nulls</strong> — one null key (bucket 0), many null values.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The senior addition:</strong> "the treeification fix in Java 8 was a response to a real <strong>hash-collision DoS</strong>: an attacker sends crafted keys that all land in one bucket and every lookup degrades to a linear scan. That's a live concern anywhere you build a map keyed on user-supplied strings." At a betting company, that is a security answer as well as a data-structures answer.</p>
<p class="bonus">Sizing: "if I know I'm inserting ~1000 entries I construct with <code>new HashMap&lt;&gt;(1365)</code> — capacity above <code>n / 0.75</code> — to avoid two resizes and their rehashing cost."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-28"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q28</span><span class="qtext">HashMap vs ConcurrentHashMap vs Hashtable vs synchronizedMap?</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Thread-safe?</th><th>How</th><th>Nulls</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>HashMap</code></td><td>No</td><td>—</td><td>1 null key, many null values</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Hashtable</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Every method <code>synchronized</code> — one lock for the whole table. Legacy.</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Collections.synchronizedMap</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Wrapper, one lock for the whole map</td><td>Same as wrapped map</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></td><td>Yes</td><td><strong>Java 8+:</strong> CAS on empty bins, <code>synchronized</code> on the first node of a bin — lock granularity is <em>per bucket</em>. Reads are lock-free.</td><td>None</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The two things they're really probing:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Why no nulls in <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>?</strong> Because <code>map.get(k) == null</code> would be ambiguous — absent, or present-with-null? In a single-threaded map you disambiguate with <code>containsKey</code>; concurrently, the answer can change between the two calls. Doug Lea removed the ambiguity by banning null.</li>
<li><strong>Thread-safe methods ≠ thread-safe code.</strong> <code>if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v);</code> is a race even on a <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>. Use the atomic composites: <code>putIfAbsent</code>, <code>computeIfAbsent</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>compute</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p class="bonus">Java 8 changed the <em>segment</em> story: pre-8 <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> used a fixed array of 16 Segments (each a mini re-entrant-locked table). If you say "it uses segment locking", expect the follow-up — the modern answer is per-bin CAS plus node-level synchronisation.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-29"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q29</span><span class="qtext">Fail-fast vs fail-safe iterators?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>Fail-fast</strong> (<code>ArrayList</code>, <code>HashMap</code>) — the collection keeps a <code>modCount</code>; the iterator snapshots it and throws <code>ConcurrentModificationException</code> if it changes. It's a <em>bug detector</em>, explicitly best-effort — never write logic that depends on it firing.</p>
<p><strong>Fail-safe</strong> (<code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code>, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>) — iterates a snapshot or is weakly consistent: never throws, but may not reflect concurrent changes.</p>
<pre><code>// throws CME
for (String s : list) if (s.isBlank()) list.remove(s);

// correct
list.removeIf(String::isBlank);
// or
Iterator&lt;String&gt; it = list.iterator();
while (it.hasNext()) if (it.next().isBlank()) it.remove();
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha">CME is <strong>not</strong> only a multithreading error — the single-threaded for-each-and-remove above is the most common cause by far. Say that; many candidates assert it's a concurrency-only exception.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-30"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q30</span><span class="qtext">ArrayList vs LinkedList — which do you actually use?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>ArrayList, almost always.</strong> The textbook answer favours <code>LinkedList</code> for insertions; the real answer is that cache locality beats asymptotics at realistic sizes.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>ArrayList</code> — contiguous array. O(1) indexed access, O(n) mid-list insert/remove (but it's <code>System.arraycopy</code>, which is brutally fast and cache-friendly). Grows by ~1.5×.</li>
<li><code>LinkedList</code> — doubly-linked nodes. O(1) insert/remove <em>given the node</em>, but O(n) to <em>find</em> it. Each node is a separate heap object with two extra pointers — poor locality and ~3× the memory overhead.</li>
</ul>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The honest answer:</strong> "I use <code>ArrayList</code> by default. <code>LinkedList</code> only earns its place when I'm genuinely using it as a <code>Deque</code> — constant-time add/remove at both ends — and even then <code>ArrayDeque</code> is usually faster because it's array-backed. I don't think I've deliberately chosen <code>LinkedList</code> in production."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-31"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q31</span><span class="qtext">HashSet vs TreeSet vs LinkedHashSet — and the same for Maps.</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Set / Map</th><th>Order</th><th>Cost</th><th>Requires</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>HashSet</code> / <code>HashMap</code></td><td>None (and not stable across runs)</td><td>O(1) average</td><td>Good <code>hashCode</code>/<code>equals</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>LinkedHashSet</code> / <code>LinkedHashMap</code></td><td><strong>Insertion order</strong> (or access order)</td><td>O(1), slight memory cost for the linked list</td><td>Same</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>TreeSet</code> / <code>TreeMap</code></td><td><strong>Sorted</strong></td><td>O(log n)</td><td><code>Comparable</code> or a <code>Comparator</code>; no nulls</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The trick worth volunteering</strong> — "<code>LinkedHashMap</code> with <code>accessOrder=true</code> and an overridden <code>removeEldestEntry</code> is an LRU cache in about six lines. I'd use Caffeine in production for the concurrency and stats, but it's the answer if they ask me to build one live." That's a plausible coding question (§9, Q59) — have it ready.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-32"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q32</span><span class="qtext">The equals/hashCode contract — state it and say what breaks.</span></p><div class="a">
<ol>
<li>Equal objects <strong>must</strong> have equal hash codes.</li>
<li>Unequal objects <em>may</em> collide — that's allowed, just slower.</li>
<li><code>hashCode</code> must be consistent while the object's <code>equals</code>-relevant state is unchanged.</li>
<li><code>equals</code> must be reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent, and <code>x.equals(null)</code> is false.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Break rule 1</strong> and hash collections silently lose entries: you <code>put</code> an object and <code>get</code> returns null, because you're looking in the wrong bucket. <strong>Break rule 3</strong> — by mutating a key after insertion — and the entry is unreachable and unremovable, which is a leak (Q11).</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The symmetry trap</strong> with inheritance: if <code>Sub.equals</code> compares extra fields but <code>Super.equals</code> doesn't, then <code>sup.equals(sub)</code> is true while <code>sub.equals(sup)</code> is false — symmetry broken, and collections behave non-deterministically. That's why Joshua Bloch's guidance is <strong>favour composition over inheritance for value types</strong>, and why <code>getClass() != o.getClass()</code> is safer than <code>instanceof</code> here. Best modern answer: <strong>use a <code>record</code></strong> — you get a correct <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code> and <code>toString</code> generated for free.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-33"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q33</span><span class="qtext">How do you create a thread, and why prefer an ExecutorService?</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li><code>extends Thread</code> — burns your one inheritance slot. Avoid.</li>
<li><code>implements Runnable</code> — better; no return value, can't throw checked exceptions.</li>
<li><code>implements Callable&lt;T&gt;</code> — returns a value, can throw. Submit to an executor, get a <code>Future</code>.</li>
<li><code>ExecutorService</code> — <strong>the answer</strong>. Decouples task submission from execution policy, reuses threads (thread creation is expensive), bounds concurrency, and gives you lifecycle control.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Avoid <code>Executors.newFixedThreadPool</code> / <code>newCachedThreadPool</code> in production.</strong> Fixed pools use an <strong>unbounded</strong> <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code> — under overload you queue until OOM instead of shedding load. Cached pools create <strong>unbounded threads</strong>. Construct <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> directly with a bounded queue, a named <code>ThreadFactory</code> (so thread dumps are readable), and an explicit <code>RejectedExecutionHandler</code>. Choosing <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code> gives you natural back-pressure: the submitting thread does the work, so it stops submitting.</p>
<p class="bonus">If Java 21 is in play, add: "for blocking I/O I'd now reach for <code>newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()</code> and move back-pressure to an explicit semaphore, because pool sizing stops being the limiter." (Q26.)</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-34"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q34</span><span class="qtext">synchronized vs ReentrantLock vs volatile vs Atomic — when each?</span></p><div class="a">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Gives you</th><th>Reach for it when</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>volatile</code></td><td>Visibility + ordering. <strong>No atomicity.</strong></td><td>A flag written by one thread, read by others. <code>volatile boolean running</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Atomic*</code></td><td>Lock-free atomic read-modify-write via CAS</td><td>Counters, sequences, single-variable state machines (<code>compareAndSet</code>).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>synchronized</code></td><td>Mutual exclusion + visibility. Reentrant. Auto-released on exception.</td><td>The default. Simple, hard to misuse, JIT-optimised.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ReentrantLock</code></td><td>Everything <code>synchronized</code> has, plus <code>tryLock</code>, timed/interruptible acquisition, fairness, multiple <code>Condition</code>s</td><td>When you need one of those four things — and only then.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ReadWriteLock</code> / <code>StampedLock</code></td><td>Many readers, one writer; <code>StampedLock</code> adds optimistic reads</td><td>Read-dominated shared state. <code>StampedLock</code> is <strong>not</strong> reentrant — a real trap.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p class="gotcha"><code>ReentrantLock</code> must be unlocked in a <code>finally</code>. <code>synchronized</code> can't be forgotten; a lock can. That's the main argument for preferring <code>synchronized</code> when it suffices.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The "counter" question</strong> is a favourite. Ladder: <code>int++</code> is broken → <code>volatile int++</code> is still broken (read-modify-write isn't atomic) → <code>AtomicInteger</code> is correct → <code>LongAdder</code> is faster under high contention, at the cost of a non-atomic <code>sum()</code>.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-35"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q35</span><span class="qtext">What is a deadlock, and how do you prevent and detect one?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Four Coffman conditions, all required: <strong>mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait</strong>. Break any one and deadlock is impossible.</p>
<p><strong>Prevention, in order of preference:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Lock ordering</strong> — impose a global order (e.g. always lock the lower account ID first). Breaks circular wait. This is the answer for a money-transfer question.</li>
<li><strong><code>tryLock</code> with a timeout</strong> — breaks hold-and-wait; back off and retry with jitter.</li>
<li><strong>Reduce lock scope</strong>, or remove the lock with an immutable or lock-free design.</li>
<li>Never call unknown/foreign code while holding a lock — that's how you acquire a lock you didn't know about.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Detection:</strong> <code>jstack &lt;pid&gt;</code> or <code>jcmd &lt;pid&gt; Thread.print</code> — HotSpot prints "Found one Java-level deadlock" and names both threads and both monitors. JConsole's "Detect Deadlock" button does the same. Programmatically, <code>ThreadMXBean.findDeadlockedThreads()</code>.</p>
<p class="bonus">Name the neighbours: <strong>livelock</strong> (threads keep responding to each other and make no progress), <strong>starvation</strong> (a thread never gets the lock — which is what <code>ReentrantLock</code>'s fairness flag addresses, at a throughput cost).</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-36"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q36</span><span class="qtext">CompletableFuture — why, and what are the traps?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><code>Future.get()</code> blocks and you can't compose or chain. <code>CompletableFuture</code> gives you a non-blocking pipeline.</p>
<pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Odds&gt;   odds   = supplyAsync(() -&gt; oddsClient.fetch(id), pool);
CompletableFuture&lt;Limits&gt; limits = supplyAsync(() -&gt; limitsClient.fetch(user), pool);

odds.thenCombine(limits, BetQuote::of)
    .orTimeout(300, MILLISECONDS)
    .exceptionally(ex -&gt; BetQuote.unavailable())
    .thenAccept(this::publish);
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><code>thenApply</code> — transform. <code>thenCompose</code> — flatMap, when the function itself returns a future (use this to avoid <code>CompletableFuture&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>).</li>
<li><code>thenCombine</code> — join two independent futures. <code>allOf</code> / <code>anyOf</code> — fan-in.</li>
<li><code>exceptionally</code> / <code>handle</code> / <code>whenComplete</code> — error paths. <code>orTimeout</code> (Java 9+) — bound the wait.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Two traps worth naming.</strong> (1) <strong>Always pass your own executor.</strong> The default is the common <code>ForkJoinPool</code>, sized to CPU count and shared with parallel streams — put blocking I/O on it and you starve the whole JVM. (2) The non-<code>Async</code> variants may run on <em>whichever thread completed the previous stage</em>, which can be a Netty event loop. If the work is non-trivial, use the <code>*Async</code> variant with an explicit pool.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-37"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q37</span><span class="qtext">How would you make a class immutable, and why bother?</span></p><div class="a">
<ol>
<li>Class <code>final</code> (or all constructors private) — no subclass can break the invariant.</li>
<li>All fields <code>private final</code>.</li>
<li>No setters, no method that mutates state.</li>
<li><strong>Defensive-copy mutable fields on the way in <em>and</em> on the way out.</strong> This is the step candidates miss.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Why:</strong> inherently thread-safe with zero synchronisation; safe as a map key; safe to cache and share; no partially-constructed states; and the JMM guarantees correctly-constructed <code>final</code> fields are visible to other threads without synchronisation.</p>
<p class="bonus">Modern answer: <strong><code>record</code></strong> (Java 16+) gives you final fields, a canonical constructor, <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code>/<code>toString</code>. But a record is only <em>shallowly</em> immutable — a <code>List</code> component is still mutable, so you still copy in the compact constructor:</p>
<pre><code>public record Bet(String id, BigDecimal stake, List&lt;Selection&gt; selections) {
    public Bet {
        selections = List.copyOf(selections);   // defensive copy
    }
}
</code></pre>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-38"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q38</span><span class="qtext">Checked vs unchecked exceptions — what's your position?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>Checked</strong> (extends <code>Exception</code>) — compiler-enforced, for recoverable conditions the caller can plausibly act on. <strong>Unchecked</strong> (extends <code>RuntimeException</code>) — programming errors and unrecoverable conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Have a position, don't recite the definition:</strong> "In practice I lean toward unchecked for service-layer code. Checked exceptions leak implementation detail up the call stack, they compose badly with lambdas and streams, and in real codebases they get caught-and-swallowed, which is worse than not having them. I use a small set of unchecked domain exceptions and handle them once at the boundary — a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code> in Spring."</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The trap question:</strong> "what if <code>finally</code> has a <code>return</code>?" It <em>discards</em> the exception or the earlier return value entirely — the exception is silently swallowed. Never return from <code>finally</code>. Related: with try-with-resources, if both the body and <code>close()</code> throw, the body's exception wins and the close exception is attached as <strong>suppressed</strong> (<code>getSuppressed()</code>) — which is exactly why try-with-resources beats a hand-written <code>finally { close(); }</code>.</p>
</div></section>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-8-coding-concepts">8. Coding Concepts — Spring Boot, SQL, Kafka, Redis<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-8-coding-concepts">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">"Coding Concepts" on the invite most likely means the applied layer: how you build a service, not just how the language works. All four topics below appear in public Entain India reports. Parts 02 and 07 go deeper on Spring; this is the Entain cut.</p>
<section class="qa" id="q12-39"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q39</span><span class="qtext">What does Spring Boot auto-configuration actually do?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><code>@SpringBootApplication</code> = <code>@Configuration</code> + <code>@ComponentScan</code> + <code>@EnableAutoConfiguration</code>.</p>
<p>Auto-configuration reads candidate configuration classes from <code>META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports</code> (Boot 3; <code>spring.factories</code> in Boot 2), then applies each one <strong>conditionally</strong>: <code>@ConditionalOnClass</code> (is the library on the classpath?), <code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> (have you already defined your own?), <code>@ConditionalOnProperty</code>.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The sentence that lands it:</strong> "<code>@ConditionalOnMissingBean</code> is the key to the whole design — auto-config always backs off if you've defined the bean yourself. That's why Boot feels like sensible defaults rather than magic you have to fight."</p>
<p class="gotcha">Know the debugging move: run with <code>--debug</code> and Boot prints the <strong>Conditions Evaluation Report</strong> — every auto-config that matched, and every one that didn't, with the reason. Saying that is more convincing than any definition.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-40"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q40</span><span class="qtext">Constructor vs field injection?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>Constructor injection, always.</strong> Reasons, in order of weight:</p>
<ol>
<li>Dependencies can be <code>final</code> → the object is immutable and fully initialised at construction.</li>
<li>You can instantiate it in a unit test with <code>new</code>, no Spring context, no reflection.</li>
<li>A constructor with eight parameters <em>looks</em> wrong — the pain is a design signal. Field injection hides it.</li>
<li>No <code>NullPointerException</code> from a half-wired bean.</li>
<li>Circular dependencies fail fast at startup rather than being silently resolved.</li>
</ol>
<p>Since Spring 4.3, a single-constructor class needs no <code>@Autowired</code> at all — with Lombok's <code>@RequiredArgsConstructor</code> it's one annotation on the class.</p>
<p class="gotcha">If asked how to break a genuine circular dependency: <code>@Lazy</code> works but is a plaster. The real fix is to extract the shared behaviour into a third bean, or publish an event. Note Boot 2.6+ <strong>disallows circular references by default</strong> — that change was deliberate.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-41"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q41</span><span class="qtext">How does @Transactional work, and where does it silently not work?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Spring creates a <strong>proxy</strong> (JDK dynamic proxy for interfaces, CGLIB subclass otherwise). The proxy opens a transaction before the method and commits or rolls back after.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The three failures they're fishing for:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Self-invocation.</strong> <code>this.otherMethod()</code> inside the same bean bypasses the proxy entirely, so <code>@Transactional</code> on <code>otherMethod</code> does nothing. Fix: move it to another bean, or self-inject.</li>
<li><strong>Non-public methods.</strong> Ignored by the default proxy mode. No error, no transaction.</li>
<li><strong>Rollback rules.</strong> By default Spring rolls back on <code>RuntimeException</code> and <code>Error</code> — <strong>not on checked exceptions</strong>. If you throw a checked exception expecting a rollback, you commit instead. Use <code>@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Know the propagation values</strong>: <code>REQUIRED</code> (default — join or start), <code>REQUIRES_NEW</code> (suspend the outer, start a genuinely independent one — the right choice for an audit record that must survive a business rollback), <code>NESTED</code> (savepoint), <code>MANDATORY</code>, <code>SUPPORTS</code>, <code>NEVER</code>.</p>
<p class="bonus">If the conversation turns to money — which at Entain it might — say: "for a wallet debit I'd want the isolation level to be explicit rather than inherited from the database default, and I'd lean on optimistic locking with <code>@Version</code> plus a retry, because pessimistic row locks on a hot account row become a throughput ceiling under peak load."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-42"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q42</span><span class="qtext">What is the N+1 problem and how do you fix it?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>You load N parents with one query, then touching a lazy association fires one query <em>per parent</em> — N+1 round trips. It passes every test with 3 rows and destroys production with 10,000.</p>
<p><strong>Fixes, in order:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><code>JOIN FETCH</code> in JPQL — one query, one join.</li>
<li><code>@EntityGraph</code> on the repository method — declarative, keeps the derived query.</li>
<li><code>@BatchSize(size = 25)</code> — turns N queries into N/25 <code>IN (…)</code> queries. Good when a join would produce a cartesian product.</li>
<li>A projection or DTO query — fetch only the columns you need.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>How you <em>detect</em> it is the better half of the answer:</strong> "I turn on <code>spring.jpa.show-sql</code> or, better, run <strong>datasource-proxy</strong> or Hibernate statistics in tests and assert on query count. Reading SQL logs by eye doesn't scale; a test that fails when a change adds a query does." That's a senior answer to a mid-level question.</p>
<p class="bonus">Beware the pair-trap: <code>JOIN FETCH</code> on <em>two</em> collections throws <code>MultipleBagFetchException</code> (cartesian product). Fix with <code>Set</code>, or fetch one collection and <code>@BatchSize</code> the other.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-43"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q43</span><span class="qtext">Write a SQL query to find the second-highest value — and then the top N per group.</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Reported Entain rounds ask SQL live. Have both shapes ready.</p>
<pre><code>-- Second highest stake, null-safe, handles ties
SELECT MAX(stake) FROM bets
WHERE stake &lt; (SELECT MAX(stake) FROM bets);

-- Or with a window function (say this one — it generalises to Nth)
SELECT stake FROM (
  SELECT stake, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY stake DESC) AS rnk
  FROM bets
) t WHERE rnk = 2;

-- Top 3 bets per customer
SELECT customer_id, bet_id, stake
FROM (
  SELECT customer_id, bet_id, stake,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY stake DESC) AS rn
  FROM bets
) t
WHERE rn &lt;= 3;
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Know the difference cold, because it's the follow-up:</strong> <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> = 1,2,3,4 (arbitrary tie-break); <code>RANK</code> = 1,2,2,4 (gaps); <code>DENSE_RANK</code> = 1,2,2,3 (no gaps). For "second highest distinct value" you want <code>DENSE_RANK</code>.</p>
<p class="bonus">Other SQL they may ask: find duplicates (<code>GROUP BY … HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code>), delete duplicates keeping one (<code>ROW_NUMBER</code> in a CTE), and the difference between <code>WHERE</code> and <code>HAVING</code> (row filter before aggregation vs group filter after). And know <strong>why <code>SELECT *</code> plus a function on an indexed column kills the index</strong> — <code>WHERE YEAR(created_at) = 2026</code> can't use the index; <code>WHERE created_at &gt;= '2026-01-01' AND created_at &lt; '2027-01-01'</code> can.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-44"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q44</span><span class="qtext">Explain database indexes — when do they hurt?</span></p><div class="a">
<p>A B+ tree keyed on the indexed columns; leaves are sorted and linked, so equality and <strong>range</strong> scans are both fast. A <strong>clustered</strong> index <em>is</em> the table's physical order (InnoDB's primary key); a secondary index stores the PK and needs a second lookup unless the index <strong>covers</strong> every column the query needs.</p>
<p><strong>When they hurt:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Every write must update every index — heavy-write tables pay for each one.</li>
<li>Low-cardinality columns (a boolean, a status with 3 values) rarely benefit; the planner often prefers a table scan anyway.</li>
<li>They consume memory and disk, and bloat the buffer pool.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Composite index order is the question that separates people.</strong> An index on <code>(customer_id, created_at)</code> serves <code>WHERE customer_id = ?</code> and <code>WHERE customer_id = ? AND created_at &gt; ?</code> — but <strong>not</strong> <code>WHERE created_at &gt; ?</code> alone. Leftmost-prefix rule. Put the equality column first, the range column last.</p>
<p class="bonus">Always finish with: "and I'd confirm with <code>EXPLAIN</code> / <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> rather than reasoning about it — the planner's row estimates are what actually decide."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-45"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q45</span><span class="qtext">Kafka — partitions, consumer groups, ordering and delivery guarantees.</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li>A <strong>topic</strong> is split into <strong>partitions</strong>; a partition is an ordered, append-only log. <strong>Ordering is guaranteed per partition, never across a topic.</strong></li>
<li>The <strong>partition key</strong> decides placement (<code>hash(key) % partitions</code>). Key by <code>customerId</code> and all of one customer's events are ordered — that's how you get ordering where it matters.</li>
<li>In a <strong>consumer group</strong>, each partition is consumed by exactly one member. <strong>Partition count is your parallelism ceiling</strong> — more consumers than partitions leaves some idle.</li>
<li><strong>Delivery</strong>: at-most-once (commit before processing), at-least-once (commit after — the sane default), exactly-once (idempotent producer + transactions, at a throughput cost).</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The senior line:</strong> "at-least-once plus an idempotent consumer is what I'd actually build. Exactly-once in Kafka only holds within Kafka — the moment you write to an external database or call a payment API, the guarantee ends. Idempotency keys on the consumer side are the real answer." At a betting company, "process this settlement exactly once" is the whole problem, so this lands well.</p>
<p class="bonus">Have answers for: consumer lag (and how you alert on it), rebalancing storms (<code>max.poll.interval.ms</code> too low for slow processing), and what you do with a poison message (retry topic + DLT, with attempt count in a header).</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-46"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q46</span><span class="qtext">Redis — what do you use it for, and what are the pitfalls?</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>Uses:</strong> cache-aside, distributed session store, distributed lock, rate limiter (token bucket via <code>INCR</code> + <code>EXPIRE</code>, or a Lua script for atomicity), leaderboards (<strong>sorted sets</strong> — a natural fit for betting), pub/sub, and Streams as a lightweight queue.</p>
<p><strong>Caching patterns:</strong> cache-aside (read-through by hand — the default), write-through, write-behind. Know the failure modes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cache stampede / thundering herd</strong> — a hot key expires and a thousand requests hit the DB at once. Fix: a per-key lock so one caller refreshes, or probabilistic early expiry.</li>
<li><strong>Cache penetration</strong> — repeated lookups for a key that doesn't exist. Fix: cache the negative result briefly, or a Bloom filter.</li>
<li><strong>Cache avalanche</strong> — many keys expiring at once. Fix: jitter the TTLs.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>On distributed locks, have the honest opinion:</strong> "a naive <code>SETNX</code> lock isn't safe — you need a unique token and a Lua script to release, so you don't delete someone else's lock after your TTL expired. Redlock is contested; Martin Kleppmann's critique is that it doesn't survive GC pauses and clock skew. So I use a Redis lock for <em>efficiency</em> — stopping duplicate work — and never for <em>correctness</em>. Correctness goes in the database with a unique constraint or a conditional update." That answer is worth a lot in a round that reportedly asks about Redis.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-47"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q47</span><span class="qtext">How do you make an API idempotent?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">Highly likely at a betting company: "the customer's place-bet request timed out and the app retried. How do you avoid taking the stake twice?"</p>
<ol>
<li>Client generates an <strong>idempotency key</strong> (a UUID) per logical operation and sends it as a header. Retries reuse the <em>same</em> key.</li>
<li>Server stores the key in a table with a <strong>unique constraint</strong>, alongside the request hash and the eventual response.</li>
<li>First request: insert the key, process, store the response. Duplicate: the insert violates the constraint → return the stored response, don't re-execute.</li>
<li>Handle the in-flight case (key present, response not yet stored) with a <code>409</code> or a short wait; expire keys after 24h.</li>
<li>Reject a reused key with a <em>different</em> request body — that's a client bug you want surfaced, not silently absorbed.</li>
</ol>
<p class="bonus">Tie it back to HTTP semantics: <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code> and <code>DELETE</code> are idempotent by definition; <code>POST</code> is not, which is exactly why it needs the key. And say where the uniqueness lives: <strong>in the database, not in Redis</strong> — see Q46.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-48"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q48</span><span class="qtext">How do you keep a service resilient when a downstream dependency is slow?</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li><strong>Timeouts on everything.</strong> A missing timeout is how one slow dependency takes down every thread in your pool. Connect timeout <em>and</em> read timeout.</li>
<li><strong>Circuit breaker</strong> (Resilience4j) — after a failure-rate threshold, open the circuit and fail fast; half-open to probe recovery. It protects <em>you</em> from waiting and <em>them</em> from a thundering herd.</li>
<li><strong>Retry with exponential backoff and jitter</strong> — and only on idempotent operations and retryable errors. Retrying a non-idempotent POST without an idempotency key is how you double-charge someone.</li>
<li><strong>Bulkhead</strong> — separate pools per dependency, so one slow integration can't consume all the threads.</li>
<li><strong>Fallback</strong> — a cached value, a degraded response, or an honest error. Decide deliberately which is acceptable.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The point to make:</strong> "retry and circuit breaker fight each other if you order them wrong — retrying inside the breaker inflates the failure count and trips it early. In Resilience4j the sane order is Retry wrapping CircuitBreaker wrapping the call, so the breaker sees real calls and the retry respects an open circuit."</p>
</div></section>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-9-problem-solving">9. Problem Solving — the coding bank<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-9-problem-solving">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">Reported Entain India coding is <strong>easy-to-medium</strong>, weighted toward <strong>strings, arrays and example programs</strong>, with the HackerRank stage described as harder. Several reports stress that they care more about <em>how you think</em> than which technology you know. So narrate.</p>
<p><strong>The protocol — run it every single time, even on a five-line problem:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Restate</strong> the problem in your own words. Thirty seconds. It catches misunderstandings while they're free.</li>
<li><strong>Ask about the edges</strong> — empty input, nulls, duplicates, negative numbers, Unicode, size of <code>n</code>. One or two questions, not ten.</li>
<li><strong>State the brute force and its complexity</strong>, then say why you're improving it. Never code the brute force silently.</li>
<li><strong>Say the approach before you type</strong> — "I'll use a HashMap of value to index, one pass, O(n) time O(n) space."</li>
<li><strong>Write it</strong>, narrating only at decision points, not every keystroke.</li>
<li><strong>Dry-run one small example out loud</strong> — this catches more bugs than re-reading does.</li>
<li><strong>State final complexity and one thing you'd change for production</strong> (validation, overflow, a test you'd add).</li>
</ol>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>If you get stuck, say what you're stuck on.</strong> Silence reads as "doesn't know". "I'm trying to decide whether to sort first — sorting costs me O(n log n) but makes the dedupe trivial; let me think about whether I actually need the order" reads as an engineer working. Interviewers hire the second one.</p>
<h3 id="p12-9-1-strings">9.1 Strings — the most likely category</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-49"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q49</span><span class="qtext">Reverse a string, and reverse the words in a sentence.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>// Reverse in place — say that String is immutable, so char[] or StringBuilder
static String reverse(String s) {
    char[] c = s.toCharArray();
    for (int i = 0, j = c.length - 1; i &lt; j; i++, j--) {
        char t = c[i]; c[i] = c[j]; c[j] = t;
    }
    return new String(c);        // O(n) time, O(n) space
}

// Reverse word order: "place the bet" -> "bet the place"
static String reverseWords(String s) {
    String[] w = s.trim().split("\\s+");
    Collections.reverse(Arrays.asList(w));
    return String.join(" ", w);
}
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha">If they ask for one-line: <code>new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString()</code> — but <strong>say the caveat</strong>: it reverses UTF-16 code units, so surrogate pairs (emoji) and combining characters break. That single sentence is worth more than the loop.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-50"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q50</span><span class="qtext">Check if two strings are anagrams.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
    if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
    int[] counts = new int[26];                 // assumes lowercase a-z: SAY THIS
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; a.length(); i++) {
        counts[a.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
        counts[b.charAt(i) - 'a']--;
    }
    for (int c : counts) if (c != 0) return false;
    return true;                                 // O(n) time, O(1) space
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">Volunteer the generalisation: "for arbitrary Unicode I'd use a <code>HashMap&lt;Character,Integer&gt;</code> — O(n) time, O(k) space. Sorting both and comparing is the one-liner but it's O(n log n)." Showing you know all three and why you picked one is the answer.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-51"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q51</span><span class="qtext">Find the first non-repeating character in a string.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static char firstUnique(String s) {
    Map&lt;Character, Integer&gt; counts = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();   // insertion order!
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) counts.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
    for (var e : counts.entrySet()) if (e.getValue() == 1) return e.getKey();
    return '_';
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Why <code>LinkedHashMap</code> is the whole trick</strong> — a plain <code>HashMap</code> loses insertion order, so "first" becomes meaningless and you'd need a second pass over the string. Say that out loud; it's the point of the question. <code>merge(c, 1, Integer::sum)</code> is also a nicer idiom than <code>getOrDefault</code> — small signal, costs nothing.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-52"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q52</span><span class="qtext">Count character / word frequency (the Java 8 streams version).</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>Map&lt;Character, Long&gt; freq = s.chars()
    .mapToObj(c -&gt; (char) c)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(c -&gt; c, Collectors.counting()));

// Most frequent word in a sentence
String top = Arrays.stream(text.toLowerCase().split("\\W+"))
    .filter(w -&gt; !w.isBlank())
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(w -&gt; w, Collectors.counting()))
    .entrySet().stream()
    .max(Map.Entry.comparingByValue())
    .map(Map.Entry::getKey).orElse("");
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha">Expect "now do it without streams" — they're checking you didn't only memorise the idiom. Have the loop version ready too. Part 08 has the full <code>Collectors</code> catalogue if you want to revise downstream collectors tonight.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-53"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q53</span><span class="qtext">Check a palindrome, ignoring case and punctuation.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
    int i = 0, j = s.length() - 1;
    while (i &lt; j) {
        while (i &lt; j &amp;&amp; !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(i))) i++;
        while (i &lt; j &amp;&amp; !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(j))) j--;
        if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(i++)) != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(j--)))
            return false;
    }
    return true;   // O(n) time, O(1) space
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">The O(1) space two-pointer version is the one to write. Mention that <code>reverse().equals()</code> works but allocates a whole second string — a fine trade at small n, wrong at large n.</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-9-2-arrays">9.2 Arrays and maps</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-54"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q54</span><span class="qtext">Two Sum — return indices of the pair summing to target.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
    Map&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; seen = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();   // value -> index
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; nums.length; i++) {
        Integer j = seen.get(target - nums[i]);
        if (j != null) return new int[]{j, i};
        seen.put(nums[i], i);                        // put AFTER the check
    }
    return new int[0];
}
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha">Putting before checking breaks <code>target = 2×nums[i]</code> by matching an element with itself. Also: this is the archetype for "I traded O(n) space for O(n) time instead of O(n²)" — say that sentence; it's the whole lesson of the problem and it generalises to half the bank.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-55"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q55</span><span class="qtext">Find duplicates in an array; find the missing number in 1..n.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>// Duplicates
Set&lt;Integer&gt; seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(), dupes = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
for (int n : nums) if (!seen.add(n)) dupes.add(n);     // add() returns false if present

// Missing number in 1..n — the O(1) space answer
static int missing(int[] nums, int n) {
    long expected = (long) n * (n + 1) / 2;            // long guards overflow
    long actual = 0;
    for (int v : nums) actual += v;
    return (int) (expected - actual);
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">Two things to volunteer: <code>Set.add</code> returning a boolean is the idiomatic dup check (no <code>contains</code>-then-<code>add</code>), and the <strong>XOR</strong> variant of "missing number" avoids overflow entirely — XOR all values with all indices and the survivors cancel. Mentioning overflow unprompted is a real signal at a company that handles money.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-56"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q56</span><span class="qtext">Maximum subarray sum (Kadane's).</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static int maxSubArray(int[] a) {
    int best = a[0], current = a[0];
    for (int i = 1; i &lt; a.length; i++) {
        current = Math.max(a[i], current + a[i]);   // extend, or start fresh here
        best = Math.max(best, current);
    }
    return best;   // O(n) time, O(1) space
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">Explain the invariant in one line — "<code>current</code> is the best sum of a subarray <em>ending at i</em>; if the running sum is worse than the element alone, start fresh" — and note it handles all-negative arrays correctly because <code>best</code> starts at <code>a[0]</code>, not 0. That edge case is the usual bug.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-57"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q57</span><span class="qtext">Sort or group with a custom Comparator.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>bets.sort(Comparator.comparing(Bet::stake).reversed()
        .thenComparing(Bet::placedAt)
        .thenComparing(Bet::id));

// Group settled bets by customer, summing stake
Map&lt;String, BigDecimal&gt; totals = bets.stream()
    .filter(Bet::isSettled)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Bet::customerId,
             Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, Bet::stake, BigDecimal::add)));
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha">Two traps. (1) <strong>Never subtract for comparison</strong> — <code>(a, b) -&gt; a.value() - b.value()</code> overflows on large ints and silently reverses the order. Use <code>Integer.compare</code>. (2) A <code>Comparator</code> inconsistent with <code>equals</code> makes <code>TreeSet</code> and <code>TreeMap</code> behave bizarrely, and an intransitive one makes <code>List.sort</code> throw <code>IllegalArgumentException: Comparison method violates its general contract</code>.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>And say this:</strong> "money is <code>BigDecimal</code> or minor units in a <code>long</code>, never <code>double</code>. <code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code> in binary floating point, and <code>BigDecimal.equals</code> compares scale too, so I use <code>compareTo</code>." At a betting company that one remark is disproportionately valuable.</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-9-3-concurrency-coding">9.3 Concurrency, written live</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-58"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q58</span><span class="qtext">Producer–consumer with a bounded buffer.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">Reported at Entain in two separate rounds under "multithreading". Write the <strong>simple</strong> version first and say why.</p>
<pre><code>// The answer you give first
BlockingQueue&lt;Bet&gt; queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(1000);

// producer
queue.put(bet);          // blocks when full -> back-pressure for free
// consumer
Bet bet = queue.take();  // blocks when empty
</code></pre>
<p>Then, if they ask you to hand-roll it:</p>
<pre><code>private final Queue&lt;Bet&gt; q = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
private final int capacity;

public synchronized void put(Bet b) throws InterruptedException {
    while (q.size() == capacity) wait();   // while, never if
    q.add(b);
    notifyAll();
}

public synchronized Bet take() throws InterruptedException {
    while (q.isEmpty()) wait();
    Bet b = q.poll();
    notifyAll();
    return b;
}
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Three things they are specifically checking.</strong> (1) <code>while</code> not <code>if</code> — because of <strong>spurious wakeups</strong>, and because another thread can win the lock between your notify and your wake. (2) <code>notifyAll</code> not <code>notify</code> — with mixed producers and consumers, <code>notify</code> can wake the wrong kind and deadlock. (3) <code>wait()</code> must be called <em>holding the monitor</em>, and it releases it while waiting — <code>sleep()</code> doesn't. Getting all three right is the whole question.</p>
<p class="bonus">Finish with the judgement call: "in production I'd use <code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code> — this is exactly the thing you don't hand-roll. And the bound matters: an unbounded queue just moves an overload problem into a slower OOM."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-59"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q59</span><span class="qtext">Implement a thread-safe LRU cache.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>class LruCache&lt;K, V&gt; extends LinkedHashMap&lt;K, V&gt; {
    private final int capacity;
    LruCache(int capacity) {
        super(16, 0.75f, true);        // true = ACCESS order, the whole trick
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }
    @Override protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry&lt;K, V&gt; eldest) {
        return size() &gt; capacity;
    }
}
Map&lt;String, Odds&gt; cache = Collections.synchronizedMap(new LruCache&lt;&gt;(10_000));
</code></pre>
<p>If they want it from scratch: a <code>HashMap&lt;K, Node&gt;</code> for O(1) lookup plus a doubly-linked list for O(1) recency updates — move to head on access, evict from tail.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><code>synchronizedMap</code> serialises every operation — one lock for the whole cache. Say so, and say the production answer: "<strong>Caffeine</strong>. It gives me concurrent access, size and time eviction, refresh-ahead and hit-rate stats. A hand-rolled LRU in production is a maintenance liability, and a naive <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> + LRU list isn't actually thread-safe anyway because the two structures can't be updated atomically."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-60"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q60</span><span class="qtext">Print odd and even numbers alternately with two threads.</span></p><div class="a">
<p>A perennial Indian-market interview favourite. Know it cold.</p>
<pre><code>class Alternator {
    private int n = 1;
    private final int max;
    Alternator(int max) { this.max = max; }

    synchronized void print(boolean odd) throws InterruptedException {
        while (n &lt;= max) {
            while (n % 2 == (odd ? 0 : 1)) {       // not my turn
                if (n &gt; max) return;
                wait();
            }
            if (n &gt; max) return;
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + ": " + n++);
            notifyAll();
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">If they let you choose the mechanism, <code>Semaphore</code> is cleaner and worth showing: two semaphores, one permit initially on the odd one; each thread acquires its own and releases the other's. Say "this is a turn-taking problem, so a pair of semaphores expresses it more directly than wait/notify" — the framing is the mark, not the code.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-61"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q61</span><span class="qtext">Transfer money between two accounts without deadlocking.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">The most Entain-flavoured concurrency question there is. Two threads, <code>transfer(A→B)</code> and <code>transfer(B→A)</code>, locking in argument order, deadlock immediately.</p>
<pre><code>void transfer(Account from, Account to, BigDecimal amount) {
    Account first  = from.id().compareTo(to.id()) &lt; 0 ? from : to;
    Account second = (first == from) ? to : from;     // GLOBAL LOCK ORDER
    synchronized (first) {
        synchronized (second) {
            if (from.balance().compareTo(amount) &lt; 0)
                throw new InsufficientFundsException(from.id());
            from.debit(amount);
            to.credit(amount);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha">Two edges to raise before they do: <strong>self-transfer</strong> (<code>from == to</code> — guard it, or you double-count), and <strong>equal IDs</strong> if the ordering key isn't unique (fall back to <code>System.identityHashCode</code>, with a tie-breaker lock for the rare collision).</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Then reframe it, and this is what separates you from an SDE I:</strong> "in a real wallet I wouldn't be holding JVM monitors at all — there's more than one instance of the service, so an in-process lock guarantees nothing. The ordering would be enforced in the database, with the transfer in one transaction, an optimistic <code>@Version</code> check and a retry, and the ledger written as immutable double-entry rows rather than a mutable balance column. The balance becomes a projection you can always rebuild and audit."</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-9-4-stretch">9.4 If they push harder</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-62"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q62</span><span class="qtext">Longest substring without repeating characters.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static int longestUnique(String s) {
    Map&lt;Character, Integer&gt; last = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    int best = 0, start = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; s.length(); i++) {
        Integer prev = last.get(s.charAt(i));
        if (prev != null &amp;&amp; prev &gt;= start) start = prev + 1;  // the guard matters
        last.put(s.charAt(i), i);
        best = Math.max(best, i - start + 1);
    }
    return best;   // O(n) time, O(k) space
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">Name the technique — <strong>sliding window</strong> — before you write it. <code>prev &gt;= start</code> is the classic bug: without it, a repeat from <em>before</em> the current window drags <code>start</code> backwards.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-63"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q63</span><span class="qtext">Merge overlapping intervals.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static List&lt;int[]&gt; merge(int[][] intervals) {
    Arrays.sort(intervals, Comparator.comparingInt(a -&gt; a[0]));
    List&lt;int[]&gt; out = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    for (int[] iv : intervals) {
        if (!out.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; iv[0] &lt;= out.get(out.size() - 1)[1])
            out.get(out.size() - 1)[1] = Math.max(out.get(out.size() - 1)[1], iv[1]);
        else
            out.add(iv.clone());
    }
    return out;   // O(n log n), dominated by the sort
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">The <code>Math.max</code> on the end is the bug people ship — a fully-contained interval would otherwise shrink the merged one. And this has a natural betting framing: merging a customer's overlapping self-exclusion or session windows.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-64"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q64</span><span class="qtext">Top K frequent elements.</span></p><div class="a">
<pre><code>static List&lt;String&gt; topK(List&lt;String&gt; items, int k) {
    Map&lt;String, Long&gt; freq = items.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(x -&gt; x, Collectors.counting()));

    PriorityQueue&lt;Map.Entry&lt;String, Long&gt;&gt; heap =
        new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Map.Entry.comparingByValue());   // MIN-heap

    for (var e : freq.entrySet()) {
        heap.offer(e);
        if (heap.size() &gt; k) heap.poll();     // drop the smallest
    }
    return heap.stream().map(Map.Entry::getKey).toList();
}
</code></pre>
<p class="bonus">Say why a <strong>min</strong>-heap of size k, not a max-heap: it gives O(n log k) instead of O(n log n), and O(k) space rather than O(n). "Most-backed selections in the last hour" is the same problem with a time window — a reasonable follow-up at Entain, and the answer there is a bucketed count with sliding expiry, or Redis sorted sets.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-65"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q65</span><span class="qtext">Given a stream of bets, detect a customer exceeding a limit in any 60-second window.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr">A plausible Entain-flavoured "problem solving" question. It's a sliding-window rate limiter wearing a domain costume — recognise it and say so.</p>
<pre><code>class WindowedLimiter {
    private final Map&lt;String, Deque&lt;Long&gt;&gt; events = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();
    private final int limit; private final long windowMs;

    boolean allow(String customerId, long nowMs) {
        Deque&lt;Long&gt; d = events.computeIfAbsent(customerId, k -&gt; new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;());
        synchronized (d) {                       // per-customer lock, not global
            while (!d.isEmpty() &amp;&amp; d.peekFirst() &lt;= nowMs - windowMs)
                d.pollFirst();                   // evict what left the window
            if (d.size() &gt;= limit) return false;
            d.addLast(nowMs);
            return true;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Then raise the two problems yourself</strong>, because that's the actual test. (1) <strong>Unbounded memory</strong> — the outer map grows one deque per customer forever; needs expiry or a bounded cache. (2) <strong>It's per-instance</strong> — with three replicas a customer gets 3× the limit, so the counter has to move to Redis (a sorted set of timestamps with <code>ZREMRANGEBYSCORE</code>, or a token bucket in a Lua script for atomicity).</p>
<p class="bonus">Then name the trade-off between algorithms: fixed window is cheapest but allows a 2× burst across the boundary; sliding log is exact but stores every timestamp; <strong>token bucket</strong> is the usual production choice — O(1) memory per key, allows a controlled burst. Knowing when a domain problem is a standard problem is the skill they're testing.</p>
</div></section>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-10-design">10. If design comes up<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-10-design">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">The invitation doesn't name system design, and reported Entain rounds put it at "basics" level for this band — but one report describes being asked to <strong>design a distributed queue</strong>, and Part 03 §5E already has a full worked <strong>betting/wagering platform</strong> design. These three are the ones worth a skim on Wednesday night.</p>
<section class="qa" id="q12-66"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q66</span><span class="qtext">Design a distributed queue.</span></p><div class="a">
<p><strong>Clarify first:</strong> ordering guarantee? delivery guarantee? durability? throughput? consumers competing or broadcast?</p>
<p><strong>Core design:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Partitioned append-only log</strong>, exactly as Kafka does it. Partition by key so per-key ordering holds; total ordering across partitions is deliberately not offered, because it would serialise everything.</li>
<li><strong>Replication</strong> — each partition has a leader and N followers; a write is acknowledged when a quorum (or the in-sync replica set) has it. Leader election on failure.</li>
<li><strong>Consumer offsets</strong> stored durably, committed <em>after</em> processing → at-least-once.</li>
<li><strong>Visibility timeout</strong> (the SQS model) if you want competing consumers on a single queue rather than partition ownership: a message is hidden while being processed, and reappears if not acknowledged in time.</li>
<li><strong>Retention</strong> — time- or size-based; consumers can replay, which is the log's superpower over a traditional queue.</li>
<li><strong>Dead-letter queue</strong> after N failed attempts, so one poison message can't block a partition forever.</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Say the three things explicitly</strong> — ordering is per-partition only; delivery is at-least-once so <strong>consumers must be idempotent</strong>; and partition count bounds consumer parallelism and is painful to change later, so over-provision it. Naming those is what makes it a design answer rather than a description of Kafka.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-67"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q67</span><span class="qtext">Design the bet placement flow.</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Part 03 §5E is the full version. The four-minute spoken skeleton:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Request</strong> → API gateway (auth, rate limit) → Bet Service.</li>
<li><strong>Validate</strong>: market still open, odds still valid (reject or re-quote if they moved), selection combination legal, customer eligible — and the <strong>responsible-gambling checks</strong>: deposit limits, self-exclusion, affordability. Say this unprompted; it is a real part of the domain.</li>
<li><strong>Reserve funds</strong> in the wallet — a single transaction, optimistic locking with <code>@Version</code>, idempotency key on the request (Q47) so a client retry can't double-stake.</li>
<li><strong>Persist the bet</strong> and emit a <code>BetPlaced</code> event (transactional outbox, so the DB write and the publish can't diverge).</li>
<li><strong>Downstream consumers</strong>: risk/liability engine, CRM, reporting, settlement — all decoupled and asynchronous.</li>
<li><strong>Settlement</strong> on result: an event-driven consumer credits winnings, idempotently, keyed on <code>(betId, resultVersion)</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p class="bonus"><strong>The two hard parts to name, because naming them is the senior move:</strong> (1) <strong>odds change between quote and placement</strong> — you need a version on the price and an explicit accept-or-reject policy; (2) <strong>settlement must be exactly-once from the customer's perspective</strong>, which you get from at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent consumer with a uniqueness constraint in the ledger, not from a broker's exactly-once flag (Q45).</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-68"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q68</span><span class="qtext">How do you keep a live odds feed fast for a million clients?</span></p><div class="a">
<ul>
<li><strong>Push, don't poll</strong> — WebSocket, or SSE if it's one-directional. Polling a million clients is a self-inflicted DDoS.</li>
<li><strong>Fan-out</strong> — a pub/sub layer (Redis pub/sub, Kafka, or a purpose-built gateway tier) with clients subscribed per market, so an odds change touches only subscribers of that market.</li>
<li><strong>Send deltas, not snapshots</strong>, with a sequence number so a client can detect a gap and request a resync.</li>
<li><strong>Conflate</strong> — if odds change five times in 200 ms, send the latest, not all five. Slow clients get the current state, not a backlog.</li>
<li><strong>Edge</strong> — CDN and regional gateways for connection termination; keep the hot path in memory.</li>
<li><strong>Back-pressure</strong> — bound the per-connection outbound buffer and drop the slow consumer rather than letting it grow your heap (tie it back to §6 — this is exactly how a feed service OOMs).</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha">Have the honest caveat ready: "the consistency model matters more than the latency number. A customer must never place a bet at a price that no longer exists, so the <em>authoritative</em> check happens server-side at placement (Q67) — the feed is an optimisation for the UI, not the source of truth."</p>
</div></section>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-11-questions-to-ask">11. Questions to ask them<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-11-questions-to-ask">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">Not asking is a red flag; asking generic questions is a wasted opportunity. Pick <strong>three</strong>, matched to whoever is actually in the room (Q1).</p>
<p><strong>If an engineer is interviewing you:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>"Is this team on the sportsbook side, or platform — wallet, payments, transaction processing?" <em>(Shows you know the estate; gets you the single most useful fact about the job.)</em></li>
<li>"What does peak look like for this service — a Grand National or a derby — and what breaks first when it arrives?" <em>(Every engineer enjoys answering this, and the answer tells you how mature the system really is.)</em></li>
<li>"How much of the stack here is the long-lived proprietary platform versus newer services? What does a migration look like in practice?"</li>
<li>"What's the deployment cadence, and who carries the pager for what you build?"</li>
<li>"Where does responsible-gambling logic live — inside each service, or as a platform capability?" <em>(Serious, domain-aware, and very few candidates ask it.)</em></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>If the recruiter is running it:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>"Who would I be meeting in the next round, and what will it focus on?"</li>
<li>"How is the SDE II band defined at Entain — what separates it from SDE III?" <em>(Directly relevant given your experience; see Q74.)</em></li>
<li>"How is the team split between Hyderabad and Pune, and how does it work with the UK?"</li>
<li>"What's the timeline from here?"</li>
</ul>
<p class="bonus"><strong>One closing line worth having.</strong> If it goes well: "This sounds like the platform side of the business rather than feature work, which is what I'm looking for. Is there anything about my background you'd want me to expand on before we finish?" It invites them to voice a doubt while you can still answer it.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-12-framing">12. Framing and the questions about you<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-12-framing">#</a></h2>
<section class="qa" id="q12-69"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q69</span><span class="qtext">"Tell me about yourself."</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Ninety seconds, three beats: <strong>where you are now and what you own</strong> → <strong>one concrete thing you built or fixed, with a number</strong> → <strong>why Entain, specifically</strong>. Then stop talking.</p>
<p class="gotcha">Do not walk your CV chronologically from college. The interviewer has it open. The only job of this answer is to hand them a thread worth pulling — so end on the thing you most want to be asked about, and make sure it's the thing you can talk about for ten minutes.</p>
<p class="bonus">Part 09 §2 has the full project-narrative structure if you want to rebuild the middle beat tonight.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-70"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q70</span><span class="qtext">"Why Entain?"</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Avoid "great company, great culture". Use one of these, in your own words:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The engineering problem</strong> — "real-time systems with money and strict latency at the same time. Odds that change per second, a wallet that has to be exactly right, peaks that are event-driven rather than smooth. That's a harder combination than most CRUD backends and it's the kind of problem I want."</li>
<li><strong>The scale and ownership</strong> — "the India centre isn't a support office; it's been building the platform since the Ivy Comptech days and owns transaction processing across 15+ brands. That's product ownership, not ticket work."</li>
<li><strong>The consolidation story</strong> — "the platform powers BetMGM in the US as well as the European brands, so the same stack serves very different regulatory regimes. The engineering constraints that come out of that are genuinely interesting."</li>
</ul>
<p class="gotcha">If they ask whether you have a problem working in gambling, answer honestly and without either defensiveness or a sales pitch. A straight "no, and I'd want to work on the platform properly, including the responsible-gambling controls" is enough.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-71"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q71</span><span class="qtext">"Tell me about the hardest bug you've fixed."</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Given the topics on this invite, <strong>pick a memory, concurrency or performance bug</strong> if you have one — it lets you re-use §6 and §7 with evidence attached instead of theory.</p>
<p>Shape it as: <strong>symptom</strong> (what the customer or the alert saw) → <strong>what you first assumed and why it was wrong</strong> → <strong>how you actually found it</strong> (the tool, the measurement) → <strong>the fix</strong> → <strong>what you changed so it couldn't recur</strong>.</p>
<p class="bonus">That fourth beat — "and here's the alert/test/bound I added" — is the one most candidates skip and the one that reads as seniority. The admission in beat two costs you nothing and buys enormous credibility.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-72"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q72</span><span class="qtext">"What's your biggest weakness / a time you failed?"</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Pick something <strong>real, bounded, and already being worked on</strong>. The failure mode is a humble-brag ("I care too much") — it reads as evasive and wastes the question.</p>
<p>Good shape: a genuine gap → the concrete cost it had once → what you changed → the evidence it's better now. Keep it under a minute, don't over-apologise, and move on.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-73"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q73</span><span class="qtext">"Do you have any questions?" — and the notice-period / salary question.</span></p><div class="a">
<p>Questions: §11. On logistics, be direct and have your numbers decided <em>before</em> the call — hesitating reads worse than any figure.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Notice period</strong> — state it accurately, plus anything you can do to shorten it.</li>
<li><strong>Compensation</strong> — if pushed in a technical round, it's fair to say "I'd rather align with the recruiter on that — my expectation is in line with the band for this role." If the recruiter is the one asking, give your number.</li>
</ul>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-74"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q74</span><span class="qtext">You have 5+ years and this is an SDE II role. How do you handle that?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Don't raise it, but don't be caught out by it.</strong> If they ask "aren't you over-qualified?" or "why this level?", the answer is about the <em>work</em>, not the title.</p>
<p>Something like: "I care more about what the system is than what the level is called. This is a real-time transactional platform at serious scale, which is a step up in problem difficulty from what I'm doing now, and the India centre owns it end to end. I'd rather be solving harder problems at SDE II here than easier ones with a bigger title elsewhere — and I'd expect the level to follow the work."</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>The real risk isn't the title — it's how you answer the technical questions.</strong> At 5+ years you can accidentally over-answer: a rapid-fire collections question gets a four-minute architectural response and the interviewer reads it as poor calibration. <strong>Answer at the altitude of the question.</strong> Lead line, one layer of depth, then stop and offer more. That discipline <em>is</em> the seniority signal — more than any amount of extra content.</p>
<p class="bonus">And the flip side, which is your actual advantage: when a question <em>does</em> open up — a leak, a deadlock, a design trade-off — you have production scars and an SDE II candidate doesn't. That's where you spend your depth. §6 Q22, §7 Q35 and §9 Q61 are the three most likely doors.</p>
</div></section>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-13-drill">13. The 48-hour drill<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-13-drill">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead">Today is Tuesday 16 September. The interview is Thursday 18 September at 4:00 PM IST. Two evenings and one morning. Don't try to read everything — do this.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Do</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Tue evening<br/>(90 min)</strong></td><td>§6 end to end, out loud. Then §7 Q27–Q29 and Q33–Q35.</td><td>Memory, collections and threads are your three highest-probability topics. Saying them out loud is not optional — reading them silently creates false confidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tue, before bed<br/>(20 min)</strong></td><td>Write Q49, Q54 and Q58 <strong>by hand or in Notepad</strong>. No IDE.</td><td>Reported coding happens in a plain editor. Find out now that you misremember <code>ArrayDeque</code>'s method names, not on the call.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wed morning<br/>(45 min)</strong></td><td>§8 — Spring (Q39–Q42), SQL (Q43–Q44), Kafka/Redis (Q45–Q46).</td><td>The applied layer. Q41's three <code>@Transactional</code> traps and Q43's window functions are the highest-yield items here.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wed evening<br/>(60 min)</strong></td><td>§9 — code Q50, Q56, Q61, Q65 cold, timed at 15 minutes each. Then read §10.</td><td>Q61 and Q65 are the two most Entain-shaped problems in the bank.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Wed, last 20 min</strong></td><td>§2 and §3 once. Write your three questions (§11) on a card. Rehearse Q69 out loud twice.</td><td>Company facts go stale in your head fast — do them last, not first.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Thu, 3:30 PM</strong></td><td>Test the Teams link in browser <em>and</em> app. Camera, mic, lighting, background. Water. Phone on silent. CV, a notepad and a pen next to you.</td><td>Thirty minutes of buffer turns a broken link from a disaster into an inconvenience.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Thu, 3:55 PM</strong></td><td>Read only your Q69 opener and your three questions. Nothing else.</td><td>Cramming in the last five minutes raises anxiety and retains nothing.</td></tr>
