<p class="gotcha"><strong>If you only have one hour total:</strong> §6 Q3, Q7, Q11, Q17, Q22 · §7 Q27, Q28, Q34, Q35 · §9 the seven-step protocol and Q54 · §11 your three questions. That's the minimum viable prep and it covers most of the probability mass.</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Offer still open</strong> — I can run a mock: I ask, you answer in the chat, I mark it and tell you where the answer would have lost points. Twenty questions from this part takes about thirty minutes and is worth more than another hour of reading. Say the word.</p>
<hr/>
<h2 id="p12-14-followups">14. Follow-up chains — level 1 and level 2<a aria-label="Link to this section" class="anchor" href="#p12-14-followups">#</a></h2>
<p class="lead"><strong>Read this section if you read nothing else.</strong> Interviewers rarely ask one question and move on. They ask, you answer, and then they <em>dig</em>. This section shows you the digging.</p>
<p>Each card has three steps:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>They ask</strong> — the opening question, and the answer you give.</li>
<li><strong>Level 1</strong> — what they ask next, once you've answered.</li>
<li><strong>Level 2</strong> — what they ask after <em>that</em>. This is where most people run out of road.</li>
</ul>
<p>Written in plain language on purpose. Short sentences. No showing off. If you can say the Level 2 answer in your own words, you are comfortably past the SDE II bar on that topic.</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>How to use it:</strong> cover the answers with your hand. Read the question. Say the answer out loud. Then reveal. Reading silently feels like learning but isn't — saying it out loud is what makes it come out under pressure.</p>
<h3 id="p12-14-1-collections-chains">14.1 Collections — the most likely topic</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-75"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q75</span><span class="qtext">What is a HashMap?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "It stores key–value pairs. You give it a key, it gives you back the value, very fast. Inside, it's an array of buckets. It takes the key, calculates a hash number from it, and that number decides which bucket to use."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "What happens if two keys land in the same bucket?"</strong></p>
<p>"That's called a collision. Both entries go in the same bucket, stored as a linked list. When you look one up, Java walks the list and uses <code>equals()</code> to find the right one.</p>
<p>Since Java 8, if one bucket gets too crowded — 8 or more entries — Java converts that list into a tree. A list takes longer and longer to search as it grows. A tree stays fast."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Why did they bother adding the tree? A list works."</strong></p>
<p>"Because of an attack. If someone knows your keys are user input, they can deliberately send thousands of keys that all hash to the same bucket. Now every lookup has to walk a huge list, your CPU goes to 100%, and the server stops responding. That's a denial-of-service attack.</p>
<p>The tree puts a floor under the worst case. Instead of getting slower and slower in a straight line, it stays reasonable."</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>If they push further:</strong> "the other thing that matters is resizing. When the map gets about 75% full, it doubles in size and moves everything. That's expensive. So if I know I'm going to store 1000 items, I create the map with a bigger starting size to avoid the resize."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-76"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q76</span><span class="qtext">HashMap is not thread-safe. What do you use instead?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "<code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>. It's built for multiple threads. The old option was <code>Hashtable</code>, but that locks the entire map for every single operation, so threads queue up behind each other. <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> only locks the one bucket being touched, so other threads can keep working."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "So if I use ConcurrentHashMap, my code is thread-safe?"</strong></p>
<p>"No — and this is the trap. Each individual method is safe. But if I combine two methods, there's a gap in between where another thread can slip in.</p>
<p>For example: <em>check if the key exists, and if not, add it.</em> Two threads can both check at the same time, both see 'not there', and both add. One overwrites the other.</p>
<p>The fix is to use the methods that do both steps in one go — <code>putIfAbsent</code>, or <code>computeIfAbsent</code>. One operation, no gap."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Why can't you put null in a ConcurrentHashMap?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because the answer would be ambiguous. If <code>get(key)</code> returns null, it could mean two things: the key isn't there, or the key is there and its value is null.</p>
<p>In a normal HashMap you can tell the difference by calling <code>containsKey</code>. But with multiple threads, the answer can change between your two calls — so you still don't know. They removed null entirely rather than leave you with a question you can't answer."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-77"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q77</span><span class="qtext">ArrayList or LinkedList — which do you use?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "ArrayList, nearly always. It's a plain array underneath. Getting item number 500 is instant. LinkedList is a chain of boxes, each pointing to the next — to reach item 500 you have to walk through 499 boxes first."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "But the textbook says LinkedList is faster for inserting in the middle. Isn't it?"</strong></p>
<p>"On paper, yes. In real life, usually no.</p>
<p>To insert in the middle of a LinkedList, you first have to <em>find</em> the middle — and that means walking the chain. The insert itself is fast, but the walk isn't.</p>
<p>ArrayList does have to shift items along. But it does that with a bulk memory copy, which processors are extremely good at. It's also all in one continuous block of memory, so the CPU cache works well. LinkedList scatters boxes all over memory, and every hop is a possible cache miss."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Is there any case where you'd pick LinkedList?"</strong></p>
<p>"Honestly, almost never. If I need to add and remove from both ends — like a queue — I'd use <code>ArrayDeque</code>, not LinkedList. ArrayDeque does the same job and is faster, because it's array-based again.</p>
<p>I don't think I've ever deliberately chosen LinkedList in production code."</p>
<p class="bonus">That last line is a good answer. Saying "the textbook answer is X but I've never actually needed it" shows real experience, not memorised theory.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-78"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q78</span><span class="qtext">What's the contract between equals() and hashCode()?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "If two objects are equal, they <em>must</em> return the same hash code. The reverse isn't required — two different objects can share a hash code, that's just a collision and it's allowed."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "What breaks if I override equals but forget hashCode?"</strong></p>
<p>"HashMap stops working, quietly.</p>
<p>You put an object in. Later you look it up with an equal object. But because the hash codes differ, the map looks in the wrong bucket, finds nothing, and returns null. Your object is in there — you just can't ever reach it.</p>
<p>Nothing throws an exception. It just silently returns the wrong answer, which is worse."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "What if I change a field on an object after putting it in a HashMap?"</strong></p>
<p>"Same problem, and now it's also a memory leak.</p>
<p>The object went into bucket 5 based on its original hash. You changed a field, so now it hashes to bucket 9. The map looks in bucket 9 and finds nothing. But the object is still sitting in bucket 5, holding memory, and you can't get it out — you can't even remove it, because <code>remove()</code> looks in bucket 9 too.</p>
<p>That's why keys should be immutable. It's why <code>String</code> and <code>Integer</code> make such good keys."</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Easy win:</strong> "these days I'd just use a <code>record</code>. Java generates a correct <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code> and <code>toString</code> for me, so there's nothing to get wrong."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-79"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q79</span><span class="qtext">Why is String immutable?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Once you create a String, you can't change it. Any method that looks like it changes a String actually returns a brand new one."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Why did they design it that way?"</strong></p>
<p>"Four reasons, and they all matter:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Safety.</strong> Strings are used for filenames, database URLs, usernames. If someone could change a String after you'd checked it was safe, they could check it, then change it.</li>
<li><strong>Threads.</strong> If nothing can change, nothing can go wrong when many threads read it at once. No locking needed.</li>
<li><strong>Caching the hash.</strong> Because it can never change, String calculates its hash code once and remembers it. That makes Strings very fast as HashMap keys.</li>
<li><strong>Sharing.</strong> Java keeps a pool of Strings and reuses them. That's only safe if nobody can modify them.</li>
</ul></p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "So what do you use when you need to build a string in a loop?"</strong></p>
<p>"<code>StringBuilder</code>. If I use <code>+</code> inside a loop, every single pass creates a whole new String and throws the old one away. With 10,000 items that's 10,000 wasted objects and it gets slower as the string grows.</p>
<p>StringBuilder keeps one buffer and appends to it.</p>
<p>Worth adding: outside a loop, <code>+</code> is fine — the compiler quietly turns it into a StringBuilder for you. It's specifically <em>loops</em> where it hurts, because the compiler can't do that across iterations."</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-14-2-memory-chains">14.2 Memory — your strongest card</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-80"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q80</span><span class="qtext">What's the difference between stack and heap?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "The heap is where objects live. Every time you write <code>new</code>, that object goes on the heap, and it's shared by all threads.</p>
<p>The stack is per-thread. Each thread has its own. It holds local variables and keeps track of which method called which. When a method finishes, its part of the stack is thrown away automatically."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "If I write <code>Point p = new Point()</code>, where does p live?"</strong></p>
<p>"Split across both. The <code>Point</code> object itself is on the heap. The variable <code>p</code> — which is just an address pointing at that object — is on the stack.</p>
<p>Think of the heap as a warehouse and the stack as a sticky note with the shelf number written on it."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Are objects <em>always</em> on the heap?"</strong></p>
<p>"By the rules of the language, yes. In the running machine, not always.</p>
<p>The JIT compiler does something called escape analysis. It checks whether an object can ever be seen outside the method that made it. If it can't, the JIT may skip creating the object entirely and just keep its fields in CPU registers.</p>
<p>That's why I don't usually try to hand-optimise away small short-lived objects — the JIT often does it for me. I'd rather write clear code and let a profiler tell me where it actually hurts."</p>
<p class="bonus">That last paragraph is a strong finish. It says: I know the deep detail, <em>and</em> I know when not to act on it.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-81"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q81</span><span class="qtext">How does garbage collection work?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Java automatically deletes objects you're no longer using. It doesn't count references. Instead it starts from a set of known starting points, called GC roots, and follows every link it can. Anything it can reach is kept. Anything it can't reach is deleted."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "What counts as a GC root?"</strong></p>
<p>"Things that are definitely alive:</p>
<ul>
<li>Local variables in any running method</li>
<li><code>static</code> fields</li>
<li>Threads that are currently running</li>
<li>Objects being locked right now</li>
</ul>
<p>Everything else is only alive if something reachable points to it."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Why reachability and not simple reference counting?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because counting can't handle circles.</p>
<p>Imagine object A points to B, and B points back to A. Nothing else points to either. Their counts are both 1, so a counting collector would keep them forever — even though nobody can reach them.</p>
<p>Reachability just asks 'can I get there from a root?' The answer is no, so both get collected. That's why a parent–child object graph isn't a leak in Java."</p>
<p class="gotcha"><strong>Then say this — it sets up every leak question:</strong> "which means a memory leak in Java is never about forgetting to free something. It's about accidentally keeping something reachable."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-82"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q82</span><span class="qtext">Can you have a memory leak in Java?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Yes. Not the C kind where you forget to free memory. In Java it's when you accidentally hold on to something you've finished with. The GC sees a live reference, so it correctly refuses to delete it. The GC is working fine — you just told it the wrong thing."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Give me a real example."</strong></p>
<p>"The classic one is a <code>static</code> Map used as a cache with nothing ever removing entries. Statics live for the whole life of the application, so everything in that map lives forever too. It grows until you run out of memory.</p>
<p>Others I've seen: registering a listener and never unregistering it; not closing streams or connections; and <code>ThreadLocal</code> in a thread pool."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Why is ThreadLocal a problem in a thread pool?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because pool threads never die — they get reused for the next request.</p>
<p>Each thread holds a map of its ThreadLocal values, and those values are held strongly. So whatever I stored stays attached to that thread after my request is finished, and the next request gets the same thread.</p>
<p>That's a leak, and in a multi-user system it's worse than a leak — request B can read request A's data. That's a security bug.</p>
<p>The fix is to always call <code>remove()</code> in a <code>finally</code> block. Not <code>set(null)</code> — that leaves an empty entry behind. <code>remove()</code> deletes it properly."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-83"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q83</span><span class="qtext">Your app throws OutOfMemoryError in production. What do you do?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "First I stop the bleeding — is it one server or all of them, and did a recent deploy cause it? If so, roll back. Customers first, root cause second."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "OK, you've stabilised it. Now how do you find the cause?"</strong></p>
<p>"Three steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Read the error message.</strong> 'Java heap space' means normal objects. 'Metaspace' means too many classes loaded. 'Unable to create new native thread' means too many threads. The message already narrows it down.</li>
<li><strong>Look at the GC log.</strong> I want one specific number: how full is the old generation <em>right after</em> each full garbage collection. If it goes up and comes back down, that's healthy — just busy. If it keeps creeping up and never comes back down, that's a leak.</li>
<li><strong>Take a heap dump</strong> and open it in Eclipse MAT. It shows what's holding the most memory and, more importantly, <em>what is keeping it alive</em>. That chain of references is the bug."</li>
</ol></p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "How do you know whether it's a leak or you just need more memory?"</strong></p>
<p>"That one number from step 2 — old generation usage after a full GC.</p>
<p>If it returns to roughly the same baseline every time, memory is being reclaimed properly. The app just genuinely needs more room, so I increase the heap.</p>
<p>If the baseline climbs a little higher every cycle and never comes back, something is being retained. Adding memory won't fix that — it just buys time and makes the eventual pause longer.</p>
<p>The fixes are opposite, so getting this right first matters."</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>Great closing line:</strong> "and I always run with <code>-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError</code> turned on. An OOM you didn't capture a dump for is an OOM you get to have twice."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-84"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q84</span><span class="qtext">What is the young generation and old generation?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "The heap is split in two. New objects go in the young generation. Objects that survive long enough get moved to the old generation."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Why split it at all?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because most objects die almost immediately. Think of a method that creates a few temporary objects and returns — they're garbage within microseconds.</p>
<p>So instead of scanning the entire heap every time, Java scans just the young part, very often. That part is small and almost everything in it is dead, so cleaning it is cheap.</p>
<p>The key insight: the collector copies out the few <em>survivors</em> and wipes the rest in one go. Dead objects cost nothing to collect. So if 98% of it is garbage, the work is proportional to the 2%."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "What's premature promotion?"</strong></p>
<p>"When objects get moved to the old generation before they were ready to.</p>
<p>Objects normally bounce around the young area a few times before being promoted. But if the young area is too small, there's no room to bounce, so they get pushed into old early.</p>
<p>The problem is that the old generation is only cleaned by a full garbage collection, which is slow and pauses the whole application. So you end up doing expensive cleanups for objects that would have died cheaply on their own.</p>
<p>The symptom is the old generation growing steadily even though the load is steady. It looks like a leak but it's actually a sizing problem."</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-14-3-threads-chains">14.3 Threads — asked in two separate rounds</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-85"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q85</span><span class="qtext">How do you create a thread in Java?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Three ways. Extend <code>Thread</code>, implement <code>Runnable</code>, or implement <code>Callable</code>. But in real code I don't create threads directly — I use an <code>ExecutorService</code> and give it tasks."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Why not just create threads?"</strong></p>
<p>"Creating a thread is expensive, and each one reserves about a megabyte of memory.</p>
<p>If I create a thread per request and traffic spikes, I create thousands of threads, run out of memory, and the server dies right when it's busiest.</p>
<p>A pool reuses a fixed set of threads. If more work arrives than the pool can handle, it queues — which is much better than falling over."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Which pool would you create?"</strong></p>
<p>"Not the convenience ones. <code>Executors.newFixedThreadPool</code> looks safe, but its queue is <strong>unlimited</strong>. So under heavy load you don't reject anything — you just queue forever until you run out of memory. You've turned a fast failure into a slow one.</p>
<p>And <code>newCachedThreadPool</code> creates unlimited <em>threads</em>, which is worse.</p>
<p>So I build a <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> myself with a <strong>bounded</strong> queue, and I decide what happens when it's full. Usually <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code> — the thread that submitted the work has to do it itself. That naturally slows down whoever is flooding you.</p>
<p>I also give threads proper names, so when I read a thread dump at 2am I can tell what they are."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-86"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q86</span><span class="qtext">What does volatile do?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "It guarantees that when one thread writes a variable, other threads see the new value. Without it, each CPU core can cache its own copy, and a thread might keep reading a stale value forever."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "So can I use volatile for a counter?"</strong></p>
<p>"No, and this is the most common mistake.</p>
<p><code>count++</code> looks like one action but it's actually three: read the value, add one, write it back.</p>
<p>Two threads can both read 5, both add one, and both write 6. You did two increments and got one. <code>volatile</code> doesn't help, because each individual read and write was fine — the problem is the gap between them.</p>
<p>For a counter I'd use <code>AtomicInteger</code>, which does all three steps as one indivisible operation."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "When <em>would</em> you use volatile then?"</strong></p>
<p>"When one thread writes and others only read. A simple flag is the classic case:</p>
<pre><code>private volatile boolean running = true;

public void stop() { running = false; }        // one thread writes
public void run()  { while (running) { … } }   // another reads
</code></pre>
<p>Without <code>volatile</code>, the loop might never see the change and run forever.</p>
<p>The rule I use: <strong>volatile for visibility, atomic or a lock for counting.</strong>"</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-87"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q87</span><span class="qtext">What is a deadlock?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Two threads each holding something the other needs, and neither will let go. Thread A holds lock 1 and wants lock 2. Thread B holds lock 2 and wants lock 1. Both wait forever."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "How do you prevent it?"</strong></p>
<p>"The simplest and most reliable way is <strong>always lock in the same order</strong>.</p>
<p>If every thread in the system agrees to take lock 1 before lock 2 — always, no exceptions — then a circular wait can't happen. Whoever gets lock 1 first will finish.</p>
<p>A common trick is to sort by something unique, like an ID, and lock in that order.</p>
<p>Other options: use <code>tryLock</code> with a timeout so a thread gives up and retries instead of waiting forever, or hold locks for less time."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Two users transfer money to each other at the same time. Show me."</strong></p>
<p>"This is the textbook case. <code>transfer(A→B)</code> and <code>transfer(B→A)</code> running together will deadlock if each locks in argument order.</p>
<pre><code>void transfer(Account from, Account to, BigDecimal amount) {
    // decide order by ID, not by argument position
    Account first  = from.id().compareTo(to.id()) &lt; 0 ? from : to;
    Account second = (first == from) ? to : from;

    synchronized (first) {
        synchronized (second) {
            from.debit(amount);
            to.credit(amount);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now both threads lock the lower ID first, so one waits politely and neither gets stuck.</p>
<p>One edge case to mention: if someone transfers to themselves, <code>from</code> and <code>to</code> are the same account — I'd guard against that separately."</p>
<p class="bonus"><strong>If you want to really impress, add:</strong> "though in a real banking system I wouldn't use Java locks at all — there's more than one server running, so a lock inside one JVM protects nothing. I'd put it in the database with a transaction and a version check."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-88"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q88</span><span class="qtext">Explain the producer–consumer problem.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "One or more threads produce items. Others consume them. They share a queue in the middle. The producer must wait if the queue is full; the consumer must wait if it's empty."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "How would you implement it?"</strong></p>
<p>"I'd use <code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code> and let Java do the work:</p>
<pre><code>BlockingQueue&lt;Bet&gt; queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue&lt;&gt;(1000);

queue.put(bet);           // producer — waits automatically if full
Bet bet = queue.take();   // consumer — waits automatically if empty
</code></pre>
<p><code>put</code> and <code>take</code> handle all the waiting and waking for me. There's no reason to write this by hand.</p>
<p>The size limit matters too — it means if consumers fall behind, producers naturally slow down instead of filling up memory."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Write it by hand with wait and notify."</strong></p>
<pre><code>public synchronized void put(Bet b) throws InterruptedException {
    while (queue.size() == capacity) wait();   // while, not if
    queue.add(b);
    notifyAll();
}

public synchronized Bet take() throws InterruptedException {
    while (queue.isEmpty()) wait();
    Bet b = queue.poll();
    notifyAll();
    return b;
}
</code></pre>
<p>"Three things they're checking here:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><code>while</code>, never <code>if</code>.</strong> A thread can wake up without anyone waking it — that's a real thing, called a spurious wakeup. Also another thread might grab the item first. So on waking, always re-check the condition.</li>
<li><strong><code>notifyAll</code>, not <code>notify</code>.</strong> With both producers and consumers waiting, <code>notify</code> might wake the wrong kind — a producer waking another producer while the queue is still full. Then everyone's stuck.</li>
<li><strong><code>wait()</code> releases the lock while waiting</strong>, which is the whole point. <code>sleep()</code> doesn't — it holds the lock and blocks everyone."</li>
</ul>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-14-4-oop-chains">14.4 OOP and core Java</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-89"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q89</span><span class="qtext">Interface or abstract class — when do you use each?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "An interface says <em>what</em> something can do. An abstract class says what it can do <em>and</em> shares some of the how. The biggest practical difference: a class can implement many interfaces but extend only one class."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Java 8 added default methods to interfaces. So what's left?"</strong></p>
<p>"<strong>State.</strong> That's the real remaining difference.</p>
<p>An interface can now carry behaviour, through default methods. But it still can't hold instance fields, and it has no constructor.</p>
<p>So: if the subclasses need to share actual data and a construction process, I use an abstract class. If I'm just describing a capability — something is comparable, or payable — I use an interface."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "What if a class implements two interfaces with the same default method?"</strong></p>
<p>"It won't compile. Java makes you resolve it yourself, so there's no silent guessing:</p>
<pre><code>class MyClass implements A, B {
    @Override public void doThing() {
        A.super.doThing();   // pick one explicitly
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>That's Java deliberately avoiding the multiple-inheritance mess that C++ has. You can inherit behaviour from several places, but if there's a genuine conflict, you must say what you mean."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-90"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q90</span><span class="qtext">Checked or unchecked exceptions?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Checked exceptions have to be handled or declared — the compiler forces it. Unchecked ones don't. Checked are meant for problems the caller could realistically recover from; unchecked for programming mistakes."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Which do you prefer in practice?"</strong></p>
<p>"I lean towards unchecked in service code, and I'd say why rather than just assert it:</p>
<ul>
<li>Checked exceptions leak implementation details upwards. If my repository throws <code>SQLException</code>, suddenly every layer above knows I'm using a database.</li>
<li>They don't work well with lambdas and streams — you end up wrapping everything anyway.</li>
<li>In real codebases they get caught and ignored just to make the compiler quiet, which is worse than not having them.</li>
</ul>
<p>So I use a small set of my own unchecked exceptions and handle them all in one place at the edge — in Spring that's a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code>."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "What happens if you return from a finally block?"</strong></p>
<p>"It silently throws away the exception.</p>
<pre><code>try {
    throw new RuntimeException("something broke");
} finally {
    return "ok";   // the exception vanishes. No trace. Nothing logged.
}
</code></pre>
<p>The caller gets 'ok' and never knows anything went wrong. So: never return from <code>finally</code>.</p>
<p>Related — this is exactly why <code>try-with-resources</code> is better than closing things by hand. If both the body and the close fail, it keeps the real exception and attaches the close failure to it, rather than losing one."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-91"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q91</span><span class="qtext">What are streams in Java 8?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "A way to describe <em>what</em> you want from a collection instead of writing the loop yourself. Filter, transform, collect. It reads closer to the intent."</p>
<pre><code>List&lt;String&gt; names = users.stream()
    .filter(u -&gt; u.age() &gt; 18)
    .map(User::name)
    .toList();
</code></pre>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "What does 'lazy' mean here?"</strong></p>
<p>"Nothing runs until the end.</p>
<p><code>filter</code> and <code>map</code> don't do any work when you call them — they just record what you asked for. Only a terminal operation like <code>toList()</code> or <code>collect()</code> actually triggers it.</p>
<p>This means Java can be clever. It processes each element through the whole chain at once, rather than making a new intermediate list at every step. And with something like <code>findFirst()</code>, it can stop as soon as it has an answer instead of processing everything."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Should you use parallelStream() to make it faster?"</strong></p>
<p>"Usually not, and I'd be careful with it.</p>
<p>Three reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li>It uses one shared thread pool for the entire application. If I put slow work or database calls on it, I slow down everything else that uses it.</li>
<li>Splitting the work and recombining costs something. For small collections, parallel is slower than sequential.</li>
<li>If my lambda touches shared mutable state, parallel turns a working program into a broken one.</li>
</ul>
<p>It's worth it for genuinely large collections doing genuinely CPU-heavy work, with no shared state — and I'd measure before and after rather than assume."</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-14-5-spring-chains">14.5 Spring, database and APIs</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-92"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q92</span><span class="qtext">What does Spring Boot actually do for you?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "It removes configuration. It looks at what libraries are on your classpath and sets up sensible defaults automatically. Put a database driver in, and it configures a connection pool without you writing any XML."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "How does it know what to configure?"</strong></p>
<p>"It has a list of configuration classes, and each one has conditions attached.</p>
<p>A typical condition is: 'only apply this <em>if</em> this class is on the classpath, <em>and</em> the user hasn't already defined their own version.'</p>
<p>That second part is the important one. If I define my own bean, Spring Boot backs off. That's why it feels like helpful defaults rather than magic you have to fight."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Something isn't being configured. How do you debug it?"</strong></p>
<p>"Start the app with <code>--debug</code>. Spring Boot prints a Conditions Evaluation Report — a full list of every auto-configuration it considered, which ones were applied, which weren't, and <strong>the reason for each</strong>.</p>
<p>So instead of guessing, it tells me directly: 'did not match because DataSource class was not found', or 'did not match because you already defined this bean'."</p>
<p class="bonus">Knowing the debugging move is worth more than knowing the definition. Most candidates can define auto-configuration; far fewer can say how to see inside it.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-93"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q93</span><span class="qtext">What does @Transactional do?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "It wraps the method in a database transaction. If the method finishes normally, everything commits. If it throws, everything rolls back. So either all the changes happen or none do."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "How does Spring make that happen?"</strong></p>
<p>"It creates a proxy — a wrapper object around my bean. Calls go to the wrapper first. The wrapper opens a transaction, calls my real method, then commits or rolls back.</p>
<p>My own code doesn't contain any transaction handling at all, which is the point."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Name a case where @Transactional silently does nothing."</strong></p>
<p>"Three, and they're all silent — no error, it just doesn't work:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Calling it from inside the same class.</strong> If method A calls method B in the same object, the call never goes through the proxy — it goes straight to the method. So <code>@Transactional</code> on B is ignored.</li>
<li><strong>Private methods.</strong> The proxy can't intercept them. Ignored.</li>
<li><strong>Checked exceptions.</strong> By default Spring only rolls back on <em>unchecked</em> exceptions. Throw a checked exception expecting a rollback and it will <strong>commit</strong> instead. You have to write <code>@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>That third one is nasty, because it fails in the direction of keeping bad data."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-94"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q94</span><span class="qtext">What is the N+1 problem?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "You run one query to fetch a list, then accidentally run one more query for every item in that list. Fetch 100 customers and then touch their orders, and you've done 101 database round trips instead of 1."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Why does it happen?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because relationships are lazy by default, which is usually the right choice. The orders aren't loaded with the customer — they're loaded the moment you first touch them.</p>
<p>Inside a loop, that means one query per iteration. Nothing in the code looks wrong. It passes every test with three rows and then destroys production with ten thousand."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "How do you fix it, and how would you catch it before production?"</strong></p>
<p>"Fixing it is easy — use <code>JOIN FETCH</code> in the query, or <code>@EntityGraph</code> on the repository method, and it becomes one query with a join.</p>
<p>Catching it is the more interesting half. Reading SQL logs by eye doesn't scale — nobody does it consistently.</p>
<p>So I'd have tests that actually <strong>count the queries</strong>, using Hibernate statistics or a datasource proxy, and fail if the number goes up. That way the build catches it when someone adds a lazy field, instead of a customer finding it."</p>
<p class="bonus">That answer — "here's the fix, and here's how I stop it coming back" — is a level above just naming <code>JOIN FETCH</code>.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-95"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q95</span><span class="qtext">Write a query to find the second-highest salary.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — start with the simple one:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees
WHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);
</code></pre>
<p>"Find the highest, then find the highest of everything below it."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Now give me the third highest. And the Nth."</strong></p>
<p>"The subquery trick doesn't extend well, so I'd switch to a window function:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT salary FROM (
  SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk
  FROM employees
) t WHERE rnk = 3;
</code></pre>
<p>Change the number and it works for any position."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "What's the difference between ROW_NUMBER, RANK and DENSE_RANK?"</strong></p>
<p>"It's all about how they handle ties. Say salaries are 100, 90, 90, 80:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Result</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>ROW_NUMBER</code></td><td>1, 2, 3, 4</td><td>Always unique. Ties broken arbitrarily.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>RANK</code></td><td>1, 2, 2, 4</td><td>Ties share a rank, then it skips a number.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>DENSE_RANK</code></td><td>1, 2, 2, 3</td><td>Ties share a rank, no gap.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>So for 'second highest <em>distinct</em> salary' I want <code>DENSE_RANK</code>. With <code>RANK</code>, asking for rank 3 would return nothing at all in that example."</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-96"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q96</span><span class="qtext">How do you make an API idempotent?</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "Idempotent means calling it twice has the same effect as calling it once. It matters because networks fail. If a customer places a bet and the response is lost, their app retries — and you must not take the money twice."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "How would you build that?"</strong></p>
<p>"The client generates a unique ID for the operation and sends it as a header. If it retries, it sends the <strong>same</strong> ID.</p>
<p>On my side I store that ID in a table with a <strong>unique constraint</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>First request: the insert succeeds, I process the bet, and I save the response.</li>
<li>Retry: the insert fails because of the unique constraint. So I know I've seen it — I return the saved response and do nothing else.</li>
</ul></p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Why a database constraint? Couldn't you just check Redis?"</strong></p>
<p>"Checking first and then acting leaves a gap. Two retries can arrive at the same instant, both check, both see nothing, and both process. That's exactly the bug I was trying to prevent.</p>
<p>A unique constraint has no gap. The database decides, and only one insert can win. There's no window between checking and acting.</p>
<p>I'd still use Redis in front of it as a fast path to avoid hitting the database for obvious duplicates. But the actual guarantee lives in the database. Redis is the optimisation; the constraint is the correctness."</p>
<p class="bonus">This is a <em>very</em> likely question at a betting company — taking money twice is their worst-case bug. If you only rehearse three answers from this section, make this one of them.</p>
</div></section>
<h3 id="p12-14-6-coding-chains">14.6 When they make you write code</h3>
<section class="qa" id="q12-97"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q97</span><span class="qtext">Find two numbers in an array that add up to a target.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Say this before you type</strong> — "The obvious way is two nested loops, checking every pair. That works but it's O(n²). I can do it in one pass with a HashMap instead, trading some memory for speed."</p>
<pre><code>static int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
    Map&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; seen = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();   // value -> position
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; nums.length; i++) {
        Integer j = seen.get(target - nums[i]);
        if (j != null) return new int[]{j, i};
        seen.put(nums[i], i);                         // store AFTER checking
    }
    return new int[0];
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Explain how that works."</strong></p>
<p>"For each number I work out what its partner would need to be — target minus this number. Then I check whether I've already seen that partner.</p>
<p>If yes, I'm done. If no, I remember this number and move on.</p>
<p>Each number is looked at once, and HashMap lookups are instant, so it's O(n) time and O(n) space."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Why do you store the number <em>after</em> checking, not before?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because otherwise a number could match with itself.</p>
<p>If the target is 10 and the current number is 5, then storing first means I immediately find 5 in the map — but that's the same 5 I'm standing on, not a different one. I'd return a wrong answer using one element twice.</p>
<p>Checking first means the map only ever contains numbers from <em>earlier</em> positions."</p>
<p class="gotcha">This is the exact question interviewers use to see whether you understood your own code or memorised it. Have the answer ready.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="qa" id="q12-98"><p class="q"><span class="qnum">Q98</span><span class="qtext">Reverse a string.</span></p><div class="a">
<p class="tldr"><strong>Your answer</strong> — "One line: <code>new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString()</code>. But if you want me to write it manually, I'd use two pointers."</p>
<pre><code>static String reverse(String s) {
    char[] c = s.toCharArray();
    int i = 0, j = c.length - 1;
    while (i &lt; j) {
        char t = c[i]; c[i] = c[j]; c[j] = t;
        i++; j--;
    }
    return new String(c);
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>↳ Level 1 — "Why convert to a char array at all?"</strong></p>
<p>"Because String is immutable — I can't change its characters. So I copy them into an array I <em>can</em> change, swap them, then build a new String from the result.</p>
<p>One pointer starts at each end, they swap and move towards the middle, and they stop when they meet. That's O(n) time, and each character is touched once."</p>
<p><strong>↳ Level 2 — "Does your solution work for emoji?"</strong></p>
<p>"No — and neither does the one-line version. That's a good catch.</p>
<p>Java stores text as UTF-16. Most characters fit in one unit, but emoji and some other symbols take two units together, called a surrogate pair. If I reverse blindly, I split those pairs apart and get broken output.</p>
<p>The proper fix is to work in <em>code points</em> rather than <code>char</code> values.</p>
<p>In practice I'd ask what the input actually is. If it's a bet reference or an ID, the simple version is correct and clear. If it's user-entered text, I need the code-point-aware version."</p>
<p class="bonus">Saying "it depends what the input is" is a genuinely senior answer. It shows you weigh correctness against complexity rather than always reaching for the most complicated option.</p>
</div></section>
